{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793915981322,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adb98bbe001eXVFdzvkxchEs7","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adb9879d001j0h1FiOIqvNKQO","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-98c2419933f56b31","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\nfind packages/bundle/headless -type f | head -30; echo \"---\"; sed -n 1,160p packages/bundle/headless/README.md 2>/dev/null"},"output":"packages/bundle/headless/README.zh.md\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/zh.json\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/ja.json\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/de.json\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/ru.json\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/pt.json\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/en.json\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/fr.json\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/es.json\npackages/bundle/headless/cordis.patch.yml\npackages/bundle/headless/tests/terminal-failures.spec.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/tests/startup.spec.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/tests/json-stream.spec.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/tests/headless.spec.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/tests/fixtures/provider-cwd.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/README.md\npackages/bundle/headless/package.json\npackages/bundle/headless/tsconfig.json\npackages/bundle/headless/README.i18n.yaml\npackages/bundle/headless/src/runner-internals.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/src/startup-internals.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/src/index.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/src/json-stream.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/src/startup.ts\n---\n---\ndescription: \"One-shot task mode for dsh: run a single task from the command line and get the final answer printed, for users scripting or automating dsh.\"\nkind: \"package-bundle\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-headless\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-headless` runs one dsh task from the command line and prints the final answer, then exits — no GUI, no server, no browser. Type `dsh --profile headless \"run the tests\"` and the agent handles it with the same model, tools, and safety defaults as every other surface. It suits scripts, CI, and one-off jobs: it opens no ports and leaves nothing running behind. It also offers a JSON event stream (`--json`) and `--session-id` to resume a conversation. Exit code 0 means the task completed; 1 means it aborted or errored. The boundary: one task per invocation, no interactive follow-up.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nRun one task, get the final answer, and exit. The task is the command-line argument, or stdin when you omit it; the whole invocation is the smallest working example.\n\n### Running a one-shot task\n\n```sh\ndsh --profile headless \"run the tests\"\n```\n\nThe agent works through the task, streams each non-empty provider reasoning delta to stderr under a `dsh: reasoning:` heading, then prints the final answer on stdout and exits. Consecutive reasoning deltas stay in one section, and the runner closes that section before later output when the provider supplied no trailing newline. A successful run without reasoning keeps stderr empty; a failure exits 1 and prints `dsh: <code>: <message>` to stderr. The task comes from the positional argument, or from stdin when the argument is omitted or is a lone `-`; a blank positional argument or an empty pipe is rejected before anything runs. A positional task is used as-is and stdin is not read, so put the whole prompt in the pipe when you want piped input; a piped task is sent verbatim, its trailing newline included.\n\n```sh\n{ echo \"Summarize these changes:\"; git diff --stat; } | dsh --profile headless\n```\n\nThe task and run options use these settings:\n\n| Field | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `task` | stdin | The task text; stdin supplies it when omitted or `-` |\n| `sessionId` | `session-<uuid>` | Exact Session identity to adopt; an unknown id fails |\n| `json` | `false` | Project the run as newline-delimited events on stdout |\n| `permissionMode` | session policy | Apply a configured permission preset before submitting the task, including on resume |\n\nThe generated [configuration catalog](../../../docs/config-catalog.md#deepseek-aidsh-headless) is the exhaustive source for every accepted field and its JSDoc.\n\n<a id=\"credentials\"></a>\n### Credentials\n\nThe shipped headless profile disables the managed credential store and its authorization and DeepSeek account consumers. API-key routes use the launch environment, then `<invocation cwd>/.env`, then `$DSH_HOME/.env`. Keys and sign-ins saved by the GUI are ignored without changing their file. Providers that require a saved sign-in fail unless stored authentication is restored; external provider credential chains, such as AWS profiles, remain available.\n\nThe DeepSeek Search and Fetch APIs [follow the session's DeepSeek credentials](../../web/web-deepseek-api/README.md#authentication). In the shipped headless profile, they send the DeepSeek model route's `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY` to that route's origin, so no sign-in is needed. Without that key, the model request omits `web_search`.\n\nUnset an empty launch variable to allow its `.env` fallback; an exported empty value shadows that fallback.\n\nTo restore stored authentication, add these rows to `$DSH_HOME/profiles/headless/cordis.patch.yml` or a `--patch` overlay:\n\n```yaml\n- id: credentials\n  disabled: false\n- id: authorization\n  disabled: false\n- id: deepseek-account\n  disabled: false\n```\n\nThe restored store uses [saved credentials before environment fallbacks](../../credentials/credentials-local/README.md). Web, SDK and ACP profiles keep that default ordering. Headless sessions, settings and tool behavior are unchanged; this profile choice is not a filesystem access boundary.\n\n### Choosing the session identity\n\nEvery invocation defaults to a fresh `session-<uuid>` identity, which `--json` reports in its opening `session` event. Pass `--session-id <id>` to continue that conversation: the runner adopts the persisted Session with that id, and an id with no stored Session fails before the task runs rather than quietly opening an empty history. Adoption requires the composed `sessionPersistence` and `sessionQuery` services, so a profile that omits either fails loudly instead of returning an id whose history dies with the process. An Agent already live under the requested id in this process is refused: another owner may still drive it, so the runner cannot claim an exclusive run interval over it. The identity is opaque, so the exact string is used, whitespace included. The working directory is resolved through the mounted filesystem provider (`fs.resolve('.')` and `fs.processPath()`), or the process cwd when no filesystem service is mounted; new Sessions record that directory. Adoption compares the recorded cwd with the same provider-resolved directory and refuses a Session that is a subagent session, that recorded no working directory, that runs under an agent preset this profile does not compose, or whose preset record is malformed — the check reads the preset the Session log currently records, so a Session that switched preset while blank is rejected too. A supervisor therefore cannot silently drive someone else's conversation under a different composition; any mismatch fails before the task runs.\n\nOn adoption, the runner records the current deployment route before submitting the task. An omitted effort preserves an explicit saved effort only for the same route. A step already prepared in this process keeps its captured route; subsequent steps read the shared Session selection.\n\n### Supervising implementation tasks\n\nUse one prepared Git worktree per implementation task. Start the process with that worktree as its working directory. Supply a focused task on stdin and capture stdout as NDJSON; diagnostics remain on stderr.\n\n```sh\ngit_dir=$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)\ndsh headless --permission-mode workspace-write --add-dir \"$git_dir\" --json - < task.txt > run.jsonl\n```\n\n`--add-dir` accepts repeatable physical absolute directory paths in the execution environment. The shared Git directory permits staging, committing, and repository hooks without granting the parent checkout's source files. It grants all metadata in that directory, including shared refs, hooks, and configuration. Later host Git commands can execute modified hooks or configuration outside the sandbox; use this grant for trusted tasks and review metadata changes before host Git operations. Additional grants apply to this invocation and must be supplied again when resuming. The shipped profile passes them to `sandbox-policy`; the runner checks the original parsed CLI grants directly. An overlay that replaces the policy row must include `ctx.headlessStartup.additionalWritableRoots`, or the runner rejects the request before model work. The [local sandbox provider](../../sandbox/sandbox-local/README.md) defines platform support.\n\nRead the opening `session.sessionId`, review the diff, and send revision instructions with `--session-id` from the same worktree. Repeat the directory grants. `--permission-mode` selects an existing permission preset through the permission service and overrides runner configuration and the session's saved selection before the task runs; omission preserves normal defaults and saved policy. These options do not edit saved profiles or user settings. `--approval-policy ask|never` applies after preset selection; `never` rejects calls that need human approval. Repeat explicit policy options when resuming an unattended task.\n\nAsk the worker to report its working directory, base and resulting commit, changed files, exact gate commands, exit codes, log paths, and blocked or unrun checks. Review that evidence and trust completed gate reports. Run a check again only when a revision or new finding invalidates its evidence. Bound investigation in the task, and keep publication with the supervisor. A gate blocked by host IPC, external caches, or nested sandboxing remains unrun until the supervisor authorizes and runs it in a suitable environment.\n\nA supervisor that interrupts a process must await its exit before resuming its Session. Keep the event log even after a nonzero exit. Require both process exit zero and `final.reason.kind === 'completed'` for a completed agent run, then assess the worker's task and gate report separately. A `tool_result` status of `completed` means that the tool returned; a shell command inside it can still have a nonzero exit.\n\n### Experimental Auto review\n\nFor model-reviewed full-access execution, explicitly enable [Auto review for headless supervision](../../experimental/auto-review/README.md#headless-supervision), select `--permission-mode auto --approval-policy never`, and use native tools. Auto reviews supported calls before execution and has no file sandbox. Its JSON denials let a supervisor inspect the failure and send scoped revision instructions through `--session-id`. Headless has no live approval-response channel.\n\n### Machine-readable output\n\n`--json` replaces the final-text stdout line with a newline-delimited JSON event stream, while stderr keeps only the `dsh:` diagnostics. The stream opens with `session` (carrying the identity the run used) and closes with `final`, and carries `status`, `text`, `thinking`, `tool_call`, and `tool_result` events in between. `text` and `thinking` are projected from committed assistant messages, so a retried or discarded attempt never reaches the stream; they arrive when the step commits, not per token, and default-mode stderr reasoning remains the only live text channel. The terminal `final` event carries the exact `sessionId`, the durable turn `reason` (or `null` when the final turn has no terminal event), and the same lossless answer as the default mode; it is not capped; every other string and object key is capped at 8 KiB and flagged with `truncated`, and one event line, its newline included, is capped at 32 KiB — an over-long event keeps its scalar fields, drops structured ones, and at the extreme reduces to `type` and `truncated`, while a payload nested 64 levels or deeper is cut at that depth. An empty tool-argument string projects as `{}`, matching what the executor runs, while arguments that JSON cannot round-trip — an overflowing number such as `1e400` — keep their raw text rather than the `null` that `JSON.stringify` would report. A failure the runner raises outside a turn writes an `error` event and ends the stream without `final`, in addition to the `dsh:` stderr line; a profile whose own plugins fail to load exits before the runner mounts, so that case keeps only the loader's stderr diagnostics. A turn that fails in-turn still ends with a `final` event (often empty) and no `error` event, so a well-formed stream can still describe a failed run: check the process exit status and `final.reason` before assessing the task report.\n\n`tool_result.error`, when present, preserves the durable `{ name, code, reason? }` error fields under the same output limits. `AUTO_REVIEW_DENIED` identifies a final policy denial; `AUTO_REVIEW_FAILED` identifies a reviewer failure. Either leaves the call body unexecuted. A truncated record may omit structured detail; inspect the persisted Session when the complete reason is needed.\n\n### When to use it\n\nUse headless for scripted or automated dsh runs — CI steps, batch jobs, quick answers from a terminal. Avoid it when you need a multi-turn interactive session or a GUI; the browser surface ([dsh-web-app](../web-app/README.md)) serves that. The process stays alive only for the run, opens no listening port, and exits on its own, so it fits pipelines that wait on the process. When a supervisor needs progress rather than just the answer, `--json` gives it the event stream and `--session-id` lets a later invocation continue the same conversation.\n\n### Help and task errors\n\n`dsh --profile headless --help` prints the command's help text and exits without running anything. A whitespace-only positional task is a usage error on its own — nothing runs and the process exits 1, even when stdin is not a terminal, so an accidental blank argument never consumes a pipe. A task that is absent entirely is a usage error only when stdin is a terminal; otherwise the runner reads the task from stdin and rejects an empty result the same way. A lone `-` is the only stdin marker; mixing it with other task words is a usage error rather than a task that starts with a dash. In `--json` mode every usage error — including commander's own grammar rejections such as an unknown option or a missing option value — also writes an `error` event to stdout before the process exits, so a line-oriented supervisor sees a well-formed stream even when the runner never mounts; the event `message` carries the text without commander's `error: ` prefix.\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"understand-the-implementation\"></a>\n## Understand the implementation\n\n<details>\n<summary>Implementation internals — click to expand</summary>\n\nThe runner is a direct driver over the core API carrier: it resolves the Agent identity — a fresh `session-<uuid>` by default, or the persisted Session `--session-id` names — and folds the owned durable event interval into one process-level outcome.\n\n### Run flow\n\nThe runner awaits the complete application (`ctx.get('loader')?.await()`) so the composed tools and adapters are not half-mounted, validates the requested permission preset, approval service, and CLI directory grants before reading stdin, resolves the task and the shared [`agentDefaultModel`](../../core/agent-default-model/README.md) selection, then resolves the Agent identity: a fresh `session-<uuid>` by default, or the persisted Session `--session-id` names, which it adopts through [`sessionQuery`](../../session-query/session-query/README.md) and refuses when no log exists. It applies the selected preset and then the explicit approval policy before submitting the task as a user message. Auto uses the message's existing user source for task authority. Without `--json` it streams that Agent's non-empty reasoning deltas to stderr; with `--json` it projects the run instead. It waits for child work and rechecks that the parent remains idle with an unchanged Session sequence before finishing, so settlement notices can drive a final parent answer. Idle children with parked inbox messages do not delay completion; process shutdown discards their unclaimed messages. It then flushes the Session and folds the owned interval (`firstSeq` onward) into the last non-empty `assistant/message` text and the last turn's terminal reason. An unclosed last turn has no terminal reason and cannot inherit an earlier completed outcome. It writes the final text to stdout (or the `final` event) and requests exit.\n\n### Patch surface over base\n\nThe patch rides over `dsh-base`: it inherits the projection cache and shared PTC runtime, sets the coding persona prefix and separate cwd suffix on the base `system-prompt` row, inherits native tool execution and accepts a [PTC overlay](../../../apps/cli/config/examples/ptc.yml), disables the shared HMR row, and mounts the startup provider and the runner. The cache checkpoints each persisted one-shot session for later consumers; its durability barrier flushes each covered log prefix before publishing the cache row and may split otherwise coalesced JSONL runs. The startup provider ([`src/startup.ts`](src/startup.ts)) injects `ctx.cmdlineArgs` ([`dsh-cmdline`](../../boot/cmdline/README.md)), reads the task and invocation options, prints the app's `--help`, and provides `headlessStartup`; the runner reads its task, Session identity, and output mode from lazy config. It reads the explicit CLI permission preset and approval policy directly from `headlessStartup`, with runner config as the default. The sandbox-policy row also waits for this service, retaining the base mode and workspace defaults while receiving the explicit directory grants.\n\n### Exit mapping\n\nA completed final `turn/end` exits 0; any other outcome — aborted, error, or no turn in the owned interval — exits 1. An `error` reason also writes `dsh: <code>: <message>` to stderr. A live Agent failure without a later committed `turn/end` is a direct driver failure, including a summary whose start or end cannot be persisted; an earlier completed turn cannot make that run succeed. A direct driver failure (for example, Agent creation or an unusable `--session-id`) writes `dsh: <message>` to stderr and exits 1, and in `--json` mode also emits an `error` event without a `final` event.\n\n### Source map\n\n| File | Role |\n|---|---|\n| [`src/index.ts`](src/index.ts) | The `headless-runner` plugin: run flow, session resolution, output contract, exit mapping |\n| [`src/startup.ts`](src/startup.ts) | The `headless-startup` provider: task input, Session identity, permission options, and output selection |\n| [`src/json-stream.ts`](src/json-stream.ts) | The `--json` projection: event vocabulary, commit-point emission, string bounding |\n| [`cordis.patch.yml`](cordis.patch.yml) | The one-shot patch over `dsh-base` |\n| [`tests/headless.spec.ts`](tests/headless.spec.ts) | Run flow, aggregation, flush, session adoption, and exit mapping |\n| [`tests/json-stream.spec.ts`](tests/json-stream.spec.ts) | Projection ordering, commit-point emission, bounding, and disposal |\n| [`tests/startup.spec.ts`](tests/startup.spec.ts) | Command-line parsing over a real Loader tree |\n\n</details>\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"further-exploration\"></a>\n## Further Exploration\n\nRead these pages when you want to go deeper into the shared core, the sibling GUI, or the command-line handoff.\n\n- [Bundle package map](../README.md) — the surfaces built on the same core.\n- [dsh-base](../base/README.md) — the shared core headless runs on.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"packages/bundle/headless/README.zh.md\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/zh.json\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/ja.json\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/de.json\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/ru.json\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/pt.json\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/en.json\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/fr.json\npackages/bundle/headless/locale/es.json\npackages/bundle/headless/cordis.patch.yml\npackages/bundle/headless/tests/terminal-failures.spec.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/tests/startup.spec.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/tests/json-stream.spec.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/tests/headless.spec.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/tests/fixtures/provider-cwd.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/README.md\npackages/bundle/headless/package.json\npackages/bundle/headless/tsconfig.json\npackages/bundle/headless/README.i18n.yaml\npackages/bundle/headless/src/runner-internals.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/src/startup-internals.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/src/index.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/src/json-stream.ts\npackages/bundle/headless/src/startup.ts\n---\n---\ndescription: \"One-shot task mode for dsh: run a single task from the command line and get the final answer printed, for users scripting or automating dsh.\"\nkind: \"package-bundle\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-headless\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-headless` runs one dsh task from the command line and prints the final answer, then exits — no GUI, no server, no browser. Type `dsh --profile headless \"run the tests\"` and the agent handles it with the same model, tools, and safety defaults as every other surface. It suits scripts, CI, and one-off jobs: it opens no ports and leaves nothing running behind. It also offers a JSON event stream (`--json`) and `--session-id` to resume a conversation. Exit code 0 means the task completed; 1 means it aborted or errored. The boundary: one task per invocation, no interactive follow-up.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nRun one task, get the final answer, and exit. The task is the command-line argument, or stdin when you omit it; the whole invocation is the smallest working example.\n\n### Running a one-shot task\n\n```sh\ndsh --profile headless \"run the tests\"\n```\n\nThe agent works through the task, streams each non-empty provider reasoning delta to stderr under a `dsh: reasoning:` heading, then prints the final answer on stdout and exits. Consecutive reasoning deltas stay in one section, and the runner closes that section before later output when the provider supplied no trailing newline. A successful run without reasoning keeps stderr empty; a failure exits 1 and prints `dsh: <code>: <message>` to stderr. The task comes from the positional argument, or from stdin when the argument is omitted or is a lone `-`; a blank positional argument or an empty pipe is rejected before anything runs. A positional task is used as-is and stdin is not read, so put the whole prompt in the pipe when you want piped input; a piped task is sent verbatim, its trailing newline included.\n\n```sh\n{ echo \"Summarize these changes:\"; git diff --stat; } | dsh --profile headless\n```\n\nThe task and run options use these settings:\n\n| Field | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `task` | stdin | The task text; stdin supplies it when omitted or `-` |\n| `sessionId` | `session-<uuid>` | Exact Session identity to adopt; an unknown id fails |\n| `json` | `false` | Project the run as newline-delimited events on stdout |\n| `permissionMode` | session policy | Apply a configured permission preset before submitting the task, including on resume |\n\nThe generated [configuration catalog](../../../docs/config-catalog.md#deepseek-aidsh-headless) is the exhaustive source for every accepted field and its JSDoc.\n\n<a id=\"credentials\"></a>\n### Credentials\n\nThe shipped headless profile disables the managed credential store and its authorization and DeepSeek account consumers. API-key routes use the launch environment, then `<invocation cwd>/.env`, then `$DSH_HOME/.env`. Keys and sign-ins saved by the GUI are ignored without changing their file. Providers that require a saved sign-in fail unless stored authentication is restored; external provider credential chains, such as AWS profiles, remain available.\n\nThe DeepSeek Search and Fetch APIs [follow the session's DeepSeek credentials](../../web/web-deepseek-api/README.md#authentication). In the shipped headless profile, they send the DeepSeek model route's `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY` to that route's origin, so no sign-in is needed. Without that key, the model request omits `web_search`.\n\nUnset an empty launch variable to allow its `.env` fallback; an exported empty value shadows that fallback.\n\nTo restore stored authentication, add these rows to `$DSH_HOME/profiles/headless/cordis.patch.yml` or a `--patch` overlay:\n\n```yaml\n- id: credentials\n  disabled: false\n- id: authorization\n  disabled: false\n- id: deepseek-account\n  disabled: false\n```\n\nThe restored store uses [saved credentials before environment fallbacks](../../credentials/credentials-local/README.md). Web, SDK and ACP profiles keep that default ordering. Headless sessions, settings and tool behavior are unchanged; this profile choice is not a filesystem access boundary.\n\n### Choosing the session identity\n\nEvery invocation defaults to a fresh `session-<uuid>` identity, which `--json` reports in its opening `session` event. Pass `--session-id <id>` to continue that conversation: the runner adopts the persisted Session with that id, and an id with no stored Session fails before the task runs rather than quietly opening an empty history. Adoption requires the composed `sessionPersistence` and `sessionQuery` services, so a profile that omits either fails loudly instead of returning an id whose history dies with the process. An Agent already live under the requested id in this process is refused: another owner may still drive it, so the runner cannot claim an exclusive run interval over it. The identity is opaque, so the exact string is used, whitespace included. The working directory is resolved through the mounted filesystem provider (`fs.resolve('.')` and `fs.processPath()`), or the process cwd when no filesystem service is mounted; new Sessions record that directory. Adoption compares the recorded cwd with the same provider-resolved directory and refuses a Session that is a subagent session, that recorded no working directory, that runs under an agent preset this profile does not compose, or whose preset record is malformed — the check reads the preset the Session log currently records, so a Session that switched preset while blank is rejected too. A supervisor therefore cannot silently drive someone else's conversation under a different composition; any mismatch fails before the task runs.\n\nOn adoption, the runner records the current deployment route before submitting the task. An omitted effort preserves an explicit saved effort only for the same route. A step already prepared in this process keeps its captured route; subsequent steps read the shared Session selection.\n\n### Supervising implementation tasks\n\nUse one prepared Git worktree per implementation task. Start the process with that worktree as its working directory. Supply a focused task on stdin and capture stdout as NDJSON; diagnostics remain on stderr.\n\n```sh\ngit_dir=$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)\ndsh headless --permission-mode workspace-write --add-dir \"$git_dir\" --json - < task.txt > run.jsonl\n```\n\n`--add-dir` accepts repeatable physical absolute directory paths in the execution environment. The shared Git directory permits staging, committing, and repository hooks without granting the parent checkout's source files. It grants all metadata in that directory, including shared refs, hooks, and configuration. Later host Git commands can execute modified hooks or configuration outside the sandbox; use this grant for trusted tasks and review metadata changes before host Git operations. Additional grants apply to this invocation and must be supplied again when resuming. The shipped profile passes them to `sandbox-policy`; the runner checks the original parsed CLI grants directly. An overlay that replaces the policy row must include `ctx.headlessStartup.additionalWritableRoots`, or the runner rejects the request before model work. The [local sandbox provider](../../sandbox/sandbox-local/README.md) defines platform support.\n\nRead the opening `session.sessionId`, review the diff, and send revision instructions with `--session-id` from the same worktree. Repeat the directory grants. `--permission-mode` selects an existing permission preset through the permission service and overrides runner configuration and the session's saved selection before the task runs; omission preserves normal defaults and saved policy. These options do not edit saved profiles or user settings. `--approval-policy ask|never` applies after preset selection; `never` rejects calls that need human approval. Repeat explicit policy options when resuming an unattended task.\n\nAsk the worker to report its working directory, base and resulting commit, changed files, exact gate commands, exit codes, log paths, and blocked or unrun checks. Review that evidence and trust completed gate reports. Run a check again only when a revision or new finding invalidates its evidence. Bound investigation in the task, and keep publication with the supervisor. A gate blocked by host IPC, external caches, or nested sandboxing remains unrun until the supervisor authorizes and runs it in a suitable environment.\n\nA supervisor that interrupts a process must await its exit before resuming its Session. Keep the event log even after a nonzero exit. Require both process exit zero and `final.reason.kind === 'completed'` for a completed agent run, then assess the worker's task and gate report separately. A `tool_result` status of `completed` means that the tool returned; a shell command inside it can still have a nonzero exit.\n\n### Experimental Auto review\n\nFor model-reviewed full-access execution, explicitly enable [Auto review for headless supervision](../../experimental/auto-review/README.md#headless-supervision), select `--permission-mode auto --approval-policy never`, and use native tools. Auto reviews supported calls before execution and has no file sandbox. Its JSON denials let a supervisor inspect the failure and send scoped revision instructions through `--session-id`. Headless has no live approval-response channel.\n\n### Machine-readable output\n\n`--json` replaces the final-text stdout line with a newline-delimited JSON event stream, while stderr keeps only the `dsh:` diagnostics. The stream opens with `session` (carrying the identity the run used) and closes with `final`, and carries `status`, `text`, `thinking`, `tool_call`, and `tool_result` events in between. `text` and `thinking` are projected from committed assistant messages, so a retried or discarded attempt never reaches the stream; they arrive when the step commits, not per token, and default-mode stderr reasoning remains the only live text channel. The terminal `final` event carries the exact `sessionId`, the durable turn `reason` (or `null` when the final turn has no terminal event), and the same lossless answer as the default mode; it is not capped; every other string and object key is capped at 8 KiB and flagged with `truncated`, and one event line, its newline included, is capped at 32 KiB — an over-long event keeps its scalar fields, drops structured ones, and at the extreme reduces to `type` and `truncated`, while a payload nested 64 levels or deeper is cut at that depth. An empty tool-argument string projects as `{}`, matching what the executor runs, while arguments that JSON cannot round-trip — an overflowing number such as `1e400` — keep their raw text rather than the `null` that `JSON.stringify` would report. A failure the runner raises outside a turn writes an `error` event and ends the stream without `final`, in addition to the `dsh:` stderr line; a profile whose own plugins fail to load exits before the runner mounts, so that case keeps only the loader's stderr diagnostics. A turn that fails in-turn still ends with a `final` event (often empty) and no `error` event, so a well-formed stream can still describe a failed run: check the process exit status and `final.reason` before assessing the task report.\n\n`tool_result.error`, when present, preserves the durable `{ name, code, reason? }` error fields under the same output limits. `AUTO_REVIEW_DENIED` identifies a final policy denial; `AUTO_REVIEW_FAILED` identifies a reviewer failure. Either leaves the call body unexecuted. A truncated record may omit structured detail; inspect the persisted Session when the complete reason is needed.\n\n### When to use it\n\nUse headless for scripted or automated dsh runs — CI steps, batch jobs, quick answers from a terminal. Avoid it when you need a multi-turn interactive session or a GUI; the browser surface ([dsh-web-app](../web-app/README.md)) serves that. The process stays alive only for the run, opens no listening port, and exits on its own, so it fits pipelines that wait on the process. When a supervisor needs progress rather than just the answer, `--json` gives it the event stream and `--session-id` lets a later invocation continue the same conversation.\n\n### Help and task errors\n\n`dsh --profile headless --help` prints the command's help text and exits without running anything. A whitespace-only positional task is a usage error on its own — nothing runs and the process exits 1, even when stdin is not a terminal, so an accidental blank argument never consumes a pipe. A task that is absent entirely is a usage error only when stdin is a terminal; otherwise the runner reads the task from stdin and rejects an empty result the same way. A lone `-` is the only stdin marker; mixing it with other task words is a usage error rather than a task that starts with a dash. In `--json` mode every usage error — including commander's own grammar rejections such as an unknown option or a missing option value — also writes an `error` event to stdout before the process exits, so a line-oriented supervisor sees a well-formed stream even when the runner never mounts; the event `message` carries the text without commander's `error: ` prefix.\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"understand-the-implementation\"></a>\n## Understand the implementation\n\n<details>\n<summary>Implementation internals — click to expand</summary>\n\nThe runner is a direct driver over the core API carrier: it resolves the Agent identity — a fresh `session-<uuid>` by default, or the persisted Session `--session-id` names — and folds the owned durable event interval into one process-level outcome.\n\n### Run flow\n\nThe runner awaits the complete application (`ctx.get('loader')?.await()`) so the composed tools and adapters are not half-mounted, validates the requested permission preset, approval service, and CLI directory grants before reading stdin, resolves the task and the shared [`agentDefaultModel`](../../core/agent-default-model/README.md) selection, then resolves the Agent identity: a fresh `session-<uuid>` by default, or the persisted Session `--session-id` names, which it adopts through [`sessionQuery`](../../session-query/session-query/README.md) and refuses when no log exists. It applies the selected preset and then the explicit approval policy before submitting the task as a user message. Auto uses the message's existing user source for task authority. Without `--json` it streams that Agent's non-empty reasoning deltas to stderr; with `--json` it projects the run instead. It waits for child work and rechecks that the parent remains idle with an unchanged Session sequence before finishing, so settlement notices can drive a final parent answer. Idle children with parked inbox messages do not delay completion; process shutdown discards their unclaimed messages. It then flushes the Session and folds the owned interval (`firstSeq` onward) into the last non-empty `assistant/message` text and the last turn's terminal reason. An unclosed last turn has no terminal reason and cannot inherit an earlier completed outcome. It writes the final text to stdout (or the `final` event) and requests exit.\n\n### Patch surface over base\n\nThe patch rides over `dsh-base`: it inherits the projection cache and shared PTC runtime, sets the coding persona prefix and separate cwd suffix on the base `system-prompt` row, inherits native tool execution and accepts a [PTC overlay](../../../apps/cli/config/examples/ptc.yml), disables the shared HMR row, and mounts the startup provider and the runner. The cache checkpoints each persisted one-shot session for later consumers; its durability barrier flushes each covered log prefix before publishing the cache row and may split otherwise coalesced JSONL runs. The startup provider ([`src/startup.ts`](src/startup.ts)) injects `ctx.cmdlineArgs` ([`dsh-cmdline`](../../boot/cmdline/README.md)), reads the task and invocation options, prints the app's `--help`, and provides `headlessStartup`; the runner reads its task, Session identity, and output mode from lazy config. It reads the explicit CLI permission preset and approval policy directly from `headlessStartup`, with runner config as the default. The sandbox-policy row also waits for this service, retaining the base mode and workspace defaults while receiving the explicit directory grants.\n\n### Exit mapping\n\nA completed final `turn/end` exits 0; any other outcome — aborted, error, or no turn in the owned interval — exits 1. An `error` reason also writes `dsh: <code>: <message>` to stderr. A live Agent failure without a later committed `turn/end` is a direct driver failure, including a summary whose start or end cannot be persisted; an earlier completed turn cannot make that run succeed. A direct driver failure (for example, Agent creation or an unusable `--session-id`) writes `dsh: <message>` to stderr and exits 1, and in `--json` mode also emits an `error` event without a `final` event.\n\n### Source map\n\n| File | Role |\n|---|---|\n| [`src/index.ts`](src/index.ts) | The `headless-runner` plugin: run flow, session resolution, output contract, exit mapping |\n| [`src/startup.ts`](src/startup.ts) | The `headless-startup` provider: task input, Session identity, permission options, and output selection |\n| [`src/json-stream.ts`](src/json-stream.ts) | The `--json` projection: event vocabulary, commit-point emission, string bounding |\n| [`cordis.patch.yml`](cordis.patch.yml) | The one-shot patch over `dsh-base` |\n| [`tests/headless.spec.ts`](tests/headless.spec.ts) | Run flow, aggregation, flush, session adoption, and exit mapping |\n| [`tests/json-stream.spec.ts`](tests/json-stream.spec.ts) | Projection ordering, commit-point emission, bounding, and disposal |\n| [`tests/startup.spec.ts`](tests/startup.spec.ts) | Command-line parsing over a real Loader tree |\n\n</details>\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"further-exploration\"></a>\n## Further Exploration\n\nRead these pages when you want to go deeper into the shared core, the sibling GUI, or the command-line handoff.\n\n- [Bundle package map](../README.md) — the surfaces built on the same core.\n- [dsh-base](../base/README.md) — the shared core headless runs on.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\nfind packages/bundle/headless -type f | head -30; echo \\\"---\\\"; sed -n 1,160p packages/bundle/headless/README.md 2>/dev/null\"}"},"time":{"start":1793915980734,"end":1793915981322}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793915981324,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adb98cb700107lVKRmBTBonTC","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adb9879d001j0h1FiOIqvNKQO","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a1978dca6249297e","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\ngrep -rn \"exitCode\\|process.exitCode\\|process.exit(\" --include=\"*.ts\" packages/bundle/headless packages/bundle/base apps/cli/src 2>/dev/null | head -30"},"output":"apps/cli/src/process-shutdown.ts:24:  forceExit: (code: number) => void = (code) => { process.exit(code) },\napps/cli/src/process-shutdown.ts:25:  complete: (code: number) => void = (code) => { process.exitCode = code },\napps/cli/src/args.ts:211:    return process.exit(error instanceof CommanderError ? error.exitCode : 1)\napps/cli/src/plugin.ts:99:  if (result.exitCode === 127) process.stderr.write('dsh: pnpm was not found; install pnpm and make it available on PATH.\\n')\napps/cli/src/plugin.ts:103:  if (result.exitCode !== 0) process.stderr.write(`dsh: plugin command failed; diagnostics: ${result.logPath}\\n`)\napps/cli/src/plugin.ts:104:  if (result.exitCode !== 0 && args.some(argument => /^git\\+|^github:|\\.git(?:#|$)/.test(argument))) {\napps/cli/src/plugin.ts:107:  return result.exitCode\napps/cli/src/dump-config-schema.ts:16: * Print one JSON Schema document; incomplete collection or projection sets exitCode to 1.\napps/cli/src/dump-config-schema.ts:47:  if (!dump['x-cordis'].complete) process.exitCode = 1\napps/cli/src/bin.ts:46:        process.exit(1)\napps/cli/src/bin.ts:52:      process.exit(await runPlugin(invocation.profile, invocation.args, options.packageManager))\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"apps/cli/src/process-shutdown.ts:24:  forceExit: (code: number) => void = (code) => { process.exit(code) },\napps/cli/src/process-shutdown.ts:25:  complete: (code: number) => void = (code) => { process.exitCode = code },\napps/cli/src/args.ts:211:    return process.exit(error instanceof CommanderError ? error.exitCode : 1)\napps/cli/src/plugin.ts:99:  if (result.exitCode === 127) process.stderr.write('dsh: pnpm was not found; install pnpm and make it available on PATH.\\n')\napps/cli/src/plugin.ts:103:  if (result.exitCode !== 0) process.stderr.write(`dsh: plugin command failed; diagnostics: ${result.logPath}\\n`)\napps/cli/src/plugin.ts:104:  if (result.exitCode !== 0 && args.some(argument => /^git\\+|^github:|\\.git(?:#|$)/.test(argument))) {\napps/cli/src/plugin.ts:107:  return result.exitCode\napps/cli/src/dump-config-schema.ts:16: * Print one JSON Schema document; incomplete collection or projection sets exitCode to 1.\napps/cli/src/dump-config-schema.ts:47:  if (!dump['x-cordis'].complete) process.exitCode = 1\napps/cli/src/bin.ts:46:        process.exit(1)\napps/cli/src/bin.ts:52:      process.exit(await runPlugin(invocation.profile, invocation.args, options.packageManager))\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\ngrep -rn \\\"exitCode\\\\|process.exitCode\\\\|process.exit(\\\" --include=\\\"*.ts\\\" packages/bundle/headless packages/bundle/base apps/cli/src 2>/dev/null | head -30\"}"},"time":{"start":1793915980983,"end":1793915981324}}}}
