{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916104645,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbb6e65001N2mjKA36iXXvbm","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbb574c001kBQCeWBJTiDMbx","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-99e828a85c975030","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled\nls -a .dsh 2>/dev/null; echo \"---pi settings---\"; sed -n 1,40p .pi/settings.json; echo \"---hermes operator---\"; ls -a ~/.hermes 2>/dev/null | head -20"},"output":"---pi settings---\n{\n  \"lastChangelogVersion\": \"1.4.0\",\n  \"theme\": \"dark\",\n  \"defaultProvider\": \"llmgateway\",\n  \"defaultModel\": \"deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017\",\n  \"defaultThinkingLevel\": \"max\",\n  \"showHardwareCursor\": true,\n  \"autocompleteMaxVisible\": 20,\n  \"steeringMode\": \"one-at-a-time\",\n  \"followUpMode\": \"one-at-a-time\",\n  \"enableInstallTelemetry\": false,\n  \"defaultProjectTrust\": \"always\",\n  \"treeFilterMode\": \"default\",\n  \"enabledModels\": [\n    \"llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017\",\n    \"openai/gpt-6-luna\",\n    \"openai/gpt-6.1-terra\",\n    \"opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash\",\n    \"llmgateway/glm-5.3-flash\",\n    \"opencode-go/glm-5.3-flash\"\n  ],\n  \"packages\": [\n    \"extensions/pi-cache-optimizer\",\n    \"extensions/pi-fast-mode-w-subagent-support\",\n    \"npm:@juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question\",\n    \"npm:@juicesharp/rpiv-todo\",\n    \"npm:pi-blackhole\",\n    \"npm:pi-statusline\",\n    \"npm:pi-web-access\",\n    \"git:github.com/dbachelder/pi-btw\",\n    \"git:github.com/janvitos/pi-plan-build@main\",\n    \"npm:pi-claude-code-ui\"\n  ],\n  \"extensions\": [],\n  \"hideThinkingBlock\": false,\n  \"terminalTheme\": false,\n  \"compaction\": {\n    \"enabled\": false\n  },\n  \"statusLine\": {\n---hermes operator---\n.\n..\n.env\n.hermes_history\n.mcp-discovery.lock\n.skills_prompt_snapshot.json\n.update_check\naudio_cache\nauth.json\nauth.lock\nbin\nbootstrap-cache\ncache\nconfig.yaml\nconfig.yaml.bak\nconfig.yaml.bak-20260914-hermes-071\nconfig.yaml.bak-20260915-hermes-071-vision\ncron\nhermes-agent\nhermes-agent.broken-20260911-065912\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---pi settings---\n{\n  \"lastChangelogVersion\": \"1.4.0\",\n  \"theme\": \"dark\",\n  \"defaultProvider\": \"llmgateway\",\n  \"defaultModel\": \"deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017\",\n  \"defaultThinkingLevel\": \"max\",\n  \"showHardwareCursor\": true,\n  \"autocompleteMaxVisible\": 20,\n  \"steeringMode\": \"one-at-a-time\",\n  \"followUpMode\": \"one-at-a-time\",\n  \"enableInstallTelemetry\": false,\n  \"defaultProjectTrust\": \"always\",\n  \"treeFilterMode\": \"default\",\n  \"enabledModels\": [\n    \"llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017\",\n    \"openai/gpt-6-luna\",\n    \"openai/gpt-6.1-terra\",\n    \"opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash\",\n    \"llmgateway/glm-5.3-flash\",\n    \"opencode-go/glm-5.3-flash\"\n  ],\n  \"packages\": [\n    \"extensions/pi-cache-optimizer\",\n    \"extensions/pi-fast-mode-w-subagent-support\",\n    \"npm:@juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question\",\n    \"npm:@juicesharp/rpiv-todo\",\n    \"npm:pi-blackhole\",\n    \"npm:pi-statusline\",\n    \"npm:pi-web-access\",\n    \"git:github.com/dbachelder/pi-btw\",\n    \"git:github.com/janvitos/pi-plan-build@main\",\n    \"npm:pi-claude-code-ui\"\n  ],\n  \"extensions\": [],\n  \"hideThinkingBlock\": false,\n  \"terminalTheme\": false,\n  \"compaction\": {\n    \"enabled\": false\n  },\n  \"statusLine\": {\n---hermes operator---\n.\n..\n.env\n.hermes_history\n.mcp-discovery.lock\n.skills_prompt_snapshot.json\n.update_check\naudio_cache\nauth.json\nauth.lock\nbin\nbootstrap-cache\ncache\nconfig.yaml\nconfig.yaml.bak\nconfig.yaml.bak-20260914-hermes-071\nconfig.yaml.bak-20260915-hermes-071-vision\ncron\nhermes-agent\nhermes-agent.broken-20260911-065912\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled\\nls -a .dsh 2>/dev/null; echo \\\"---pi settings---\\\"; sed -n 1,40p .pi/settings.json; echo \\\"---hermes operator---\\\"; ls -a ~/.hermes 2>/dev/null | head -20\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916104293,"end":1793916104645}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916104647,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbb6cd9002OYjuGsuAWumWao","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbb574c001kBQCeWBJTiDMbx","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b34634ef6be0e5a2","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\nsed -n 1,60p packages/settings/settings/README.md; echo \"===PROJECT CONFIG SEARCH===\"; grep -rn \"\\.dsh/config\\|project config\\|workspace config\\|per-project\" packages/settings packages/boot --include=\"*.md\" | head -10"},"output":"---\ndescription: \"Inspect and edit live plugin configuration through Config-derived forms.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-settings\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nEdit fields that plugins declare with `.volatile()` and inspect their effective values. Forms identify each plugin by its profile entry id, preserve secret values, and refuse stale writes. Changes persist through the active profile’s Cordis patch.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nMount this plugin with Loader and [config-editor](../../boot/config-editor/README.md). The base bundle provides this composition.\n\n```yaml\n- id: settings\n  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-settings'\n```\n\nThis plugin has no configuration fields. Forms expose only volatile fields from active, uniquely addressed profile entries. Ordinary configuration remains editable through Cordis configuration files.\n\nOnce the Loader has settled every entry after Settings starts, a `settings.yaml` left in the harness home by earlier releases is imported once: each section is written into the entry of the same id (`ui-developer-tools` → `ui-settings`, `ui-onboarding` → `ui-settings-general`, `shell` → the platform's shell executor entry), the file is renamed to `settings.yaml.imported` before the first write, and a section the running composition rejects is logged and stays only in the renamed file.\n\nReset restores the value beneath the profile override, including schema defaults. Home patches and command-line overlays take precedence; a form write that they would override is refused.\n\nEach form reports `autoGenerate`, enabled by default, for clients that build pages from the schema; no shipped client does so yet. A plugin that ships its own page registers `configure({ auto: false }, ctx.fiber)` as an effect inside an optional `ctx.inject(['settings'], ...)` child from `apply`: the child names the plugin fiber the policy belongs to, a late-loading or replaced Settings service picks the policy up, and the business plugin runs without Settings. The policy does not remove configuration reads or writes.\n\n<a id=\"understand-the-implementation\"></a>\n## Understand the implementation\n\n<details>\n<summary>Implementation internals — click to expand</summary>\n\nThe [form projection](src/schema.ts) strips runtime references and ordinary fields. The [service](src/index.ts) supplies revisioned descriptors and validates edits against the full plugin Config before delegating persistence. Business plugins read their Config references directly.\n\nSecret roles are redacted from values, inherited values, profile overrides, and schema defaults; clients receive presence markers. Path edits preserve fields a client did not receive.\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"further-exploration\"></a>\n## Further Exploration\n\n- [Settings reference](../../../docs/subsystems/settings.md) — form values and mutations.\n- [Volatile configuration](../../../vendor/loader/README.md) — reference lifetime and notifications.\n- [Configuration editor](../../boot/config-editor/README.md) — persistence and reload ordering.\n\n<a id=\"model-experience\"></a>\n===PROJECT CONFIG SEARCH===\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\ndescription: \"Inspect and edit live plugin configuration through Config-derived forms.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-settings\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nEdit fields that plugins declare with `.volatile()` and inspect their effective values. Forms identify each plugin by its profile entry id, preserve secret values, and refuse stale writes. Changes persist through the active profile’s Cordis patch.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nMount this plugin with Loader and [config-editor](../../boot/config-editor/README.md). The base bundle provides this composition.\n\n```yaml\n- id: settings\n  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-settings'\n```\n\nThis plugin has no configuration fields. Forms expose only volatile fields from active, uniquely addressed profile entries. Ordinary configuration remains editable through Cordis configuration files.\n\nOnce the Loader has settled every entry after Settings starts, a `settings.yaml` left in the harness home by earlier releases is imported once: each section is written into the entry of the same id (`ui-developer-tools` → `ui-settings`, `ui-onboarding` → `ui-settings-general`, `shell` → the platform's shell executor entry), the file is renamed to `settings.yaml.imported` before the first write, and a section the running composition rejects is logged and stays only in the renamed file.\n\nReset restores the value beneath the profile override, including schema defaults. Home patches and command-line overlays take precedence; a form write that they would override is refused.\n\nEach form reports `autoGenerate`, enabled by default, for clients that build pages from the schema; no shipped client does so yet. A plugin that ships its own page registers `configure({ auto: false }, ctx.fiber)` as an effect inside an optional `ctx.inject(['settings'], ...)` child from `apply`: the child names the plugin fiber the policy belongs to, a late-loading or replaced Settings service picks the policy up, and the business plugin runs without Settings. The policy does not remove configuration reads or writes.\n\n<a id=\"understand-the-implementation\"></a>\n## Understand the implementation\n\n<details>\n<summary>Implementation internals — click to expand</summary>\n\nThe [form projection](src/schema.ts) strips runtime references and ordinary fields. The [service](src/index.ts) supplies revisioned descriptors and validates edits against the full plugin Config before delegating persistence. Business plugins read their Config references directly.\n\nSecret roles are redacted from values, inherited values, profile overrides, and schema defaults; clients receive presence markers. Path edits preserve fields a client did not receive.\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"further-exploration\"></a>\n## Further Exploration\n\n- [Settings reference](../../../docs/subsystems/settings.md) — form values and mutations.\n- [Volatile configuration](../../../vendor/loader/README.md) — reference lifetime and notifications.\n- [Configuration editor](../../boot/config-editor/README.md) — persistence and reload ordering.\n\n<a id=\"model-experience\"></a>\n===PROJECT CONFIG SEARCH===\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\nsed -n 1,60p packages/settings/settings/README.md; echo \\\"===PROJECT CONFIG SEARCH===\\\"; grep -rn \\\"\\\\.dsh/config\\\\|project config\\\\|workspace config\\\\|per-project\\\" packages/settings packages/boot --include=\\\"*.md\\\" | head -10\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916103897,"end":1793916104647}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1793916105398,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbb72b6001vWg4bdm0pIJIFb","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbb574c001kBQCeWBJTiDMbx","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"77de50115440e640987987f0252e1220afeb19da","cost":0.001614936,"tokens":{"input":2332,"output":256,"reasoning":1210,"cache":{"read":128512,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793916106738,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbb77f2001O37FkpF9wcgfgR","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbb72ba001HYAZFS612X3rkn","type":"step-start","snapshot":"77de50115440e640987987f0252e1220afeb19da"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916109440,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbb80e6001D3av8v1ucik0yH","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbb72ba001HYAZFS612X3rkn","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a3ac1a6e1780923b","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\ngrep -n \"maxBytes\" packages/context/agent-instructions/README.md docs/config-catalog.md | head -12; echo \"===REPO AGENTS SIZE===\"; cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled; wc -c AGENTS.md; grep -c \"\" AGENTS.md"},"output":"packages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:30:Mount this plugin when agents should work from the workspace's own instruction files. `dsh-base` already includes it with a 65,536-byte budget for each instruction chain, so base-backed profiles only need to replace the row when they want another `maxBytes`; providerless trees load nothing until a filesystem provider is present.\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:38:The defaults suit a typical checkout: `.git` marks the project root, `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` are the base candidates, and `AGENTS.local.md` and `CLAUDE.local.md` are additive local overlays. Only `maxBytes` is required — it caps the user-global chain and the project chain separately within each rendered message, so each deployment chooses its prompt budget explicitly; a message that holds both chains is at most twice `maxBytes`.\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:47:    maxBytes: 65536\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:55:  maxBytes: number\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:64:| `maxBytes` | required | Cap on each instruction chain (user-global, project) in one rendered message, in bytes; a message holding both chains is at most twice this size |\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:74:Rendering budgets the user-global chain and the project chain separately, each under `maxBytes`. Within a chain it keeps the most specific files first: it drops whole broader files, truncates the last remaining file, and then falls back to a compact intro, to the budget notice with that file's empty heading, and finally to the notice alone. User-global files are therefore omitted or truncated only when they exceed their own budget, with the harness-home file omitted before the shared-root file, except in the fallback below. A visible `Workspace instruction budget ...` notice names the omitted and truncated paths of both chains. An over-budget broad file is ignored; during refresh it is treated as temporarily unavailable rather than removed.\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:76:When all files fit within `maxBytes` together, the message holds them with no budget notice; when only one chain has files, that chain renders alone under `maxBytes`. Otherwise one `<system-reminder>` frame holds the merged budget notice when a file was omitted or truncated, one intro when a chain kept its frame, and the rendered sections of both chains in their loaded order, which puts user-global files first in a baseline; this message can exceed `maxBytes` and is at most twice that size. When a chain renders without a frame and the merged frame would exceed twice `maxBytes`, all files render as one chain under one `maxBytes` instead, which can omit a user-global file that fit its own budget; this needs a budget of a few hundred bytes or a budget notice that nearly fills or exceeds `maxBytes`. The [per-chain budget decision](../../../.agents/notes/implemented/architecture/2026-10-16-separate-instruction-chain-budgets.md) records why the two chains are budgeted separately.\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:158:The rendered baseline is appended once and remains in derived history until compaction. `maxBytes` bounds the user-global chain and the project chain separately, so a baseline that holds both is at most twice `maxBytes`; within each chain, broader files are omitted before the most-specific file is truncated, and an empty chain contributes zero tokens.\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:208:Each batch of confirmed changes or removals is one retained history message. `maxBytes` bounds each instruction chain in it, so a batch with user-global and project changes is at most twice `maxBytes`. Provider failures add no message, and an update omitted by the budget remains eligible for a later filesystem touch.\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:229:- **Budget is not shared across chains** — bytes one chain leaves unused do not extend the other, so a 70 KiB user-global file is truncated to fit `maxBytes` even when the project chain uses 1 KiB.\ndocs/config-catalog.md:90:  maxBytes: number\ndocs/config-catalog.md:377:  readonly maxBytes: number\n===REPO AGENTS SIZE===\n   27891 AGENTS.md\n302\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"packages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:30:Mount this plugin when agents should work from the workspace's own instruction files. `dsh-base` already includes it with a 65,536-byte budget for each instruction chain, so base-backed profiles only need to replace the row when they want another `maxBytes`; providerless trees load nothing until a filesystem provider is present.\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:38:The defaults suit a typical checkout: `.git` marks the project root, `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` are the base candidates, and `AGENTS.local.md` and `CLAUDE.local.md` are additive local overlays. Only `maxBytes` is required — it caps the user-global chain and the project chain separately within each rendered message, so each deployment chooses its prompt budget explicitly; a message that holds both chains is at most twice `maxBytes`.\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:47:    maxBytes: 65536\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:55:  maxBytes: number\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:64:| `maxBytes` | required | Cap on each instruction chain (user-global, project) in one rendered message, in bytes; a message holding both chains is at most twice this size |\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:74:Rendering budgets the user-global chain and the project chain separately, each under `maxBytes`. Within a chain it keeps the most specific files first: it drops whole broader files, truncates the last remaining file, and then falls back to a compact intro, to the budget notice with that file's empty heading, and finally to the notice alone. User-global files are therefore omitted or truncated only when they exceed their own budget, with the harness-home file omitted before the shared-root file, except in the fallback below. A visible `Workspace instruction budget ...` notice names the omitted and truncated paths of both chains. An over-budget broad file is ignored; during refresh it is treated as temporarily unavailable rather than removed.\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:76:When all files fit within `maxBytes` together, the message holds them with no budget notice; when only one chain has files, that chain renders alone under `maxBytes`. Otherwise one `<system-reminder>` frame holds the merged budget notice when a file was omitted or truncated, one intro when a chain kept its frame, and the rendered sections of both chains in their loaded order, which puts user-global files first in a baseline; this message can exceed `maxBytes` and is at most twice that size. When a chain renders without a frame and the merged frame would exceed twice `maxBytes`, all files render as one chain under one `maxBytes` instead, which can omit a user-global file that fit its own budget; this needs a budget of a few hundred bytes or a budget notice that nearly fills or exceeds `maxBytes`. The [per-chain budget decision](../../../.agents/notes/implemented/architecture/2026-10-16-separate-instruction-chain-budgets.md) records why the two chains are budgeted separately.\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:158:The rendered baseline is appended once and remains in derived history until compaction. `maxBytes` bounds the user-global chain and the project chain separately, so a baseline that holds both is at most twice `maxBytes`; within each chain, broader files are omitted before the most-specific file is truncated, and an empty chain contributes zero tokens.\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:208:Each batch of confirmed changes or removals is one retained history message. `maxBytes` bounds each instruction chain in it, so a batch with user-global and project changes is at most twice `maxBytes`. Provider failures add no message, and an update omitted by the budget remains eligible for a later filesystem touch.\npackages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:229:- **Budget is not shared across chains** — bytes one chain leaves unused do not extend the other, so a 70 KiB user-global file is truncated to fit `maxBytes` even when the project chain uses 1 KiB.\ndocs/config-catalog.md:90:  maxBytes: number\ndocs/config-catalog.md:377:  readonly maxBytes: number\n===REPO AGENTS SIZE===\n   27891 AGENTS.md\n302\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\ngrep -n \\\"maxBytes\\\" packages/context/agent-instructions/README.md docs/config-catalog.md | head -12; echo \\\"===REPO AGENTS SIZE===\\\"; cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled; wc -c AGENTS.md; grep -c \\\"\\\" AGENTS.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916109030,"end":1793916109440}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1793916110241,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbb85a1001xa3MNfY9FPwfPC","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbb72ba001HYAZFS612X3rkn","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"77de50115440e640987987f0252e1220afeb19da","cost":0.001289406,"tokens":{"input":2817,"output":163,"reasoning":628,"cache":{"read":130752,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793916111628,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbb8b0c001uM7X6Z0edNw50T","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbb85a6001gjCWJxnX4b51tS","type":"step-start","snapshot":"77de50115440e640987987f0252e1220afeb19da"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916120049,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbb8fd4002tiboD3buYAVvPI","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbb85a6001gjCWJxnX4b51tS","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b8fa6b4725b2dfe","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 3: Profiles, `$DSH_HOME` and repo-local configuration\n\n## Focus\n\nAngle 3: what the harness reads from the working directory and its parents, what lives only under\n`$DSH_HOME`, whether a repository can carry a project profile or patch overlay the way `.pi/` and\n`.hermes/` carry runtime configuration, and what a repo-root `.dsh/` folder should contain.\n\n## Actions Taken\n\n- Read the `agent-instructions` package reference for the workspace instruction chain.\n- Read the `home-paths` and `app-boot` references for home resolution and profile layout.\n- Read the `settings` reference to establish what is profile-scoped and what is not.\n- Inspected the profile a live `dsh headless --help`/smoke initialized on this machine and the\n  lineage-local one.\n- Compared the `.pi/` and `.hermes/` surface inventories and their `SYNC.md` manifests.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. The harness home resolves explicit configuration first, then `$DSH_HOME`, then `~/.dsh`; an\n   empty or whitespace-only `$DSH_HOME` is treated as unset. The shared agents root resolves the\n   same way from `$DSH_AGENTS_HOME` or `~/.agents` and is deliberately outside the harness home\n   because other agent tools share it. [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/util/home-paths/README.md:34-52]\n2. A profile is a directory at `$DSH_HOME/profiles/<name>` containing `cordis.yml`,\n   `cordis.patch.yml`, `package.json` and `pnpm-workspace.yaml` (the four files observed in a\n   first boot), plus a shared `profiles/node_modules`. There is no project-level profile lookup:\n   the composition comes from the harness home, never from the checkout.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/boot/app-boot/README.md:52]\n   [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/.dsh-home/profiles/headless]\n3. Workspace instructions are the one project-surface the harness reads natively. The first request\n   carries a baseline of `$DSH_HOME/AGENTS.md`, then `<agentsHome>/AGENTS.md`, then every project\n   candidate from the `.git` root down to the session working directory, broad to specific.\n   Candidates default to `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md`, with `AGENTS.local.md` and\n   `CLAUDE.local.md` as additive local overlays; identical content renders once.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:34-38]\n4. Each instruction chain gets its own `maxBytes` budget, 65,536 bytes in `dsh-base`; broader files\n   are dropped before the most specific file is truncated, and an over-budget broad file is ignored.\n   The repo's root `AGENTS.md` is 27,891 bytes, so it fits, but a nested chain could still be\n   truncated. Loading refreshes on a working-directory change and on first-party `read`, `write` or\n   `edit` touches; shell navigation does not participate.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:30-40]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:74-76]\n   [SOURCE: AGENTS.md:1]\n5. `--patch <path>` is the only repo-local configuration lever: a repeatable overlay list applied\n   after the profile layer, with the same row grammar as `cordis.patch.yml` (`id`, `disabled`,\n   `config`). It can replace the default-model row, enable a route, or disable a plugin per\n   dispatch, which is exactly the \"project overlay\" role `.pi/settings.json` plays for Pi.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/cli-help.md:41-42]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/apps/cli/config/examples/ptc.yml:1-4]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/apps/cli/config/examples/team-mode.yml:1-2]\n6. `$DSH_HOME` can be relocated per lineage; pointing it at a lineage subdirectory produced a\n   self-contained `headless` profile, its own session tree and its own storages, with no dependency\n   on `~/.dsh`. That is the state-isolation mechanism a fan-out lineage needs, and it also means the\n   repo folder must not itself be `$DSH_HOME` by default, or every checkout would own a profile and\n   package tree. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/.dsh-home]\n7. A `.dsh/` repo folder therefore has a smaller configuration surface than `.pi/` or `.hermes/`:\n   `patches/` for route and behavior overlays, `SYNC.md` for the derivation inventory, generated\n   `skills/`, `agents/` and `prompts/` trees where the later angles show the loader needs them, and\n   a hand-authored `plugins/` bridge for guards. It has no `settings.json` analogue, because\n   defaults are an `agent-default-model` config row and settings persist into the active profile\n   patch. [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/settings/settings/README.md:12-24]\n   [SOURCE: .pi/settings.json:1-40]\n   [SOURCE: .hermes/SYNC.md:20-40]\n8. The `.pi/` precedent splits generated from authored exactly where the harness forces it: agents\n   and prompts are generated compiler output, skills are a whole-directory symlink, extensions are\n   hand-authored bridges, and `settings.json`/`models.json`/`mcp.json` are hand-authored. `.hermes/`\n   generates markdown-only skill copies because its scanner quarantines symlinked trees. `.dsh/`\n   should follow the Pi split for generated trees and reserve hand authoring for the patch files\n   and the plugin bridge. [SOURCE: .pi/SYNC.md:18-40]\n   [SOURCE: .hermes/SYNC.md:20-40]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- A repo-local profile directory: profile lookup is `$DSH_HOME`-only, and a checked-in profile would\n  need `node_modules` and a package install per checkout.\n- A `.dsh/settings.json` analogue: settings persist through the active profile's Cordis patch, and\n  the defaults a Pi settings file carries live in an `agent-default-model` row.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Trying to discover a project config folder: none is documented in `settings`, `boot` or the\n  config catalog; the only project reads found are the instruction chain and a cwd `.env`.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: \"what the harness reads from the working directory\" could include the sandbox\n  workspace root; the instruction chain and `.env` are the two confirmed reads, the sandbox root is\n  a write boundary rather than a config source.\n- Contradictory evidence: the resource map said no `~/.dsh` existed; a read-only `dsh headless\n  --help` created `~/.dsh/profiles/headless` on its own, which the angle's read-only allowance\n  permits. The map's statement was true when it was recorded and is now stale, not wrong.\n- Missing dependencies: none.\n- Partial success: the profile file list is observed from a first boot, not from documentation.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- `dsh-src/packages/util/home-paths/README.md:34-52`\n- `dsh-src/packages/boot/app-boot/README.md:52`\n- `dsh-src/packages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:30-76`\n- `dsh-src/packages/settings/settings/README.md:12-24`\n- `dsh-src/docs/cli-help.md:41-42`\n- `dsh-src/apps/cli/config/examples/ptc.yml`, `team-mode.yml`\n- `AGENTS.md:1` (27,891 bytes)\n- `.pi/SYNC.md:18-40`, `.hermes/SYNC.md:20-40`, `.pi/settings.json:1-40`\n- Lineage-local `.dsh-home` profile and session tree\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 0.86 (five fully new findings, two that extend the resource map's\n  `$DSH_HOME` and `--patch` facts)\n- Questions addressed: Angle 3 profiles, home, project configuration and repo folder shape\n- Questions answered: What does the harness read from the working directory and `$DSH_HOME`, and what should a repo-local runtime folder contain?\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: inspecting the profile a real boot created answered the \"what does a\n  profile contain\" question faster than the boot README, which describes the composition rather\n  than the file list.\n- What did not work and why: searching for a project config folder returned nothing, which is\n  itself the answer but needed corroboration from three package references before it was safe to\n  state.\n- What I would do differently: record the first-boot side effect (`~/.dsh`) in iteration 1, so the\n  resource map's now-stale line is traceable to a specific command.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nAngle 4: the skill loader, its directories and frontmatter contract, and whether `.dsh/skills`\nshould be a symlink or a generated copy tree.\n","path":"specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-003.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-003.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-003.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 3: Profiles, `$DSH_HOME` and repo-local configuration\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nAngle 3: what the harness reads from the working directory and its parents, what lives only under\\n`$DSH_HOME`, whether a repository can carry a project profile or patch overlay the way `.pi/` and\\n`.hermes/` carry runtime configuration, and what a repo-root `.dsh/` folder should contain.\\n\\n## Actions Taken\\n\\n- Read the `agent-instructions` package reference for the workspace instruction chain.\\n- Read the `home-paths` and `app-boot` references for home resolution and profile layout.\\n- Read the `settings` reference to establish what is profile-scoped and what is not.\\n- Inspected the profile a live `dsh headless --help`/smoke initialized on this machine and the\\n  lineage-local one.\\n- Compared the `.pi/` and `.hermes/` surface inventories and their `SYNC.md` manifests.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. The harness home resolves explicit configuration first, then `$DSH_HOME`, then `~/.dsh`; an\\n   empty or whitespace-only `$DSH_HOME` is treated as unset. The shared agents root resolves the\\n   same way from `$DSH_AGENTS_HOME` or `~/.agents` and is deliberately outside the harness home\\n   because other agent tools share it. [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/util/home-paths/README.md:34-52]\\n2. A profile is a directory at `$DSH_HOME/profiles/<name>` containing `cordis.yml`,\\n   `cordis.patch.yml`, `package.json` and `pnpm-workspace.yaml` (the four files observed in a\\n   first boot), plus a shared `profiles/node_modules`. There is no project-level profile lookup:\\n   the composition comes from the harness home, never from the checkout.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/boot/app-boot/README.md:52]\\n   [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/.dsh-home/profiles/headless]\\n3. Workspace instructions are the one project-surface the harness reads natively. The first request\\n   carries a baseline of `$DSH_HOME/AGENTS.md`, then `<agentsHome>/AGENTS.md`, then every project\\n   candidate from the `.git` root down to the session working directory, broad to specific.\\n   Candidates default to `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md`, with `AGENTS.local.md` and\\n   `CLAUDE.local.md` as additive local overlays; identical content renders once.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:34-38]\\n4. Each instruction chain gets its own `maxBytes` budget, 65,536 bytes in `dsh-base`; broader files\\n   are dropped before the most specific file is truncated, and an over-budget broad file is ignored.\\n   The repo's root `AGENTS.md` is 27,891 bytes, so it fits, but a nested chain could still be\\n   truncated. Loading refreshes on a working-directory change and on first-party `read`, `write` or\\n   `edit` touches; shell navigation does not participate.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:30-40]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:74-76]\\n   [SOURCE: AGENTS.md:1]\\n5. `--patch <path>` is the only repo-local configuration lever: a repeatable overlay list applied\\n   after the profile layer, with the same row grammar as `cordis.patch.yml` (`id`, `disabled`,\\n   `config`). It can replace the default-model row, enable a route, or disable a plugin per\\n   dispatch, which is exactly the \\\"project overlay\\\" role `.pi/settings.json` plays for Pi.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/cli-help.md:41-42]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/apps/cli/config/examples/ptc.yml:1-4]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/apps/cli/config/examples/team-mode.yml:1-2]\\n6. `$DSH_HOME` can be relocated per lineage; pointing it at a lineage subdirectory produced a\\n   self-contained `headless` profile, its own session tree and its own storages, with no dependency\\n   on `~/.dsh`. That is the state-isolation mechanism a fan-out lineage needs, and it also means the\\n   repo folder must not itself be `$DSH_HOME` by default, or every checkout would own a profile and\\n   package tree. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/.dsh-home]\\n7. A `.dsh/` repo folder therefore has a smaller configuration surface than `.pi/` or `.hermes/`:\\n   `patches/` for route and behavior overlays, `SYNC.md` for the derivation inventory, generated\\n   `skills/`, `agents/` and `prompts/` trees where the later angles show the loader needs them, and\\n   a hand-authored `plugins/` bridge for guards. It has no `settings.json` analogue, because\\n   defaults are an `agent-default-model` config row and settings persist into the active profile\\n   patch. [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/settings/settings/README.md:12-24]\\n   [SOURCE: .pi/settings.json:1-40]\\n   [SOURCE: .hermes/SYNC.md:20-40]\\n8. The `.pi/` precedent splits generated from authored exactly where the harness forces it: agents\\n   and prompts are generated compiler output, skills are a whole-directory symlink, extensions are\\n   hand-authored bridges, and `settings.json`/`models.json`/`mcp.json` are hand-authored. `.hermes/`\\n   generates markdown-only skill copies because its scanner quarantines symlinked trees. `.dsh/`\\n   should follow the Pi split for generated trees and reserve hand authoring for the patch files\\n   and the plugin bridge. [SOURCE: .pi/SYNC.md:18-40]\\n   [SOURCE: .hermes/SYNC.md:20-40]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- A repo-local profile directory: profile lookup is `$DSH_HOME`-only, and a checked-in profile would\\n  need `node_modules` and a package install per checkout.\\n- A `.dsh/settings.json` analogue: settings persist through the active profile's Cordis patch, and\\n  the defaults a Pi settings file carries live in an `agent-default-model` row.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Trying to discover a project config folder: none is documented in `settings`, `boot` or the\\n  config catalog; the only project reads found are the instruction chain and a cwd `.env`.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: \\\"what the harness reads from the working directory\\\" could include the sandbox\\n  workspace root; the instruction chain and `.env` are the two confirmed reads, the sandbox root is\\n  a write boundary rather than a config source.\\n- Contradictory evidence: the resource map said no `~/.dsh` existed; a read-only `dsh headless\\n  --help` created `~/.dsh/profiles/headless` on its own, which the angle's read-only allowance\\n  permits. The map's statement was true when it was recorded and is now stale, not wrong.\\n- Missing dependencies: none.\\n- Partial success: the profile file list is observed from a first boot, not from documentation.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- `dsh-src/packages/util/home-paths/README.md:34-52`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/boot/app-boot/README.md:52`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/context/agent-instructions/README.md:30-76`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/settings/settings/README.md:12-24`\\n- `dsh-src/docs/cli-help.md:41-42`\\n- `dsh-src/apps/cli/config/examples/ptc.yml`, `team-mode.yml`\\n- `AGENTS.md:1` (27,891 bytes)\\n- `.pi/SYNC.md:18-40`, `.hermes/SYNC.md:20-40`, `.pi/settings.json:1-40`\\n- Lineage-local `.dsh-home` profile and session tree\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 0.86 (five fully new findings, two that extend the resource map's\\n  `$DSH_HOME` and `--patch` facts)\\n- Questions addressed: Angle 3 profiles, home, project configuration and repo folder shape\\n- Questions answered: What does the harness read from the working directory and `$DSH_HOME`, and what should a repo-local runtime folder contain?\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: inspecting the profile a real boot created answered the \\\"what does a\\n  profile contain\\\" question faster than the boot README, which describes the composition rather\\n  than the file list.\\n- What did not work and why: searching for a project config folder returned nothing, which is\\n  itself the answer but needed corroboration from three package references before it was safe to\\n  state.\\n- What I would do differently: record the first-boot side effect (`~/.dsh`) in iteration 1, so the\\n  resource map's now-stale line is traceable to a specific command.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nAngle 4: the skill loader, its directories and frontmatter contract, and whether `.dsh/skills`\\nshould be a symlink or a generated copy tree.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-003.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916112852,"end":1793916120049}}}}
