{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916142648,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbc02dc001a6gUEvfu53ClqD","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbbfb81001kbTcexxPaNQQy5","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-aeffb7d21e5509a2","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\nsed -n 1,60p packages/skill/skill/README.md 2>/dev/null; echo \"===TOOL CATALOG===\"; grep -n \"skill\" docs/tool-catalog.md | head -20"},"output":"---\ndescription: \"The skill provider registry for users and maintainers choosing, configuring, or debugging how skills from any source are merged, resolved, and loaded.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-skill\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nUse this package to give agents and users one catalog of reusable, task-specific instructions collected from local directories, embedded plugin data, or remote services. It resolves duplicate names predictably, validates entries, tolerates unavailable sources without discarding usable results, and loads the selected skill's full instructions on demand. Mount it when a composition needs skills from multiple or non-filesystem sources; pair it with `dsh-skill-filesystem` for local discovery and `dsh-tool-skill` for model access, because it includes no skill content itself.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nMount the plugin to give a composition one skill registry. Skill sources (providers) and consumers (the model-facing catalog and loader, or your own code) all talk to `ctx.skills`; the registry merges everything any provider reports, so one lookup sees skills from every source.\n\n### When to choose it\n\nUse `dsh-skill` when agents should load skills from more than one source through one interface, or when the source of skills is not the local filesystem. Avoid it when a composition needs no skill loading at all — the plugin adds a service and a per-lookup discovery cost. The shipped local provider (`dsh-skill-filesystem`) and the model-facing consumer (`dsh-tool-skill`) are separate packages; mount them alongside when the deployment wants local skills and model access.\n\n### Mount and configure\n\nLoad the plugin like any Cordis plugin. The only configuration limits how many completed provider catalogs are kept in memory; everything else is provider behavior.\n\n```yaml\n- name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-skill'\n```\n\n| Field | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `collectCacheMaxEntries` | `128` | Completed cwd/provider catalogs kept in memory |\n\nThe generated [configuration catalog](../../../docs/config-catalog.md#deepseek-aidsh-skill) is the exhaustive source for every accepted field.\n\n### What the registry gives you\n\n- **One merged catalog.** A consumer asks for the current catalog of a workspace and receives every winning skill summary from every provider, sorted by name — no provider-specific ordering or deduplication to do.\n- **On-demand loading.** Asking for one skill by name returns the full instruction body from whichever provider owns the winning candidate; the registry re-validates the loaded definition and rejects a stale selection whose name changed between discovery and load.\n- **Embedded skills.** Plugins register an in-memory skill with `ctx.skills.register(...)`; the registry fills in a default invocation policy and the `runtime` provider label. Same-name runtime registrations in one layer are first-wins with a warning.\n- **Provider registration.** A provider contributes its catalog with `ctx.skills.registerProvider(...)`; registration is synchronous, and the returned disposer removes the provider. `runtime` is a reserved provider name.\n\nAn invocation policy on every skill decides which surfaces may advertise and load it: `modelInvocable` for model-facing tools and catalogs, `userInvocable` for human-facing commands. The registry keeps all four combinations, so one discovery result can serve both surfaces without conflating their catalogs.\n\n| Policy | Model | User |\n|---|---|---|\n| `{ modelInvocable: true, userInvocable: true }` | included | included |\n| `{ modelInvocable: true, userInvocable: false }` | included | excluded |\n===TOOL CATALOG===\n22:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-action-cards` | `propose_action` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.agents`, `ctx.sessionProjections`, `ctx.remoteAccess` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `plugin:action-cards/decision`, `user/message` | - | Declared only to an Agent that loaded the skill of a registered card kind; the kind packages register their actions, and the Personalized recommendations bundle supplies the shipped kinds. |\n40:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-skill` | `skill` | `ctx.workingDirectory`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.agents`, `ctx.skills` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `user/message replacement catalogs via agent.inject()` | - | - |\n507:Show the user one optional action card under this answer. Call only as the skill that names the action instructs.\n515:      \"description\": \"Action name from the skill.\"\n527:      \"description\": \"Fields the skill lists for this action.\",\n542:Declared only to an Agent that loaded the skill of a registered card kind; the kind packages register their actions, and the Personalized recommendations bundle supplies the shipped kinds.\n1731:<a id=\"deepseek-aidsh-tool-skill\"></a>\n1733:## `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-skill`\n1735:### `skill`\n1737:Load the full instructions for a skill. Call it before acting on a task that names or clearly matches a skill in the session skill catalog.\n1745:      \"description\": \"The exact skill name from the available skills list.\"\n1754:Source: [`packages/skill/tool-skill/src/index.ts`](../packages/skill/tool-skill/src/index.ts)\n2648:Source: [`packages/skill/tool-workspace-dependencies/src/index.ts`](../packages/skill/tool-workspace-dependencies/src/index.ts)\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\ndescription: \"The skill provider registry for users and maintainers choosing, configuring, or debugging how skills from any source are merged, resolved, and loaded.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-skill\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nUse this package to give agents and users one catalog of reusable, task-specific instructions collected from local directories, embedded plugin data, or remote services. It resolves duplicate names predictably, validates entries, tolerates unavailable sources without discarding usable results, and loads the selected skill's full instructions on demand. Mount it when a composition needs skills from multiple or non-filesystem sources; pair it with `dsh-skill-filesystem` for local discovery and `dsh-tool-skill` for model access, because it includes no skill content itself.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nMount the plugin to give a composition one skill registry. Skill sources (providers) and consumers (the model-facing catalog and loader, or your own code) all talk to `ctx.skills`; the registry merges everything any provider reports, so one lookup sees skills from every source.\n\n### When to choose it\n\nUse `dsh-skill` when agents should load skills from more than one source through one interface, or when the source of skills is not the local filesystem. Avoid it when a composition needs no skill loading at all — the plugin adds a service and a per-lookup discovery cost. The shipped local provider (`dsh-skill-filesystem`) and the model-facing consumer (`dsh-tool-skill`) are separate packages; mount them alongside when the deployment wants local skills and model access.\n\n### Mount and configure\n\nLoad the plugin like any Cordis plugin. The only configuration limits how many completed provider catalogs are kept in memory; everything else is provider behavior.\n\n```yaml\n- name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-skill'\n```\n\n| Field | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `collectCacheMaxEntries` | `128` | Completed cwd/provider catalogs kept in memory |\n\nThe generated [configuration catalog](../../../docs/config-catalog.md#deepseek-aidsh-skill) is the exhaustive source for every accepted field.\n\n### What the registry gives you\n\n- **One merged catalog.** A consumer asks for the current catalog of a workspace and receives every winning skill summary from every provider, sorted by name — no provider-specific ordering or deduplication to do.\n- **On-demand loading.** Asking for one skill by name returns the full instruction body from whichever provider owns the winning candidate; the registry re-validates the loaded definition and rejects a stale selection whose name changed between discovery and load.\n- **Embedded skills.** Plugins register an in-memory skill with `ctx.skills.register(...)`; the registry fills in a default invocation policy and the `runtime` provider label. Same-name runtime registrations in one layer are first-wins with a warning.\n- **Provider registration.** A provider contributes its catalog with `ctx.skills.registerProvider(...)`; registration is synchronous, and the returned disposer removes the provider. `runtime` is a reserved provider name.\n\nAn invocation policy on every skill decides which surfaces may advertise and load it: `modelInvocable` for model-facing tools and catalogs, `userInvocable` for human-facing commands. The registry keeps all four combinations, so one discovery result can serve both surfaces without conflating their catalogs.\n\n| Policy | Model | User |\n|---|---|---|\n| `{ modelInvocable: true, userInvocable: true }` | included | included |\n| `{ modelInvocable: true, userInvocable: false }` | included | excluded |\n===TOOL CATALOG===\n22:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-action-cards` | `propose_action` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.agents`, `ctx.sessionProjections`, `ctx.remoteAccess` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `plugin:action-cards/decision`, `user/message` | - | Declared only to an Agent that loaded the skill of a registered card kind; the kind packages register their actions, and the Personalized recommendations bundle supplies the shipped kinds. |\n40:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-skill` | `skill` | `ctx.workingDirectory`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.agents`, `ctx.skills` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `user/message replacement catalogs via agent.inject()` | - | - |\n507:Show the user one optional action card under this answer. Call only as the skill that names the action instructs.\n515:      \"description\": \"Action name from the skill.\"\n527:      \"description\": \"Fields the skill lists for this action.\",\n542:Declared only to an Agent that loaded the skill of a registered card kind; the kind packages register their actions, and the Personalized recommendations bundle supplies the shipped kinds.\n1731:<a id=\"deepseek-aidsh-tool-skill\"></a>\n1733:## `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-skill`\n1735:### `skill`\n1737:Load the full instructions for a skill. Call it before acting on a task that names or clearly matches a skill in the session skill catalog.\n1745:      \"description\": \"The exact skill name from the available skills list.\"\n1754:Source: [`packages/skill/tool-skill/src/index.ts`](../packages/skill/tool-skill/src/index.ts)\n2648:Source: [`packages/skill/tool-workspace-dependencies/src/index.ts`](../packages/skill/tool-workspace-dependencies/src/index.ts)\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\nsed -n 1,60p packages/skill/skill/README.md 2>/dev/null; echo \\\"===TOOL CATALOG===\\\"; grep -n \\\"skill\\\" docs/tool-catalog.md | head -20\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916142300,"end":1793916142648}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916142651,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbc0136002RWciHMkIGHkfCa","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbbfb81001kbTcexxPaNQQy5","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-88dcdcdbd5253eb5","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\nls packages/skill; echo \"===FS README===\"; grep -n \"skills\\|SKILL.md\\|frontmatter\\|scan\\|quarantine\\|limit\\|directory\" packages/skill/skill-filesystem/README.md | head -40"},"output":"README.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\nskill\nskill-filesystem\nskill-office\ntool-skill\ntool-workspace-dependencies\n===FS README===\n2:description: \"The local filesystem skill provider for users and maintainers authoring local skills or configuring how project, custom, and user skill roots are discovered and watched.\"\n12:Agents can use local skills from the repository, a custom directory, or the user's agent configuration: author a skill as a directory bundle with a `SKILL.md` or a flat `<name>.md` file under any scanned root, and it appears in the session catalog. The provider discovers the project, custom, and user roots, parses each skill's YAML frontmatter, and watches the directories, so new, renamed, or deleted skills reach agents without a restart. Choose it when skills live on disk — the registry (`dsh-skill`) accepts any provider, and another provider can supply skills from elsewhere.\n20:- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n28:Mount the plugin to make local skills available to agents. It scans the project, custom, and user skill roots below, parses each skill's frontmatter into a catalog entry, and loads the body on demand; it also watches the roots so new, renamed, or deleted skills reach the next catalog without a restart.\n32:Use this provider when skills live on disk — in the repository, a custom directory, or the user's agent configuration. Avoid it when skills come from a remote registry or embedded plugin data: the registry accepts any provider, and this package is one implementation.\n36:A skill is either a directory bundle `<name>/SKILL.md` or a flat file `<name>.md` at the top level of a scanned root; nested `**/SKILL.md` files are deliberately not discovered. The file starts with YAML frontmatter: required `name` and `description`, plus optional `whenToUse`, `metadata`, `disable-model-invocation`, and `user-invocable`.\n40:Catalog entries and loaded skills expose the resolved instruction-file path, so symlinked directories and flat files can open as regular-file previews. Reload locators and resource bases retain the discovered paths, including symlinks.\n42:The catalog and the body have separate lifecycles: discovery parses frontmatter into the catalog entry, and every load re-reads the current file, so editing a skill body needs no versioning or cache invalidation.\n46:Default roots are scanned in this provider's rank order:\n50:| 100 | `project-dsh` | `<projectRoot>/.dsh/skills` |\n51:| 200 | `project-agents` | `<projectRoot>/.agents/skills` |\n53:| 400 | `user-dsh` | `<dshHome>/skills` |\n54:| 500 | `user-agents` | `<agentsHome>/skills` |\n60:Load the plugin alongside the skill registry; it requires `ctx.skills`.\n69:| `providerName` | `filesystem` | Unique provider name registered on `ctx.skills` |\n71:| `dshHome` | `$DSH_HOME` or `~/.dsh` | Harness config root; its `skills` subdirectory is scanned |\n72:| `agentsHome` | `$DSH_AGENTS_HOME` or `~/.agents` | Shared agent config root scanned for compatible skills |\n75:| `bundledSkillDir` | — | Bundled skill root scanned at rank 600 when configured |\n81:Existing roots are watched, so adding, renaming, or deleting a skill (or editing its frontmatter) triggers a catalog refresh for the next model step; edits below `references`, `scripts`, `assets`, and other bundle resources do not. The first-party `write` and `edit` tools invalidate the provider directly when their target could affect a watched skill, so the model observes its own filesystem mutation without waiting for the host watcher. External IDE, Git, and shell changes are picked up by the host watcher, and a root that does not exist yet is probed until it appears.\n85:A valid skill under any scanned root appears in the session catalog sorted by name, and loading it returns the current file body. A file without valid frontmatter, an invalid name, or an invalid invocation value is skipped with a warning, so the model catalog receives no per-skill diagnostic and cannot distinguish an absent skill from an invalid one. A root whose discovery or file read fails is logged with its path and omitted; other roots remain available. The observation is incomplete and is retried on the next lookup rather than cached. The model catalog identifies incomplete discovery separately from confirmed absence.\n99:The provider is built on two separations. First, catalog versus body: discovery parses frontmatter into summaries, while every load re-reads the file, so body edits need no hash, revision, or cache invalidation. Second, discovery versus watching: `list()` scans roots and resolves the project root through `ctx.fs` when a filesystem service is present (falling back to abortable Node I/O), while a separate watch manager owns Chokidar handles, missing-root probes, and invalidation.\n105:| [`src/index.ts`](src/index.ts) | Plugin entry, provider, root resolution, frontmatter parsing, watch manager |\n109:Discovery resolves the root list for the lookup cwd, asks the watch manager to attach to each root, then scans each root's direct entries: directory bundles resolve `<name>/SKILL.md`, flat files resolve `<name>.md`. Each file is parsed for frontmatter — `name` must be kebab-case, `description` is required, and the invocation keys resolve through the strict boolean grammar — and candidates carry the root's source label and rank so the registry can merge them with other providers. Confirmed missing paths are valid empty state; malformed or non-text entries warn and skip.\n113:Existing roots are watched by Chokidar at depth 1; a root that does not exist is followed from its nearest existing ancestor one missing segment at a time using `fs.watchFile`. Relevant events — direct bundle add/remove, flat `.md` add/remove, and direct `SKILL.md` add/remove/change — coalesce into one provider invalidation per microtask batch, while resource-subtree changes are ignored. The watch manager is bounded by `watchMaxProjects`, logs and retries failed startup, and closes every handle at teardown. First-party `write`/`edit` mutations invalidate synchronously through the `fs/observed` event.\n122:Read these pages when the package-level contract is not enough. They move from the registry contract to the consumer that renders discovered skills and the home-path resolution used by the config defaults.\n124:- [Skill subsystem reference](../../../docs/subsystems/skills.md) — the registry contract and the local discovery priority table.\n126:- [tool-skill package](../tool-skill/README.md) — how discovered skills reach the session catalog and the model.\n134:Indirectly, through `dsh-tool-skill`, which renders this provider's invocable names and capped descriptions into the initial or replacement catalog and a selected current instruction body plus resource-base guidance into retained tool history while paths, provider ranks, and disabled skills remain hidden.\n142:<a id=\"known-limitations-and-deferred-work\"></a>\n145:These limits define when the provider is a poor fit or needs special operational care. They are current package constraints, not a task backlog.\n147:- **Discovery is one level deep** — only `<root>/<name>/SKILL.md` and `<root>/<name>.md` are recognized; nested skill trees and package manifests are ignored.\n160:This Dev Note is working context for maintainers and is explicitly non-authoritative — shipped behavior and limits live in the sections above and in the code. A TODO in `src/index.ts` proposes extracting the Chokidar and missing-root observation into a Cordis file-watch service, keeping skill filtering and invalidation here; the missing-root polling tradeoff documented above is part of that open design.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"README.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\nskill\nskill-filesystem\nskill-office\ntool-skill\ntool-workspace-dependencies\n===FS README===\n2:description: \"The local filesystem skill provider for users and maintainers authoring local skills or configuring how project, custom, and user skill roots are discovered and watched.\"\n12:Agents can use local skills from the repository, a custom directory, or the user's agent configuration: author a skill as a directory bundle with a `SKILL.md` or a flat `<name>.md` file under any scanned root, and it appears in the session catalog. The provider discovers the project, custom, and user roots, parses each skill's YAML frontmatter, and watches the directories, so new, renamed, or deleted skills reach agents without a restart. Choose it when skills live on disk — the registry (`dsh-skill`) accepts any provider, and another provider can supply skills from elsewhere.\n20:- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n28:Mount the plugin to make local skills available to agents. It scans the project, custom, and user skill roots below, parses each skill's frontmatter into a catalog entry, and loads the body on demand; it also watches the roots so new, renamed, or deleted skills reach the next catalog without a restart.\n32:Use this provider when skills live on disk — in the repository, a custom directory, or the user's agent configuration. Avoid it when skills come from a remote registry or embedded plugin data: the registry accepts any provider, and this package is one implementation.\n36:A skill is either a directory bundle `<name>/SKILL.md` or a flat file `<name>.md` at the top level of a scanned root; nested `**/SKILL.md` files are deliberately not discovered. The file starts with YAML frontmatter: required `name` and `description`, plus optional `whenToUse`, `metadata`, `disable-model-invocation`, and `user-invocable`.\n40:Catalog entries and loaded skills expose the resolved instruction-file path, so symlinked directories and flat files can open as regular-file previews. Reload locators and resource bases retain the discovered paths, including symlinks.\n42:The catalog and the body have separate lifecycles: discovery parses frontmatter into the catalog entry, and every load re-reads the current file, so editing a skill body needs no versioning or cache invalidation.\n46:Default roots are scanned in this provider's rank order:\n50:| 100 | `project-dsh` | `<projectRoot>/.dsh/skills` |\n51:| 200 | `project-agents` | `<projectRoot>/.agents/skills` |\n53:| 400 | `user-dsh` | `<dshHome>/skills` |\n54:| 500 | `user-agents` | `<agentsHome>/skills` |\n60:Load the plugin alongside the skill registry; it requires `ctx.skills`.\n69:| `providerName` | `filesystem` | Unique provider name registered on `ctx.skills` |\n71:| `dshHome` | `$DSH_HOME` or `~/.dsh` | Harness config root; its `skills` subdirectory is scanned |\n72:| `agentsHome` | `$DSH_AGENTS_HOME` or `~/.agents` | Shared agent config root scanned for compatible skills |\n75:| `bundledSkillDir` | — | Bundled skill root scanned at rank 600 when configured |\n81:Existing roots are watched, so adding, renaming, or deleting a skill (or editing its frontmatter) triggers a catalog refresh for the next model step; edits below `references`, `scripts`, `assets`, and other bundle resources do not. The first-party `write` and `edit` tools invalidate the provider directly when their target could affect a watched skill, so the model observes its own filesystem mutation without waiting for the host watcher. External IDE, Git, and shell changes are picked up by the host watcher, and a root that does not exist yet is probed until it appears.\n85:A valid skill under any scanned root appears in the session catalog sorted by name, and loading it returns the current file body. A file without valid frontmatter, an invalid name, or an invalid invocation value is skipped with a warning, so the model catalog receives no per-skill diagnostic and cannot distinguish an absent skill from an invalid one. A root whose discovery or file read fails is logged with its path and omitted; other roots remain available. The observation is incomplete and is retried on the next lookup rather than cached. The model catalog identifies incomplete discovery separately from confirmed absence.\n99:The provider is built on two separations. First, catalog versus body: discovery parses frontmatter into summaries, while every load re-reads the file, so body edits need no hash, revision, or cache invalidation. Second, discovery versus watching: `list()` scans roots and resolves the project root through `ctx.fs` when a filesystem service is present (falling back to abortable Node I/O), while a separate watch manager owns Chokidar handles, missing-root probes, and invalidation.\n105:| [`src/index.ts`](src/index.ts) | Plugin entry, provider, root resolution, frontmatter parsing, watch manager |\n109:Discovery resolves the root list for the lookup cwd, asks the watch manager to attach to each root, then scans each root's direct entries: directory bundles resolve `<name>/SKILL.md`, flat files resolve `<name>.md`. Each file is parsed for frontmatter — `name` must be kebab-case, `description` is required, and the invocation keys resolve through the strict boolean grammar — and candidates carry the root's source label and rank so the registry can merge them with other providers. Confirmed missing paths are valid empty state; malformed or non-text entries warn and skip.\n113:Existing roots are watched by Chokidar at depth 1; a root that does not exist is followed from its nearest existing ancestor one missing segment at a time using `fs.watchFile`. Relevant events — direct bundle add/remove, flat `.md` add/remove, and direct `SKILL.md` add/remove/change — coalesce into one provider invalidation per microtask batch, while resource-subtree changes are ignored. The watch manager is bounded by `watchMaxProjects`, logs and retries failed startup, and closes every handle at teardown. First-party `write`/`edit` mutations invalidate synchronously through the `fs/observed` event.\n122:Read these pages when the package-level contract is not enough. They move from the registry contract to the consumer that renders discovered skills and the home-path resolution used by the config defaults.\n124:- [Skill subsystem reference](../../../docs/subsystems/skills.md) — the registry contract and the local discovery priority table.\n126:- [tool-skill package](../tool-skill/README.md) — how discovered skills reach the session catalog and the model.\n134:Indirectly, through `dsh-tool-skill`, which renders this provider's invocable names and capped descriptions into the initial or replacement catalog and a selected current instruction body plus resource-base guidance into retained tool history while paths, provider ranks, and disabled skills remain hidden.\n142:<a id=\"known-limitations-and-deferred-work\"></a>\n145:These limits define when the provider is a poor fit or needs special operational care. They are current package constraints, not a task backlog.\n147:- **Discovery is one level deep** — only `<root>/<name>/SKILL.md` and `<root>/<name>.md` are recognized; nested skill trees and package manifests are ignored.\n160:This Dev Note is working context for maintainers and is explicitly non-authoritative — shipped behavior and limits live in the sections above and in the code. A TODO in `src/index.ts` proposes extracting the Chokidar and missing-root observation into a Cordis file-watch service, keeping skill filtering and invalidation here; the missing-root polling tradeoff documented above is part of that open design.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\nls packages/skill; echo \\\"===FS README===\\\"; grep -n \\\"skills\\\\|SKILL.md\\\\|frontmatter\\\\|scan\\\\|quarantine\\\\|limit\\\\|directory\\\" packages/skill/skill-filesystem/README.md | head -40\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916141878,"end":1793916142651}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1793916143394,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbc0722001d0lokYjeA2jqhX","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbbfb81001kbTcexxPaNQQy5","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"d44b3126a13523d1caf0575b620b7c3ca6e9fe63","cost":0.000681138,"tokens":{"input":451,"output":275,"reasoning":47,"cache":{"read":140096,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793916144855,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbc0cd7001UjOYa4MdLqkFtq","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbc0727001zk0HSIHOS2oq18","type":"step-start","snapshot":"d44b3126a13523d1caf0575b620b7c3ca6e9fe63"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916150726,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbc2091001Gf426RSW7RBiJc","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbc0727001zk0HSIHOS2oq18","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a7f85a3711e4c02a","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\nsed -n 44,60p packages/skill/skill-filesystem/README.md; echo \"===PROJECT ROOT===\"; grep -n \"projectRoot\\|project root\" packages/skill/skill-filesystem/README.md | head -12; echo \"===NAME RULE===\"; grep -n \"kebab\\|name must\\|invalid name\" packages/skill/skill-filesystem/README.md | head -8"},"output":"### Roots and priority\n\nDefault roots are scanned in this provider's rank order:\n\n| Rank | Source | Path |\n|---|---|---|\n| 100 | `project-dsh` | `<projectRoot>/.dsh/skills` |\n| 200 | `project-agents` | `<projectRoot>/.agents/skills` |\n| 300 | `custom` | `Config.customSkillDirs` |\n| 400 | `user-dsh` | `<dshHome>/skills` |\n| 500 | `user-agents` | `<agentsHome>/skills` |\n\nThe project root is the nearest ancestor containing `.git`; without one, the current cwd is used. The user DSH root skips its `.system` child. `includeDefaultRoots: false` omits the project and user rows plus the `$DSH_BUNDLED_SKILL_DIR` default so an isolated provider sees only its own configured roots; `bundledSkillDir` adds a bundled root at rank 600.\n\n### Mount and configure\n\nLoad the plugin alongside the skill registry; it requires `ctx.skills`.\n===PROJECT ROOT===\n50:| 100 | `project-dsh` | `<projectRoot>/.dsh/skills` |\n51:| 200 | `project-agents` | `<projectRoot>/.agents/skills` |\n56:The project root is the nearest ancestor containing `.git`; without one, the current cwd is used. The user DSH root skips its `.system` child. `includeDefaultRoots: false` omits the project and user rows plus the `$DSH_BUNDLED_SKILL_DIR` default so an isolated provider sees only its own configured roots; `bundledSkillDir` adds a bundled root at rank 600.\n73:| `customSkillDirs` | `[]` | Additional local skill roots, after project roots and before user roots |\n99:The provider is built on two separations. First, catalog versus body: discovery parses frontmatter into summaries, while every load re-reads the file, so body edits need no hash, revision, or cache invalidation. Second, discovery versus watching: `list()` scans roots and resolves the project root through `ctx.fs` when a filesystem service is present (falling back to abortable Node I/O), while a separate watch manager owns Chokidar handles, missing-root probes, and invalidation.\n===NAME RULE===\n85:A valid skill under any scanned root appears in the session catalog sorted by name, and loading it returns the current file body. A file without valid frontmatter, an invalid name, or an invalid invocation value is skipped with a warning, so the model catalog receives no per-skill diagnostic and cannot distinguish an absent skill from an invalid one. A root whose discovery or file read fails is logged with its path and omitted; other roots remain available. The observation is incomplete and is retried on the next lookup rather than cached. The model catalog identifies incomplete discovery separately from confirmed absence.\n109:Discovery resolves the root list for the lookup cwd, asks the watch manager to attach to each root, then scans each root's direct entries: directory bundles resolve `<name>/SKILL.md`, flat files resolve `<name>.md`. Each file is parsed for frontmatter — `name` must be kebab-case, `description` is required, and the invocation keys resolve through the strict boolean grammar — and candidates carry the root's source label and rank so the registry can merge them with other providers. Confirmed missing paths are valid empty state; malformed or non-text entries warn and skip.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"### Roots and priority\n\nDefault roots are scanned in this provider's rank order:\n\n| Rank | Source | Path |\n|---|---|---|\n| 100 | `project-dsh` | `<projectRoot>/.dsh/skills` |\n| 200 | `project-agents` | `<projectRoot>/.agents/skills` |\n| 300 | `custom` | `Config.customSkillDirs` |\n| 400 | `user-dsh` | `<dshHome>/skills` |\n| 500 | `user-agents` | `<agentsHome>/skills` |\n\nThe project root is the nearest ancestor containing `.git`; without one, the current cwd is used. The user DSH root skips its `.system` child. `includeDefaultRoots: false` omits the project and user rows plus the `$DSH_BUNDLED_SKILL_DIR` default so an isolated provider sees only its own configured roots; `bundledSkillDir` adds a bundled root at rank 600.\n\n### Mount and configure\n\nLoad the plugin alongside the skill registry; it requires `ctx.skills`.\n===PROJECT ROOT===\n50:| 100 | `project-dsh` | `<projectRoot>/.dsh/skills` |\n51:| 200 | `project-agents` | `<projectRoot>/.agents/skills` |\n56:The project root is the nearest ancestor containing `.git`; without one, the current cwd is used. The user DSH root skips its `.system` child. `includeDefaultRoots: false` omits the project and user rows plus the `$DSH_BUNDLED_SKILL_DIR` default so an isolated provider sees only its own configured roots; `bundledSkillDir` adds a bundled root at rank 600.\n73:| `customSkillDirs` | `[]` | Additional local skill roots, after project roots and before user roots |\n99:The provider is built on two separations. First, catalog versus body: discovery parses frontmatter into summaries, while every load re-reads the file, so body edits need no hash, revision, or cache invalidation. Second, discovery versus watching: `list()` scans roots and resolves the project root through `ctx.fs` when a filesystem service is present (falling back to abortable Node I/O), while a separate watch manager owns Chokidar handles, missing-root probes, and invalidation.\n===NAME RULE===\n85:A valid skill under any scanned root appears in the session catalog sorted by name, and loading it returns the current file body. A file without valid frontmatter, an invalid name, or an invalid invocation value is skipped with a warning, so the model catalog receives no per-skill diagnostic and cannot distinguish an absent skill from an invalid one. A root whose discovery or file read fails is logged with its path and omitted; other roots remain available. The observation is incomplete and is retried on the next lookup rather than cached. The model catalog identifies incomplete discovery separately from confirmed absence.\n109:Discovery resolves the root list for the lookup cwd, asks the watch manager to attach to each root, then scans each root's direct entries: directory bundles resolve `<name>/SKILL.md`, flat files resolve `<name>.md`. Each file is parsed for frontmatter — `name` must be kebab-case, `description` is required, and the invocation keys resolve through the strict boolean grammar — and candidates carry the root's source label and rank so the registry can merge them with other providers. Confirmed missing paths are valid empty state; malformed or non-text entries warn and skip.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\nsed -n 44,60p packages/skill/skill-filesystem/README.md; echo \\\"===PROJECT ROOT===\\\"; grep -n \\\"projectRoot\\\\|project root\\\" packages/skill/skill-filesystem/README.md | head -12; echo \\\"===NAME RULE===\\\"; grep -n \\\"kebab\\\\|name must\\\\|invalid name\\\" packages/skill/skill-filesystem/README.md | head -8\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916149905,"end":1793916150726}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916151274,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbc2250001zkzWlHtGpRj3TK","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbc0727001zk0HSIHOS2oq18","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-aedc4599b8ba3229","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled\nfind .skilled/skills -maxdepth 1 -type d | wc -l; find .skilled/skills -name SKILL.md | wc -l; echo \"---top-level skill names---\"; for f in .skilled/skills/*/SKILL.md; do sed -n '2,3p' \"$f\" | head -2 | tr '\\n' ' '; echo; done | head -12"},"output":"      16\n      57\n---top-level skill names---\nname: cli-classifier description: \"Routes typed classifier judgment requests to the hosted Jev transport via mode-registry.json; future classifiers join as modes.\" \nname: cli-external-orchestration description: \"Parent hub for external CLI dispatch: routes to seven workflow modes through mode-registry.json; dispatches by workflowMode.\" \nname: cli-orca description: \"Route Orca CLI work: worktrees, terminals, browser, automations, handoffs, artifacts, and the eight official Orca skills.\" \nname: local-obsidian description: \"Read-only Obsidian-plugin design-system and source-convention evidence for the Note Database plugin.\" \nname: local-webflow description: \"sk-code WEBFLOW surface: frontend evidence (CSS/HTML/JavaScript standards, implementation and performance patterns, CDN deployment, browser debugging and verification) plus shared implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine.\" \nname: mcp-code-mode description: \"MCP orchestration via TypeScript execution. Use Code Mode for ALL external MCP tool calls; ~98% context reduction, type-safe.\" \nname: mcp-tooling description: \"Parent hub for eight modes: five workflow modes and three read-only design transports routed by workflowMode.\" \nname: sk-code description: \"Implement, debug and verify code: TypeScript, Python, shell, JSON; quality and review workflow modes with stack surface packets.\" \nname: sk-design description: \"Design parent hub. Routes one design identity to the mode that owns the artifact: a measured Style Reference, a chart or a diagram.\" \nname: sk-doc description: \"Documentation & component authoring hub: skills, agents, commands, READMEs, catalogs, playbooks, changelogs, goals, frontmatter.\" \nname: sk-git description: \"Git: numbered worktrees, conventional commits, PRs, merge/rebase, and finish; single-skill workflow guidance with no spec.\" \nname: sk-prompt description: \"Prompt engineering: turns a request into a structured, checked AI prompt, with a component skeleton and CLEAR screening.\" \n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"      16\n      57\n---top-level skill names---\nname: cli-classifier description: \"Routes typed classifier judgment requests to the hosted Jev transport via mode-registry.json; future classifiers join as modes.\" \nname: cli-external-orchestration description: \"Parent hub for external CLI dispatch: routes to seven workflow modes through mode-registry.json; dispatches by workflowMode.\" \nname: cli-orca description: \"Route Orca CLI work: worktrees, terminals, browser, automations, handoffs, artifacts, and the eight official Orca skills.\" \nname: local-obsidian description: \"Read-only Obsidian-plugin design-system and source-convention evidence for the Note Database plugin.\" \nname: local-webflow description: \"sk-code WEBFLOW surface: frontend evidence (CSS/HTML/JavaScript standards, implementation and performance patterns, CDN deployment, browser debugging and verification) plus shared implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine.\" \nname: mcp-code-mode description: \"MCP orchestration via TypeScript execution. Use Code Mode for ALL external MCP tool calls; ~98% context reduction, type-safe.\" \nname: mcp-tooling description: \"Parent hub for eight modes: five workflow modes and three read-only design transports routed by workflowMode.\" \nname: sk-code description: \"Implement, debug and verify code: TypeScript, Python, shell, JSON; quality and review workflow modes with stack surface packets.\" \nname: sk-design description: \"Design parent hub. Routes one design identity to the mode that owns the artifact: a measured Style Reference, a chart or a diagram.\" \nname: sk-doc description: \"Documentation & component authoring hub: skills, agents, commands, READMEs, catalogs, playbooks, changelogs, goals, frontmatter.\" \nname: sk-git description: \"Git: numbered worktrees, conventional commits, PRs, merge/rebase, and finish; single-skill workflow guidance with no spec.\" \nname: sk-prompt description: \"Prompt engineering: turns a request into a structured, checked AI prompt, with a component skeleton and CLEAR screening.\" \n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled\\nfind .skilled/skills -maxdepth 1 -type d | wc -l; find .skilled/skills -name SKILL.md | wc -l; echo \\\"---top-level skill names---\\\"; for f in .skilled/skills/*/SKILL.md; do sed -n '2,3p' \\\"$f\\\" | head -2 | tr '\\\\n' ' '; echo; done | head -12\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916150352,"end":1793916151274}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1793916152124,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbc293c001CKMd0WlPZ3G4Iz","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbc0727001zk0HSIHOS2oq18","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"d44b3126a13523d1caf0575b620b7c3ca6e9fe63","cost":0.00166449,"tokens":{"input":3427,"output":323,"reasoning":892,"cache":{"read":140480,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793916154072,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbc30d8001e1WPbsbyu9JXBD","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbc2940001Co7iaE63vm7Ic7","type":"step-start","snapshot":"d44b3126a13523d1caf0575b620b7c3ca6e9fe63"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916159113,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbc421a002dCIpPOPkUaL65R","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbc2940001Co7iaE63vm7Ic7","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-bbcf4e871950a654","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\ngrep -n \"cap\\|character\\|catalog\\|limit\\|description\" packages/skill/tool-skill/README.md | head -20; echo \"===FRONTMATTER EXTRA===\"; grep -n \"unknown\\|additional\\|extra\\|whenToUse\\|metadata\" packages/skill/skill-filesystem/README.md | head -12"},"output":"2:description: \"The model-facing skill catalog and loader tool for users and maintainers understanding what agents see, or configuring the session skill catalog.\"\n12:Agents can discover and load skills during a session. When the `skill` tool is visible, model-invocable skills or incomplete discovery produce a durable catalog before the first request. The tool loads full instructions; users can invoke user-invocable skills with `/name`. Entry, visibility, and completeness changes append a replacement catalog. Incomplete discovery publishes confirmed skills with a warning, including when none are confirmed. Configure `catalogDescriptionMaxLength` to cap descriptions.\n20:- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n28:Skill discovery, catalog updates, and explicit skill invocation use the Session's current working directory.\n30:Mount the plugin alongside the skill registry to give agents a session skill catalog and the `skill` loader tool. It requires `ctx.agents`, `ctx.tools`, and `ctx.skills`.\n34:Use it when agents should discover and load skills during a session. Skip it when skill loading is handled by another consumer or not needed at all — without it, providers and the registry still work, but nothing renders a catalog or a tool for the model.\n38:Load the plugin together with the skill registry and at least one provider. The only configuration caps the normalized description length rendered in the catalog.\n48:| `catalogDescriptionMaxLength` | `500` | Maximum normalized description length rendered in the session catalog; minimum 3 |\n50:The generated [configuration catalog](../../../docs/config-catalog.md#deepseek-aidsh-tool-skill) is the exhaustive source for every accepted field.\n54:- **A session catalog.** When the `skill` tool is visible and either model-invocable skills exist or discovery is incomplete, the agent receives a durable user-role message before its first request, listing each confirmed skill's name and a capped description; a non-empty catalog tells the model to load a skill with the tool before acting on it, and never to infer instructions from the summary alone.\n57:- **Live catalog updates.** Later membership, description, visibility, or completeness changes append a replacement catalog; removing every skill appends an empty catalog that retires older names.\n61:Loading a listed skill returns its full instructions; the model sees one canonical shape whether the load came from the tool or from a user's explicit invocation. An invalid name reports `Error: invalid skill name \"<name>\"`, an unknown name reports the skill is unknown or no longer available, and a skill disabled for model invocation reports it is not available for model invocation. The catalog is omitted entirely when no catalog was ever published and either discovery is complete with no model-invocable skills or the `skill` tool is hidden or shadowed; after a catalog has been published, either visibility loss — the `skill` tool hidden or shadowed by a same-name scoped tool — or removal of every skill instead appends an empty catalog that retires older names.\n71:This section explains how the catalog and the invocation boundary are built; the observable behavior is fully covered in [Use this package](#use-this-package) and the Model Experience section below.\n75:The package is built on two ideas. First, the catalog is a durable projection, diffed by a digest over the published entries and completeness rather than the rendered prose, so the `<system-reminder>` framing can never force a republish and consumers never re-parse the `<available_skills>` block. Second, one canonical rendering serves both load paths — the tool result and the user-explicit injection — through `renderSkillContent` shared from `dsh-skill`, so the model sees the same `<skill_content>` shape regardless of who initiated the load.\n81:| [`src/index.ts`](src/index.ts) | Plugin entry: tool registration, catalog and gesture pre-step listeners, rendering and digest |\n85:At each eligible `agent/pre-step`, the plugin snapshots the calling session's skill catalog, applies exact `skill` tool visibility, filters to model-invocable skills, and compares a digest of the entries and completeness against the newest visible `skill-catalog` message in the session log. When the digest changed, it hands the `enter` decision a durable user-role message containing the replacement catalog; an empty replacement explicitly retires earlier names. An incomplete snapshot publishes its readable entries with a discovery warning and durable `incomplete: true` source metadata, including when no entries remain. Completeness participates in the digest, so recovery clears the warning even when the entries stay the same; incomplete observations are retried rather than cached. The visibility check compares against the exact tool definition this plugin registered, so a scoped same-name shadow removes both the schema and its guidance; the plugin works mounted globally or inside one agent's composition.\n89:The `/name` gesture listener scans only claimed user messages: a whitespace-bounded token naming a user-invocable skill in the workspace catalog injects the same `<skill_content>` rendering as a `user`-role instructions context appended after every other injection. Unknown names and user-disabled skills stay ordinary prose. This is the only entry point for `disable-model-invocation` skills, which the catalog and the `skill` tool never expose.\n98:Read these pages when the package-level contract is not enough. They move from the registry vocabulary behind the catalog to the exact tool schema and the design rationale.\n100:- [Skill subsystem reference](../../../docs/subsystems/skills.md) — the registry and provider vocabulary behind the catalog.\n102:- [Generated tool catalog](../../../docs/tool-catalog.md#deepseek-aidsh-tool-skill) — the exact `skill` schema the model receives.\n===FRONTMATTER EXTRA===\n36:A skill is either a directory bundle `<name>/SKILL.md` or a flat file `<name>.md` at the top level of a scanned root; nested `**/SKILL.md` files are deliberately not discovered. The file starts with YAML frontmatter: required `name` and `description`, plus optional `whenToUse`, `metadata`, `disable-model-invocation`, and `user-invocable`.\n160:This Dev Note is working context for maintainers and is explicitly non-authoritative — shipped behavior and limits live in the sections above and in the code. A TODO in `src/index.ts` proposes extracting the Chokidar and missing-root observation into a Cordis file-watch service, keeping skill filtering and invalidation here; the missing-root polling tradeoff documented above is part of that open design.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2:description: \"The model-facing skill catalog and loader tool for users and maintainers understanding what agents see, or configuring the session skill catalog.\"\n12:Agents can discover and load skills during a session. When the `skill` tool is visible, model-invocable skills or incomplete discovery produce a durable catalog before the first request. The tool loads full instructions; users can invoke user-invocable skills with `/name`. Entry, visibility, and completeness changes append a replacement catalog. Incomplete discovery publishes confirmed skills with a warning, including when none are confirmed. Configure `catalogDescriptionMaxLength` to cap descriptions.\n20:- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n28:Skill discovery, catalog updates, and explicit skill invocation use the Session's current working directory.\n30:Mount the plugin alongside the skill registry to give agents a session skill catalog and the `skill` loader tool. It requires `ctx.agents`, `ctx.tools`, and `ctx.skills`.\n34:Use it when agents should discover and load skills during a session. Skip it when skill loading is handled by another consumer or not needed at all — without it, providers and the registry still work, but nothing renders a catalog or a tool for the model.\n38:Load the plugin together with the skill registry and at least one provider. The only configuration caps the normalized description length rendered in the catalog.\n48:| `catalogDescriptionMaxLength` | `500` | Maximum normalized description length rendered in the session catalog; minimum 3 |\n50:The generated [configuration catalog](../../../docs/config-catalog.md#deepseek-aidsh-tool-skill) is the exhaustive source for every accepted field.\n54:- **A session catalog.** When the `skill` tool is visible and either model-invocable skills exist or discovery is incomplete, the agent receives a durable user-role message before its first request, listing each confirmed skill's name and a capped description; a non-empty catalog tells the model to load a skill with the tool before acting on it, and never to infer instructions from the summary alone.\n57:- **Live catalog updates.** Later membership, description, visibility, or completeness changes append a replacement catalog; removing every skill appends an empty catalog that retires older names.\n61:Loading a listed skill returns its full instructions; the model sees one canonical shape whether the load came from the tool or from a user's explicit invocation. An invalid name reports `Error: invalid skill name \"<name>\"`, an unknown name reports the skill is unknown or no longer available, and a skill disabled for model invocation reports it is not available for model invocation. The catalog is omitted entirely when no catalog was ever published and either discovery is complete with no model-invocable skills or the `skill` tool is hidden or shadowed; after a catalog has been published, either visibility loss — the `skill` tool hidden or shadowed by a same-name scoped tool — or removal of every skill instead appends an empty catalog that retires older names.\n71:This section explains how the catalog and the invocation boundary are built; the observable behavior is fully covered in [Use this package](#use-this-package) and the Model Experience section below.\n75:The package is built on two ideas. First, the catalog is a durable projection, diffed by a digest over the published entries and completeness rather than the rendered prose, so the `<system-reminder>` framing can never force a republish and consumers never re-parse the `<available_skills>` block. Second, one canonical rendering serves both load paths — the tool result and the user-explicit injection — through `renderSkillContent` shared from `dsh-skill`, so the model sees the same `<skill_content>` shape regardless of who initiated the load.\n81:| [`src/index.ts`](src/index.ts) | Plugin entry: tool registration, catalog and gesture pre-step listeners, rendering and digest |\n85:At each eligible `agent/pre-step`, the plugin snapshots the calling session's skill catalog, applies exact `skill` tool visibility, filters to model-invocable skills, and compares a digest of the entries and completeness against the newest visible `skill-catalog` message in the session log. When the digest changed, it hands the `enter` decision a durable user-role message containing the replacement catalog; an empty replacement explicitly retires earlier names. An incomplete snapshot publishes its readable entries with a discovery warning and durable `incomplete: true` source metadata, including when no entries remain. Completeness participates in the digest, so recovery clears the warning even when the entries stay the same; incomplete observations are retried rather than cached. The visibility check compares against the exact tool definition this plugin registered, so a scoped same-name shadow removes both the schema and its guidance; the plugin works mounted globally or inside one agent's composition.\n89:The `/name` gesture listener scans only claimed user messages: a whitespace-bounded token naming a user-invocable skill in the workspace catalog injects the same `<skill_content>` rendering as a `user`-role instructions context appended after every other injection. Unknown names and user-disabled skills stay ordinary prose. This is the only entry point for `disable-model-invocation` skills, which the catalog and the `skill` tool never expose.\n98:Read these pages when the package-level contract is not enough. They move from the registry vocabulary behind the catalog to the exact tool schema and the design rationale.\n100:- [Skill subsystem reference](../../../docs/subsystems/skills.md) — the registry and provider vocabulary behind the catalog.\n102:- [Generated tool catalog](../../../docs/tool-catalog.md#deepseek-aidsh-tool-skill) — the exact `skill` schema the model receives.\n===FRONTMATTER EXTRA===\n36:A skill is either a directory bundle `<name>/SKILL.md` or a flat file `<name>.md` at the top level of a scanned root; nested `**/SKILL.md` files are deliberately not discovered. The file starts with YAML frontmatter: required `name` and `description`, plus optional `whenToUse`, `metadata`, `disable-model-invocation`, and `user-invocable`.\n160:This Dev Note is working context for maintainers and is explicitly non-authoritative — shipped behavior and limits live in the sections above and in the code. A TODO in `src/index.ts` proposes extracting the Chokidar and missing-root observation into a Cordis file-watch service, keeping skill filtering and invalidation here; the missing-root polling tradeoff documented above is part of that open design.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\ngrep -n \\\"cap\\\\|character\\\\|catalog\\\\|limit\\\\|description\\\" packages/skill/tool-skill/README.md | head -20; echo \\\"===FRONTMATTER EXTRA===\\\"; grep -n \\\"unknown\\\\|additional\\\\|extra\\\\|whenToUse\\\\|metadata\\\" packages/skill/skill-filesystem/README.md | head -12\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916158490,"end":1793916159113}}}}
