{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916204185,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbcf3380018H5CkjBQuzvTSn","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbceb3b001ulMDZzHQUUenNp","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc354a24ec4f325a","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\ngrep -rn \"subagent\\|agents\" docs/tool-catalog.md | head -20; echo \"===PRESET===\"; grep -n \"preset\\|agent\" docs/config-catalog.md | grep -i preset | head -15"},"output":"docs/tool-catalog.md:10:Scope: shipped product tools under `packages/*/tool-*`, each booted with its DEFAULT config, except where a Config field is REQUIRED with no default — there the generator must choose, and the per-package note records which branch this page shows. The registered tool NAME can be a load-time config (e.g. `tool-subagent`'s `toolName`), so a deployment may expose a package under a different or additional name — a per-package note records those shipped aliases where they exist. The `examples/` demo tools (e.g. `echo`) are excluded, matching the cordis catalog's packages-only scope.\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:21:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-experimental-browser-use-stagehand-native` | `stagehand_act`, `stagehand_extract`, `stagehand_navigate`, `stagehand_observe`, `stagehand_screenshot`, `stagehand_tabs` | `ctx.browserUse`, `ctx.agents`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.systemPrompt` | `tool/call`, `tool/result` | - | - |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:22:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-action-cards` | `propose_action` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.agents`, `ctx.sessionProjections`, `ctx.remoteAccess` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `plugin:action-cards/decision`, `user/message` | - | Declared only to an Agent that loaded the skill of a registered card kind; the kind packages register their actions, and the Personalized recommendations bundle supplies the shipped kinds. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:25:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-plan-mode` | `exit_plan_mode` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `ctx.agents`, `ctx.userQuestions (execution time, opportunistic)` | `tool/call`, `plan/mode inactive on an approved review`, `tool/result` | - | exit_plan_mode stays in the model-facing schema while planning is inactive so transitions add no tool-catalog churn on top of the plan-policy change. Its execute path rejects calls outside plan mode; in plan mode it presents the plan over the user-questions seam (approve / keep planning with feedback), and approval logs plan mode inactive at the step boundary. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:27:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-present` | `present` | `ctx.workingDirectory`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.fs`, `ctx.agents` | `tool/call`, `deliverables/presented after a successful final result`, `tool/result` | - | Deliveries belong to the calling Session; Web ui-deliverables supplies source-file opening and cards. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:36:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-goal` | `create_goal`, `get_goal`, `update_goal` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.agents`, `ctx.goals`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `a calling Agent in an authorized open turn` | `tool/call`, `goal/change for mutations`, `tool/result` | - | create, edit, pause, and resume require direct-human root authority; complete and blocked also accept the exact current goal round. The default blocked lower bound is three admitted rounds. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:37:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-schedule` | `schedule_create`, `schedule_edit`, `schedule_list`, `schedule_view` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.schedule`, `ctx.systemPrompt` | `tool/call`, `Schedule storage domain create, update, or delete`, `tool/result` | - | A preset or Agent scope mounts this package and decides which agents receive its tools. Management reads and writes Host tasks by id across Sessions. Tasks store an RFC 5545 recurrence and an existing, first-created-and-reused, or per-run-new Session target. Delegated subagents cannot call these tools. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:39:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-ralph` | `ralph` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.workflowEngine`, `ctx.subagents`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `a calling Agent (exec.agent parents every fresh round)` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `workflow and child session events during execution` | - | A fixed foreground workflow starts one fresh structured child per round; the model selects only the immutable objective and an optional round cap. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:40:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-skill` | `skill` | `ctx.workingDirectory`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.agents`, `ctx.skills` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `user/message replacement catalogs via agent.inject()` | - | - |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:41:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-session-query` | `session_event_read`, `session_event_search`, `session_event_trace`, `session_search`, `session_trace` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `ctx.sessionQuery`, `ctx.agents`, `a calling Agent for workspace authority` | `tool/call`, `tool/result` | - | The five read-only tools hide provider cursors and authorize every result from the immutable calling agent session. The package is opt-in; compositions that need enforced deadlines or bounded inline output also mount the generic timeout or spill policies. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:42:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-experimental-tool-subagent-oar` | `subagent_oar` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.subagents` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `parent subagent catalog and completion notices` | - | The subagent_oar tool selects among configured aliases for external providers. This catalog shows Codex and Claude Code aliases; each deployment chooses its providers. Native model and permission settings belong to the selected provider configuration. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:43:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-subagent` | `list_subagent_models`, `subagent` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.subagents`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `ctx.sessionProjections`, `ctx.llm for model discovery and selected-route validation` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `child session events through the chosen provider` | `subagent`, `subagent_fork` | The load-time `toolName` selects the delegation name (default `subagent`); `modelSelection` adds optional route fields. `modelDiscovery` defaults off and registers shared discovery on one instance per scope. Web and Desktop presets enable spawn discovery and route fields and leave `subagent_fork` route fields disabled. The Host settings service records authorization for each new top-level Session; child Sessions inherit it. Without a recorded policy, discovery and explicit selection permit only the current parent route. Every delegation returns the child's name promptly; local children accept follow-ups, while external backends report their single execution automatically. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:44:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-subagent-control` | `interrupt_agent`, `list_agents`, `send_message`, `wait_agent` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.subagents`, `ctx.agents` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `child session events through ctx.subagents` | - | The shared control tools discover and contact agents in the subagent tree, interrupt owned descendants, and wait for input in the caller’s inbox. Provider-bound `tool-subagent` instances supply delegation tools. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:46:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-agent-team` | `team_task_create`, `team_task_get`, `team_task_list`, `team_task_update` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `ctx.agentTeams`, `ctx.subagents`, `an exact live Team member Agent` | `tool/call`, `team/task`, `tool/result` | - | Four task-board tools share the subagent tree’s root Session. Enabling Team mode on the root Session adds task-board guidance alongside standard subagent delegation and control tools. Standard subagent controls provide required identity independently of Team mode. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:1650:A preset or Agent scope mounts this package and decides which agents receive its tools. Management reads and writes Host tasks by id across Sessions. Tasks store an RFC 5545 recurrence and an existing, first-created-and-reused, or per-run-new Session target. Delegated subagents cannot call these tools.\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:1991:<a id=\"deepseek-aidsh-experimental-tool-subagent-oar\"></a>\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:1993:## `@deepseek-ai/dsh-experimental-tool-subagent-oar`\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:1995:### `subagent_oar`\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:2017:      \"description\": \"Optional short name for the subagent, unique among the subagents you create: lower-case letters, digits, and hyphens, starting with a letter, at most 32 characters; lead is reserved for the root agent. Omitted names default to agent-1, agent-2, and so on.\"\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:2036:Source: [`packages/experimental/tool-subagent-oar/src/index.ts`](../packages/experimental/tool-subagent-oar/src/index.ts)\n===PRESET===\n139:<!-- BEGIN GENERATED config-catalog:@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset -->\n140:<a id=\"deepseek-aidsh-agent-preset\"></a>\n142:## `@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset`\n144:- `inject`: `agentPresets`\n145:- `refs`: [`PresetDefinition`](../packages/preset/agent-preset-registry/src/index.ts)\n146:- `source`: [`packages/preset/agent-preset/src/index.ts:9`](../packages/preset/agent-preset/src/index.ts)\n149:/** Definition submitted to the preset registry. */\n152:<!-- END GENERATED config-catalog:@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset -->\n154:<!-- BEGIN GENERATED config-catalog:@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset-registry -->\n155:<a id=\"deepseek-aidsh-agent-preset-registry\"></a>\n157:## `@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset-registry`\n161:- `source`: [`packages/preset/agent-preset-registry/src/preset.ts:13`](../packages/preset/agent-preset-registry/src/preset.ts)\n166:  /** Deployment default when the caller omits a preset. */\n172:<!-- END GENERATED config-catalog:@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset-registry -->\n210:   * Required rather than defaulted: the deployment default is what a preset\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"docs/tool-catalog.md:10:Scope: shipped product tools under `packages/*/tool-*`, each booted with its DEFAULT config, except where a Config field is REQUIRED with no default — there the generator must choose, and the per-package note records which branch this page shows. The registered tool NAME can be a load-time config (e.g. `tool-subagent`'s `toolName`), so a deployment may expose a package under a different or additional name — a per-package note records those shipped aliases where they exist. The `examples/` demo tools (e.g. `echo`) are excluded, matching the cordis catalog's packages-only scope.\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:21:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-experimental-browser-use-stagehand-native` | `stagehand_act`, `stagehand_extract`, `stagehand_navigate`, `stagehand_observe`, `stagehand_screenshot`, `stagehand_tabs` | `ctx.browserUse`, `ctx.agents`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.systemPrompt` | `tool/call`, `tool/result` | - | - |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:22:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-action-cards` | `propose_action` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.agents`, `ctx.sessionProjections`, `ctx.remoteAccess` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `plugin:action-cards/decision`, `user/message` | - | Declared only to an Agent that loaded the skill of a registered card kind; the kind packages register their actions, and the Personalized recommendations bundle supplies the shipped kinds. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:25:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-plan-mode` | `exit_plan_mode` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `ctx.agents`, `ctx.userQuestions (execution time, opportunistic)` | `tool/call`, `plan/mode inactive on an approved review`, `tool/result` | - | exit_plan_mode stays in the model-facing schema while planning is inactive so transitions add no tool-catalog churn on top of the plan-policy change. Its execute path rejects calls outside plan mode; in plan mode it presents the plan over the user-questions seam (approve / keep planning with feedback), and approval logs plan mode inactive at the step boundary. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:27:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-present` | `present` | `ctx.workingDirectory`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.fs`, `ctx.agents` | `tool/call`, `deliverables/presented after a successful final result`, `tool/result` | - | Deliveries belong to the calling Session; Web ui-deliverables supplies source-file opening and cards. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:36:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-goal` | `create_goal`, `get_goal`, `update_goal` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.agents`, `ctx.goals`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `a calling Agent in an authorized open turn` | `tool/call`, `goal/change for mutations`, `tool/result` | - | create, edit, pause, and resume require direct-human root authority; complete and blocked also accept the exact current goal round. The default blocked lower bound is three admitted rounds. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:37:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-schedule` | `schedule_create`, `schedule_edit`, `schedule_list`, `schedule_view` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.schedule`, `ctx.systemPrompt` | `tool/call`, `Schedule storage domain create, update, or delete`, `tool/result` | - | A preset or Agent scope mounts this package and decides which agents receive its tools. Management reads and writes Host tasks by id across Sessions. Tasks store an RFC 5545 recurrence and an existing, first-created-and-reused, or per-run-new Session target. Delegated subagents cannot call these tools. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:39:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-ralph` | `ralph` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.workflowEngine`, `ctx.subagents`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `a calling Agent (exec.agent parents every fresh round)` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `workflow and child session events during execution` | - | A fixed foreground workflow starts one fresh structured child per round; the model selects only the immutable objective and an optional round cap. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:40:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-skill` | `skill` | `ctx.workingDirectory`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.agents`, `ctx.skills` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `user/message replacement catalogs via agent.inject()` | - | - |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:41:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-session-query` | `session_event_read`, `session_event_search`, `session_event_trace`, `session_search`, `session_trace` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `ctx.sessionQuery`, `ctx.agents`, `a calling Agent for workspace authority` | `tool/call`, `tool/result` | - | The five read-only tools hide provider cursors and authorize every result from the immutable calling agent session. The package is opt-in; compositions that need enforced deadlines or bounded inline output also mount the generic timeout or spill policies. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:42:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-experimental-tool-subagent-oar` | `subagent_oar` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.subagents` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `parent subagent catalog and completion notices` | - | The subagent_oar tool selects among configured aliases for external providers. This catalog shows Codex and Claude Code aliases; each deployment chooses its providers. Native model and permission settings belong to the selected provider configuration. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:43:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-subagent` | `list_subagent_models`, `subagent` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.subagents`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `ctx.sessionProjections`, `ctx.llm for model discovery and selected-route validation` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `child session events through the chosen provider` | `subagent`, `subagent_fork` | The load-time `toolName` selects the delegation name (default `subagent`); `modelSelection` adds optional route fields. `modelDiscovery` defaults off and registers shared discovery on one instance per scope. Web and Desktop presets enable spawn discovery and route fields and leave `subagent_fork` route fields disabled. The Host settings service records authorization for each new top-level Session; child Sessions inherit it. Without a recorded policy, discovery and explicit selection permit only the current parent route. Every delegation returns the child's name promptly; local children accept follow-ups, while external backends report their single execution automatically. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:44:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-subagent-control` | `interrupt_agent`, `list_agents`, `send_message`, `wait_agent` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.subagents`, `ctx.agents` | `tool/call`, `tool/result`, `child session events through ctx.subagents` | - | The shared control tools discover and contact agents in the subagent tree, interrupt owned descendants, and wait for input in the caller’s inbox. Provider-bound `tool-subagent` instances supply delegation tools. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:46:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-agent-team` | `team_task_create`, `team_task_get`, `team_task_list`, `team_task_update` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `ctx.agentTeams`, `ctx.subagents`, `an exact live Team member Agent` | `tool/call`, `team/task`, `tool/result` | - | Four task-board tools share the subagent tree’s root Session. Enabling Team mode on the root Session adds task-board guidance alongside standard subagent delegation and control tools. Standard subagent controls provide required identity independently of Team mode. |\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:1650:A preset or Agent scope mounts this package and decides which agents receive its tools. Management reads and writes Host tasks by id across Sessions. Tasks store an RFC 5545 recurrence and an existing, first-created-and-reused, or per-run-new Session target. Delegated subagents cannot call these tools.\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:1991:<a id=\"deepseek-aidsh-experimental-tool-subagent-oar\"></a>\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:1993:## `@deepseek-ai/dsh-experimental-tool-subagent-oar`\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:1995:### `subagent_oar`\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:2017:      \"description\": \"Optional short name for the subagent, unique among the subagents you create: lower-case letters, digits, and hyphens, starting with a letter, at most 32 characters; lead is reserved for the root agent. Omitted names default to agent-1, agent-2, and so on.\"\ndocs/tool-catalog.md:2036:Source: [`packages/experimental/tool-subagent-oar/src/index.ts`](../packages/experimental/tool-subagent-oar/src/index.ts)\n===PRESET===\n139:<!-- BEGIN GENERATED config-catalog:@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset -->\n140:<a id=\"deepseek-aidsh-agent-preset\"></a>\n142:## `@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset`\n144:- `inject`: `agentPresets`\n145:- `refs`: [`PresetDefinition`](../packages/preset/agent-preset-registry/src/index.ts)\n146:- `source`: [`packages/preset/agent-preset/src/index.ts:9`](../packages/preset/agent-preset/src/index.ts)\n149:/** Definition submitted to the preset registry. */\n152:<!-- END GENERATED config-catalog:@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset -->\n154:<!-- BEGIN GENERATED config-catalog:@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset-registry -->\n155:<a id=\"deepseek-aidsh-agent-preset-registry\"></a>\n157:## `@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset-registry`\n161:- `source`: [`packages/preset/agent-preset-registry/src/preset.ts:13`](../packages/preset/agent-preset-registry/src/preset.ts)\n166:  /** Deployment default when the caller omits a preset. */\n172:<!-- END GENERATED config-catalog:@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset-registry -->\n210:   * Required rather than defaulted: the deployment default is what a preset\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\ngrep -rn \\\"subagent\\\\|agents\\\" docs/tool-catalog.md | head -20; echo \\\"===PRESET===\\\"; grep -n \\\"preset\\\\|agent\\\" docs/config-catalog.md | grep -i preset | head -15\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916203832,"end":1793916204185}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916204209,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbcf1da001vYIHUHz2X5tpB2","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbceb3b001ulMDZzHQUUenNp","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b23619c220172f17","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\nfor p in subagent team preset workflow goal plan; do echo \"== $p\"; ls packages/$p; head -20 packages/$p/*/README.md 2>/dev/null | sed -n 1,14p; done"},"output":"== subagent\nREADME.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\nsubagent\nsubagent-acp\nsubagent-claude-code\nsubagent-codex\nsubagent-dsh-sdk\nsubagent-fork-in-process\nsubagent-spawn-in-process\ntool-subagent\ntool-subagent-control\n==> packages/subagent/subagent-acp/README.md <==\n---\ndescription: \"The out-of-process ACP subagent backend for users and maintainers choosing a delegation provider, configuring a child ACP agent command, or debugging remote child runs.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-subagent-acp\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nUse this package to delegate a task to an ACP-compatible agent running in a fresh subprocess with its own runtime, session, model, and tools. Each run shares only the selected working directory, sends the task over ACP, and returns the child's final answer or a safe error; intermediate messages and tool traffic stay outside the parent conversation. Permission prompts are answered by configured policy without human interaction. Choose it when delegation needs process isolation or a non-Harness ACP agent, and choose an in-process backend when the child must share parent capabilities.\n\n== team\nagent-team\nREADME.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\ntool-agent-team\n==> packages/team/agent-team/README.md <==\n---\ndescription: \"Coordinate subagents through a durable shared task board in Team-enabled Sessions.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-team\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-agent-team` adds a durable shared task board to an existing subagent tree. Members use standard subagent tools to discover and contact one another. Team adds compare-and-set task ownership. Mount `dsh-tool-agent-team` for task tools. Members share one checkout.\n\n== preset\nagent-preset\nagent-preset-registry\npersona\nREADME.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\n==> packages/preset/agent-preset-registry/README.md <==\n---\ndescription: \"Choose an Agent’s tools, prompt sections and skills through declarative presets. One process can run several compositions. Failed definitions remain visible, while existing Agents retain the composition they already use.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset-registry\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nChoose an Agent’s tools, prompt sections and skills through declarative presets. One process can run several compositions. Failed definitions remain visible, while existing Agents retain the composition they already use.\n\n== workflow\nREADME.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\ntool-workflow\nworkflow\nworkflow-ptc\n==> packages/workflow/tool-workflow/README.md <==\n---\ndescription: \"The model-facing workflow tool: run a JavaScript orchestration script that fans out subagents, for users and maintainers choosing or configuring model-driven orchestration.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-workflow\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-tool-workflow` lets a model run JavaScript orchestration that delegates to many subagents and returns a final JSON value. Use it only when the user explicitly requests a workflow or large multi-agent orchestration; prefer plain subagent calls for one or two delegations. Foreground execution waits for all work; cancellation or abnormal completion returns an error rather than partial success. `run_in_background: true` returns an owned job id immediately and exposes live output. Deployments can rename the tool with `toolName` and cap rendered results with `maxResultChars`.\n\n== goal\ncommand-goal\ngoal\ngoal-round-driver\nREADME.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\ntool-goal\n==> packages/goal/command-goal/README.md <==\n---\ndescription: \"The human-facing /goal slash command for users and maintainers choosing, composing, or debugging goal control in UI command planes.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-command-goal\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-command-goal` gives users the `/goal` command to create, edit, pause, resume, clear, and inspect the current goal directly in an interactive UI. Commands and their direct output stay in the UI and do not enter model requests. Accepted changes persist, and ordered image or file attachments on a create or edit become one ordinary user message that later goal rounds can read. Use this package in interactive deployments with a command adapter; headless and automation apps without one do not need it.\n\n== plan\nplan-mode\nREADME.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\n---\ndescription: \"Plan mode for users and maintainers choosing, configuring, or debugging the per-agent planning feature with deployment guidance, a /plan command, and a user-reviewed exit.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-plan-mode\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nPlan mode asks an agent to explore and design before execution, then presents the finished plan for your approval. Enter it with `/plan`, optionally with a message or ordered image and file attachments; leave with `/plan off`, approve the review to continue, or return feedback for more planning. Deployment-defined guidance controls planning behavior, but every tool remains available, so use sandbox mode and approval prompts for enforced limits. The active state survives session resume and forks. Choose it when you want a reviewed plan before the agent acts.\n\n## Table of Contents\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"== subagent\nREADME.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\nsubagent\nsubagent-acp\nsubagent-claude-code\nsubagent-codex\nsubagent-dsh-sdk\nsubagent-fork-in-process\nsubagent-spawn-in-process\ntool-subagent\ntool-subagent-control\n==> packages/subagent/subagent-acp/README.md <==\n---\ndescription: \"The out-of-process ACP subagent backend for users and maintainers choosing a delegation provider, configuring a child ACP agent command, or debugging remote child runs.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-subagent-acp\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nUse this package to delegate a task to an ACP-compatible agent running in a fresh subprocess with its own runtime, session, model, and tools. Each run shares only the selected working directory, sends the task over ACP, and returns the child's final answer or a safe error; intermediate messages and tool traffic stay outside the parent conversation. Permission prompts are answered by configured policy without human interaction. Choose it when delegation needs process isolation or a non-Harness ACP agent, and choose an in-process backend when the child must share parent capabilities.\n\n== team\nagent-team\nREADME.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\ntool-agent-team\n==> packages/team/agent-team/README.md <==\n---\ndescription: \"Coordinate subagents through a durable shared task board in Team-enabled Sessions.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-team\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-agent-team` adds a durable shared task board to an existing subagent tree. Members use standard subagent tools to discover and contact one another. Team adds compare-and-set task ownership. Mount `dsh-tool-agent-team` for task tools. Members share one checkout.\n\n== preset\nagent-preset\nagent-preset-registry\npersona\nREADME.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\n==> packages/preset/agent-preset-registry/README.md <==\n---\ndescription: \"Choose an Agent’s tools, prompt sections and skills through declarative presets. One process can run several compositions. Failed definitions remain visible, while existing Agents retain the composition they already use.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset-registry\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nChoose an Agent’s tools, prompt sections and skills through declarative presets. One process can run several compositions. Failed definitions remain visible, while existing Agents retain the composition they already use.\n\n== workflow\nREADME.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\ntool-workflow\nworkflow\nworkflow-ptc\n==> packages/workflow/tool-workflow/README.md <==\n---\ndescription: \"The model-facing workflow tool: run a JavaScript orchestration script that fans out subagents, for users and maintainers choosing or configuring model-driven orchestration.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-workflow\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-tool-workflow` lets a model run JavaScript orchestration that delegates to many subagents and returns a final JSON value. Use it only when the user explicitly requests a workflow or large multi-agent orchestration; prefer plain subagent calls for one or two delegations. Foreground execution waits for all work; cancellation or abnormal completion returns an error rather than partial success. `run_in_background: true` returns an owned job id immediately and exposes live output. Deployments can rename the tool with `toolName` and cap rendered results with `maxResultChars`.\n\n== goal\ncommand-goal\ngoal\ngoal-round-driver\nREADME.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\ntool-goal\n==> packages/goal/command-goal/README.md <==\n---\ndescription: \"The human-facing /goal slash command for users and maintainers choosing, composing, or debugging goal control in UI command planes.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-command-goal\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-command-goal` gives users the `/goal` command to create, edit, pause, resume, clear, and inspect the current goal directly in an interactive UI. Commands and their direct output stay in the UI and do not enter model requests. Accepted changes persist, and ordered image or file attachments on a create or edit become one ordinary user message that later goal rounds can read. Use this package in interactive deployments with a command adapter; headless and automation apps without one do not need it.\n\n== plan\nplan-mode\nREADME.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\n---\ndescription: \"Plan mode for users and maintainers choosing, configuring, or debugging the per-agent planning feature with deployment guidance, a /plan command, and a user-reviewed exit.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-plan-mode\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nPlan mode asks an agent to explore and design before execution, then presents the finished plan for your approval. Enter it with `/plan`, optionally with a message or ordered image and file attachments; leave with `/plan off`, approve the review to continue, or return feedback for more planning. Deployment-defined guidance controls planning behavior, but every tool remains available, so use sandbox mode and approval prompts for enforced limits. The active state survives session resume and forks. Choose it when you want a reviewed plan before the agent acts.\n\n## Table of Contents\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\nfor p in subagent team preset workflow goal plan; do echo \\\"== $p\\\"; ls packages/$p; head -20 packages/$p/*/README.md 2>/dev/null | sed -n 1,14p; done\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916203482,"end":1793916204209}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1793916205169,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbcf871001fj1H604UQIXWGT","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbceb3b001ulMDZzHQUUenNp","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"5c3fec71fa62e2159229ac2e01ca4b74706f8b45","cost":0.000745212,"tokens":{"input":442,"output":275,"reasoning":77,"cache":{"read":155904,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793916206788,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbcfec4001JsjTHOLa3kQXzj","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbcf876001UEt74m5BMczNKt","type":"step-start","snapshot":"5c3fec71fa62e2159229ac2e01ca4b74706f8b45"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916207802,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbd01cb001MVcst05Lm5YLvY","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbcf876001UEt74m5BMczNKt","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9835084df169a61e","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\nsed -n 1,50p packages/goal/goal/README.md; echo \"===GOAL TOOL===\"; sed -n 1,30p packages/goal/tool-goal/README.md"},"output":"---\ndescription: \"The persisted same-session goal service for users and maintainers choosing, configuring, or debugging one durable completion objective per session.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-goal\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-goal` lets one long-running completion objective persist across turns, session resume, fork, and process restarts. Users and agents can create, edit, pause, resume, complete, block, or clear it; compare-and-set updates reject stale views. A configurable round cap (256 by default) bounds automatic continuation, and blocked goals retain a stable policy code with a human-readable explanation. The package stores goal state but does not schedule work, and continuation permission remains process-local rather than durable. Choose it for one objective spanning many turns; skip it for routine single-turn work or parallel objectives.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nMount `dsh-goal` whenever a session should remember one long-running completion objective across many turns and restarts. The package is a service: the model tools, the `/goal` command, and the continuation driver are separate packages that consume the same goal state, so mounting only this package stores and serves the goal without starting any work.\n\n### When to use it\n\nA goal suits one long-running completion objective that should continue across autonomous goal rounds — for example shipping a migration or fixing every failing documentation gate. Routine single-turn work should not create a goal. The service keeps at most one current goal per session: an unfinished goal must be edited, paused, resumed, blocked, or cleared before another takes its place, while a completed goal can be replaced directly.\n\n### Set up the service\n\nLoad the package with a composition entry; the only deployment choice is the default round cap applied to creates that do not name their own.\n\n```yaml\n- name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-goal'\n  config:\n    defaultMaxGoalRounds: 256\n```\n\n| Field | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `defaultMaxGoalRounds` | `256` | Round cap applied when a create request omits its own |\n\n`defaultMaxGoalRounds` must be a positive safe integer; a create request that names its own cap overrides it. The generated [configuration catalog](../../../docs/config-catalog.md#deepseek-aidsh-goal) is the exhaustive source for every accepted field.\n\n### Session projection\n===GOAL TOOL===\n---\ndescription: \"The model-facing goal tools for users and maintainers choosing, composing, or debugging get_goal, create_goal, and update_goal.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-goal\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-tool-goal` lets a model read persisted goals and infer and create a long-running goal from a direct human request. Creating, editing, pausing, or resuming requires that direct request in a top-level agent turn; completing or blocking also works in an autonomous goal round. Updates require the exact goal id and revision returned by a prior read. `resume` rearms active-but-disarmed or blocked goals, while users resume durable paused goals through Web or `/goal resume`. Autonomous blocking requires the same condition for a configurable threshold of three consecutive rounds by default.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nMount `dsh-tool-goal` beside the goal service when the model should create and update persisted goals itself. The tools are the model-facing half of the goal surface; the `/goal` command is the human-facing half, and the continuation driver uses the same tools to complete or block goals at the end of autonomous rounds.\n\n### Tools\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\ndescription: \"The persisted same-session goal service for users and maintainers choosing, configuring, or debugging one durable completion objective per session.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-goal\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-goal` lets one long-running completion objective persist across turns, session resume, fork, and process restarts. Users and agents can create, edit, pause, resume, complete, block, or clear it; compare-and-set updates reject stale views. A configurable round cap (256 by default) bounds automatic continuation, and blocked goals retain a stable policy code with a human-readable explanation. The package stores goal state but does not schedule work, and continuation permission remains process-local rather than durable. Choose it for one objective spanning many turns; skip it for routine single-turn work or parallel objectives.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nMount `dsh-goal` whenever a session should remember one long-running completion objective across many turns and restarts. The package is a service: the model tools, the `/goal` command, and the continuation driver are separate packages that consume the same goal state, so mounting only this package stores and serves the goal without starting any work.\n\n### When to use it\n\nA goal suits one long-running completion objective that should continue across autonomous goal rounds — for example shipping a migration or fixing every failing documentation gate. Routine single-turn work should not create a goal. The service keeps at most one current goal per session: an unfinished goal must be edited, paused, resumed, blocked, or cleared before another takes its place, while a completed goal can be replaced directly.\n\n### Set up the service\n\nLoad the package with a composition entry; the only deployment choice is the default round cap applied to creates that do not name their own.\n\n```yaml\n- name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-goal'\n  config:\n    defaultMaxGoalRounds: 256\n```\n\n| Field | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `defaultMaxGoalRounds` | `256` | Round cap applied when a create request omits its own |\n\n`defaultMaxGoalRounds` must be a positive safe integer; a create request that names its own cap overrides it. The generated [configuration catalog](../../../docs/config-catalog.md#deepseek-aidsh-goal) is the exhaustive source for every accepted field.\n\n### Session projection\n===GOAL TOOL===\n---\ndescription: \"The model-facing goal tools for users and maintainers choosing, composing, or debugging get_goal, create_goal, and update_goal.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-goal\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-tool-goal` lets a model read persisted goals and infer and create a long-running goal from a direct human request. Creating, editing, pausing, or resuming requires that direct request in a top-level agent turn; completing or blocking also works in an autonomous goal round. Updates require the exact goal id and revision returned by a prior read. `resume` rearms active-but-disarmed or blocked goals, while users resume durable paused goals through Web or `/goal resume`. Autonomous blocking requires the same condition for a configurable threshold of three consecutive rounds by default.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nMount `dsh-tool-goal` beside the goal service when the model should create and update persisted goals itself. The tools are the model-facing half of the goal surface; the `/goal` command is the human-facing half, and the continuation driver uses the same tools to complete or block goals at the end of autonomous rounds.\n\n### Tools\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\nsed -n 1,50p packages/goal/goal/README.md; echo \\\"===GOAL TOOL===\\\"; sed -n 1,30p packages/goal/tool-goal/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916207563,"end":1793916207802}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916207803,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbd00ef001aYL2yM2ca2XzVX","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbcf876001UEt74m5BMczNKt","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b177279682c14531","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\nsed -n 1,80p packages/preset/agent-preset-registry/README.md; echo \"===PERSONA===\"; sed -n 1,40p packages/preset/persona/README.md"},"output":"---\ndescription: \"Choose an Agent’s tools, prompt sections and skills through declarative presets. One process can run several compositions. Failed definitions remain visible, while existing Agents retain the composition they already use.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset-registry\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nChoose an Agent’s tools, prompt sections and skills through declarative presets. One process can run several compositions. Failed definitions remain visible, while existing Agents retain the composition they already use.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\n### Minimal configuration\n\n```yaml\n- id: agent-preset-registry\n  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset-registry'\n  config:\n    default: standard\n- id: preset-standard\n  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset'\n  config:\n    id: standard\n    plugins: []\n```\n\n| Field | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `default` | required | Preset ID used when none is requested |\n\nThe Web definitions come from the `dsh-web-app` bundle. Definitions are ordinary plugin rows; the registry neither scans directories nor accepts preset paths. The `selectedDefault` volatile field of the `agent-preset-registry` entry retains the user default, which new sessions resolve over the deployment `default`. A profile patch may still carry the retired `modeSelectionEnabled` field; the registry declares no such field and neither reads nor rewrites it.\n\nThe registry writes no declarations. The `read` Remote renders one declaration’s child list back as entry-list YAML (`!!js` conditions included) so a client can show what a preset composes; nothing accepts YAML back. A new preset or an override of a shipped one is a bundle patch: an `insert` of a `@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset` row, or a patch keyed by that row’s id, installed into the profile with `plugin_manager`; Creator mode authors such bundles in conversation.\n\n<a id=\"understand-the-implementation\"></a>\n## Understand the implementation\n\n<details>\n<summary>Implementation internals — click to expand</summary>\n\nEach declaration eagerly creates a registry-owned scope and an in-memory Loader tree. Updating or removing a declaration retires its previous revision. Agents, children and temporary historical reads retain references; releasing the final reference disposes the retired tree. Plugin registrations inherit the preset scope, and the Agent scope’s parent link controls visibility. The Host continues to share the Agent loop.\n\nA preset service provider and its consumers must share one `cordis:group` isolation realm. Entry-local isolation does not propagate through the Agent registration scope: ordinary Context lookup outside the group, including `agent.ctx`, still selects the Host realm. `agentPresets.serviceFor()` explicitly inspects the retained composition; it does not change ordinary service lookup.\n\nActivation auditing checks imports, missing services and globally leaked services. Import failures, activation failures and leaks reject the mount. A row waiting for a Host service stays mounted, and every read and binding re-audits it after the Host Loader tree settles, so startup order does not decide the outcome. Failure prevents new bindings to that definition. `presetForSession(session)` synchronously reads the latest recorded preset ID, or null when none is recorded. Session logs retain the preset ID and blank-session selections; recovery after restart uses the current definition of that ID and rejects a missing definition. `select` leaves a blank Session that records the requested preset and already runs its current revision unchanged, without another selection record or `tools/change`.\n\n`inspectCompositions()` reads all revisions retained by this registry; passing an Agent context selects its exact revision, or returns an empty list when it has no binding here. Each `AgentPresetInspection` contains a preset ID, detached active-module references with their resolution bases, and leaked-service names. Inspectors query the runtime service rather than a module-local mount table; the results contain no Loader trees or fibers. [composition-inventory.ts](src/composition-inventory.ts) defines these records.\n\n| File | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| [index.ts](src/index.ts) | Registration, revisions and Agent bindings |\n| [mount.ts](src/mount.ts) | Scoped plugin trees and activation auditing |\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"further-exploration\"></a>\n## Further Exploration\n\n- [Scope](../../core/scope/README.md) — Registration isolation.\n- [Agent](../../core/agent/README.md) — Session runtime.\n- [Cordis](../../../docs/cordis-primer.md) — Plugin configuration and lifecycle.\n\n<a id=\"model-experience\"></a>\n## Model Experience\n\n===PERSONA===\n---\ndescription: \"The composable persona row presets mount to give one agent its own system-prompt persona, for users and maintainers configuring or debugging it.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-persona\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-persona` gives one agent its own persona: a preset mounts this composable row to register persona prefix and suffix sections, shadowing the deployment-wide defaults for that session. It can also make the prefix the session's complete system prompt, suppressing every other section, and can turn off optional runtime context for the session. Mount it inside a preset composition — mounting it globally collides with the prompt registry's own persona registration and fails loud. Without this row, a preset could change an agent's tools but never its identity.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nMount this row inside a preset composition to give that preset's sessions their own persona. The row needs an agent scope: mounted outside one it collides with the prompt registry's own `deployment:persona-prefix` registration and fails loud — the deployment persona already has an owner, and the whole point of this row is to shadow it for one agent.\n\n### Configuration\n\n```yaml\n- name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-persona'\n  config:\n    prefix: You are a terse systems engineer who answers in short commands.\n```\n\n| Field | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `prefix` | required | Persona prose rendered as the `deployment:persona-prefix` section |\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\ndescription: \"Choose an Agent’s tools, prompt sections and skills through declarative presets. One process can run several compositions. Failed definitions remain visible, while existing Agents retain the composition they already use.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset-registry\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nChoose an Agent’s tools, prompt sections and skills through declarative presets. One process can run several compositions. Failed definitions remain visible, while existing Agents retain the composition they already use.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\n### Minimal configuration\n\n```yaml\n- id: agent-preset-registry\n  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset-registry'\n  config:\n    default: standard\n- id: preset-standard\n  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset'\n  config:\n    id: standard\n    plugins: []\n```\n\n| Field | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `default` | required | Preset ID used when none is requested |\n\nThe Web definitions come from the `dsh-web-app` bundle. Definitions are ordinary plugin rows; the registry neither scans directories nor accepts preset paths. The `selectedDefault` volatile field of the `agent-preset-registry` entry retains the user default, which new sessions resolve over the deployment `default`. A profile patch may still carry the retired `modeSelectionEnabled` field; the registry declares no such field and neither reads nor rewrites it.\n\nThe registry writes no declarations. The `read` Remote renders one declaration’s child list back as entry-list YAML (`!!js` conditions included) so a client can show what a preset composes; nothing accepts YAML back. A new preset or an override of a shipped one is a bundle patch: an `insert` of a `@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset` row, or a patch keyed by that row’s id, installed into the profile with `plugin_manager`; Creator mode authors such bundles in conversation.\n\n<a id=\"understand-the-implementation\"></a>\n## Understand the implementation\n\n<details>\n<summary>Implementation internals — click to expand</summary>\n\nEach declaration eagerly creates a registry-owned scope and an in-memory Loader tree. Updating or removing a declaration retires its previous revision. Agents, children and temporary historical reads retain references; releasing the final reference disposes the retired tree. Plugin registrations inherit the preset scope, and the Agent scope’s parent link controls visibility. The Host continues to share the Agent loop.\n\nA preset service provider and its consumers must share one `cordis:group` isolation realm. Entry-local isolation does not propagate through the Agent registration scope: ordinary Context lookup outside the group, including `agent.ctx`, still selects the Host realm. `agentPresets.serviceFor()` explicitly inspects the retained composition; it does not change ordinary service lookup.\n\nActivation auditing checks imports, missing services and globally leaked services. Import failures, activation failures and leaks reject the mount. A row waiting for a Host service stays mounted, and every read and binding re-audits it after the Host Loader tree settles, so startup order does not decide the outcome. Failure prevents new bindings to that definition. `presetForSession(session)` synchronously reads the latest recorded preset ID, or null when none is recorded. Session logs retain the preset ID and blank-session selections; recovery after restart uses the current definition of that ID and rejects a missing definition. `select` leaves a blank Session that records the requested preset and already runs its current revision unchanged, without another selection record or `tools/change`.\n\n`inspectCompositions()` reads all revisions retained by this registry; passing an Agent context selects its exact revision, or returns an empty list when it has no binding here. Each `AgentPresetInspection` contains a preset ID, detached active-module references with their resolution bases, and leaked-service names. Inspectors query the runtime service rather than a module-local mount table; the results contain no Loader trees or fibers. [composition-inventory.ts](src/composition-inventory.ts) defines these records.\n\n| File | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| [index.ts](src/index.ts) | Registration, revisions and Agent bindings |\n| [mount.ts](src/mount.ts) | Scoped plugin trees and activation auditing |\n\n</details>\n\n<a id=\"further-exploration\"></a>\n## Further Exploration\n\n- [Scope](../../core/scope/README.md) — Registration isolation.\n- [Agent](../../core/agent/README.md) — Session runtime.\n- [Cordis](../../../docs/cordis-primer.md) — Plugin configuration and lifecycle.\n\n<a id=\"model-experience\"></a>\n## Model Experience\n\n===PERSONA===\n---\ndescription: \"The composable persona row presets mount to give one agent its own system-prompt persona, for users and maintainers configuring or debugging it.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-persona\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-persona` gives one agent its own persona: a preset mounts this composable row to register persona prefix and suffix sections, shadowing the deployment-wide defaults for that session. It can also make the prefix the session's complete system prompt, suppressing every other section, and can turn off optional runtime context for the session. Mount it inside a preset composition — mounting it globally collides with the prompt registry's own persona registration and fails loud. Without this row, a preset could change an agent's tools but never its identity.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nMount this row inside a preset composition to give that preset's sessions their own persona. The row needs an agent scope: mounted outside one it collides with the prompt registry's own `deployment:persona-prefix` registration and fails loud — the deployment persona already has an owner, and the whole point of this row is to shadow it for one agent.\n\n### Configuration\n\n```yaml\n- name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-persona'\n  config:\n    prefix: You are a terse systems engineer who answers in short commands.\n```\n\n| Field | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `prefix` | required | Persona prose rendered as the `deployment:persona-prefix` section |\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\nsed -n 1,80p packages/preset/agent-preset-registry/README.md; echo \\\"===PERSONA===\\\"; sed -n 1,40p packages/preset/persona/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916207343,"end":1793916207803}}}}
