{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793916226152,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbd4a68001KfhlLp7duT2KJQ","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbd44a0001puE7DhJU02DUSr","type":"step-start","snapshot":"c13018833e9941d94451f1c0969a170766818f78"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916229378,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbd55e30025PwvfeJcFb8eFb","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbd44a0001puE7DhJU02DUSr","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b103575fcec2cf10","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\nsed -n 1,40p packages/goal/goal-round-driver/README.md; echo \"===PLAN EXIT===\"; grep -n \"user-questions\\|approval\\|headless\\|interactive\" packages/plan/plan-mode/README.md | head -12"},"output":"---\ndescription: \"The same-session continuation driver for users and maintainers choosing, composing, or debugging automatic goal rounds.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-goal-round-driver\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-goal-round-driver` automatically continues an active goal in the same session while the agent is idle, continuation is armed, and the configured round allowance remains. Each round gives the model another turn toward the objective; only goal rounds that reach model history consume the allowance, and exhaustion records a blocker. The driver has no configuration: the goal defines the round limit, and `dsh-tool-goal` defines when repeated blocking stops continuation. Mount it with `dsh-goal` and `dsh-tool-goal` for unattended multi-round progress; omit it when each step requires human steering.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nMount `dsh-goal-round-driver` when an active goal should keep making progress without human intervention. It composes with the goal service and the goal tools: the service owns the state, the tools give the model control over it, and this package schedules the rounds.\n\n### Compose it\n\nMount the driver beside the goal service and the goal tools; the driver itself takes no configuration.\n\n```yaml\n- id: goal\n  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-goal'\n\n- id: tool-goal\n  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-goal'\n\n===PLAN EXIT===\n12:Plan mode asks an agent to explore and design before execution, then presents the finished plan for your approval. Enter it with `/plan`, optionally with a message or ordered image and file attachments; leave with `/plan off`, approve the review to continue, or return feedback for more planning. Deployment-defined guidance controls planning behavior, but every tool remains available, so use sandbox mode and approval prompts for enforced limits. The active state survives session resume and forks. Choose it when you want a reviewed plan before the agent acts.\n32:Choose plan mode when the agent should explore and design before executing and you want to approve the plan first. It does not restrict the agent: every tool stays callable, so use sandbox mode and approval prompts when you need enforced limits. Skip it when the agent should act on your requests immediately, without a planning phase.\n63:Choosing `Keep planning` (optionally with free-text feedback) sends the agent back to revise the plan; closing the review to type a message instead ends the agent's turn, and your next message starts the next request. If no interactive review is available, `exit_plan_mode` cannot run and you can still leave plan mode with `/plan off`.\n93:`exit_plan_mode` is disclosed progressively: it is admitted from the first step whose selected plan state is active and stays admitted for the rest of the Session, so entering plan mode the first time adds it together with the guidance, and later transitions change only the guidance; Native and PTC execution use the same admitted set. A call while plan mode is not committed fails. An approved review records a silent pending exit that the next accepted in-turn pre-step appends, keeping plan guidance for the rest of the current tool batch. Without a user-questions channel, or after a service reload while the review is pending, the call fails closed and `/plan off` remains the manual escape.\n186:- **Guidance, not enforcement** — plan mode restrains through text only; deployments that need enforced restrictions configure sandbox mode and approval policy independently.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\ndescription: \"The same-session continuation driver for users and maintainers choosing, composing, or debugging automatic goal rounds.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-goal-round-driver\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-goal-round-driver` automatically continues an active goal in the same session while the agent is idle, continuation is armed, and the configured round allowance remains. Each round gives the model another turn toward the objective; only goal rounds that reach model history consume the allowance, and exhaustion records a blocker. The driver has no configuration: the goal defines the round limit, and `dsh-tool-goal` defines when repeated blocking stops continuation. Mount it with `dsh-goal` and `dsh-tool-goal` for unattended multi-round progress; omit it when each step requires human steering.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nMount `dsh-goal-round-driver` when an active goal should keep making progress without human intervention. It composes with the goal service and the goal tools: the service owns the state, the tools give the model control over it, and this package schedules the rounds.\n\n### Compose it\n\nMount the driver beside the goal service and the goal tools; the driver itself takes no configuration.\n\n```yaml\n- id: goal\n  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-goal'\n\n- id: tool-goal\n  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-goal'\n\n===PLAN EXIT===\n12:Plan mode asks an agent to explore and design before execution, then presents the finished plan for your approval. Enter it with `/plan`, optionally with a message or ordered image and file attachments; leave with `/plan off`, approve the review to continue, or return feedback for more planning. Deployment-defined guidance controls planning behavior, but every tool remains available, so use sandbox mode and approval prompts for enforced limits. The active state survives session resume and forks. Choose it when you want a reviewed plan before the agent acts.\n32:Choose plan mode when the agent should explore and design before executing and you want to approve the plan first. It does not restrict the agent: every tool stays callable, so use sandbox mode and approval prompts when you need enforced limits. Skip it when the agent should act on your requests immediately, without a planning phase.\n63:Choosing `Keep planning` (optionally with free-text feedback) sends the agent back to revise the plan; closing the review to type a message instead ends the agent's turn, and your next message starts the next request. If no interactive review is available, `exit_plan_mode` cannot run and you can still leave plan mode with `/plan off`.\n93:`exit_plan_mode` is disclosed progressively: it is admitted from the first step whose selected plan state is active and stays admitted for the rest of the Session, so entering plan mode the first time adds it together with the guidance, and later transitions change only the guidance; Native and PTC execution use the same admitted set. A call while plan mode is not committed fails. An approved review records a silent pending exit that the next accepted in-turn pre-step appends, keeping plan guidance for the rest of the current tool batch. Without a user-questions channel, or after a service reload while the review is pending, the call fails closed and `/plan off` remains the manual escape.\n186:- **Guidance, not enforcement** — plan mode restrains through text only; deployments that need enforced restrictions configure sandbox mode and approval policy independently.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\nsed -n 1,40p packages/goal/goal-round-driver/README.md; echo \\\"===PLAN EXIT===\\\"; grep -n \\\"user-questions\\\\|approval\\\\|headless\\\\|interactive\\\" packages/plan/plan-mode/README.md | head -12\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916229091,"end":1793916229378}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1793916230159,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbd5a0f0014j4ukox2sGd6MK","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbd44a0001puE7DhJU02DUSr","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"c13018833e9941d94451f1c0969a170766818f78","cost":0.001237272,"tokens":{"input":1404,"output":152,"reasoning":714,"cache":{"read":169024,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793916231667,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbd5ff3001w9YC70W9Qaq2sx","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbd5a14001mAfLCwAEGSfWBo","type":"step-start","snapshot":"c13018833e9941d94451f1c0969a170766818f78"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916240108,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbd68b7002jSWLvuKc3if5fg","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbd5a14001mAfLCwAEGSfWBo","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a1c7dfe5a58087e5","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 5: Agents, subagents, commands and goal\n\n## Focus\n\nAngle 5: how this repo's `.skilled/agents/*.md` personas can be reached (subagents, presets, skills\nor prompt inlining), how the nested command tree can be exposed, and whether the harness's goal and\nplan support maps onto the repo's packet `goal.md` binding.\n\n## Actions Taken\n\n- Read the base bundle patch to establish what a headless profile actually mounts.\n- Read the `agent-preset-registry`, `persona`, `tool-subagent`, `command-goal`, `goal`,\n  `goal-round-driver` and `plan-mode` references.\n- Read the subagent tool schema and the command-plane line in the architecture note.\n- Compared against `.pi/prompts`, `.pi/agents` and `.hermes/prompts`.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. The base bundle already mounts the goal service, the goal round driver, `command-goal`,\n   `plan-mode`, `tool-goal`, both in-process subagent providers, the subagent and subagent-control\n   tools, the workflow engine and tool, `skill-filesystem` and `tool-skill`. Headless inherits all of\n   it and disables only HMR, credentials, authorization and the DeepSeek account row, so delegation,\n   workflows and goals need no extra configuration to be present.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:315-453]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/cordis.patch.yml:35-46]\n2. Agent identity in a headless dispatch is the `system-prompt` row's `personaPrefix`. Headless\n   ships a one-line coding persona, and a `--patch` overlay can replace that prefix with an agent\n   body, giving one persona per dispatch. There is no agent-file loader: nothing scans\n   `.skilled/agents` or `.dsh/agents`. [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/cordis.patch.yml:9-12]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:521-526]\n3. Presets are the declarative multi-composition mechanism: an `@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset` row\n   with `id` and `plugins`, mounted beside `agent-preset-registry`, with a `persona` row able to set\n   the prefix and suffix for that composition only. Preset definitions are plugin rows, not scanned\n   directories, and the registry is mounted by the web app, not by base or headless.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/preset/agent-preset-registry/README.md:26-41]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/preset/persona/README.md:24-35]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/web-app/cordis.patch.yml:598-599]\n4. The `subagent` tool takes `description`, `prompt`, optional `name` and `cwd`; a child preset is\n   selectable only through `agent_preset` when the tool is configured with a non-empty\n   `allowedAgentPresets`, and there is no persona-file argument. A repo agent therefore reaches a\n   subagent either as an inlined persona in the `prompt` or as a preset that must first be mounted.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/tool-catalog.md:2064-2110]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/subagent/tool-subagent/README.md:47-53]\n5. Human commands are plugin registrations on `ctx.commands` that dispatch without a model turn;\n   there is no filesystem command loader. `command-goal` states that headless and automation apps\n   without a command adapter do not need the `/goal` command, so `.skilled/commands/**` cannot\n   become native slash commands in a headless dispatch and must be composed into the prompt as\n   templates by the dispatcher. [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/architecture.md:155]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/goal/command-goal/README.md:12]\n6. The goal service stores at most one goal per session, with compare-and-set updates keyed by id and\n   revision, a default 256-round cap, and create/edit/pause/resume requiring a direct human request\n   in a top-level agent turn; completing or blocking also works in an autonomous goal round. Nothing\n   reads a `goal.md` file, so a packet goal binding needs either a plugin at session start or\n   pre-step, or explicit instructions that make the model call `create_goal` with the packet goal's\n   text. [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/goal/goal/README.md:12-34]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/goal/tool-goal/README.md:12-18]\n7. The goal round driver automatically continues an active goal while the agent is idle and the round\n   allowance remains. In an unattended headless dispatch that is a real overrun risk: a goal created\n   from the task could keep the process alive for up to 256 rounds, far past a fan-out lineage's\n   per-iteration timeout. [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/goal/goal-round-driver/README.md:12-30]\n8. Plan mode is mounted in base, but `exit_plan_mode` presents the plan over the user-questions seam\n   and fails closed when no interactive review exists; headless has no live approval-response\n   channel. Plan mode must therefore stay off in unattended dispatches, and the exit tool is the\n   first thing to disappear if a profile trims tools.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/plan/plan-mode/README.md:63]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/plan/plan-mode/README.md:93]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/README.md:100]\n9. The Pi and Hermes precedents both expose commands as prompt templates\n   (`.pi/prompts/<flattened-name>.md`, `.hermes/prompts/*.md`) and agents as mirrored files or\n   persona skills. The harness has no `--prompt-template` or agent flag, so those two surfaces must\n   be carried by the dispatch builder and the skill catalog, not by the runtime.\n   [SOURCE: .pi/SYNC.md:26-34]\n   [SOURCE: .hermes/SYNC.md:22-26]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- An agent-file loader or an `--agent` flag: none exists; personas come from the system-prompt row,\n  presets or the prompt body.\n- Native slash commands from `.skilled/commands/**`: the command plane is plugin-registered and\n  headless has no adapter.\n- Plan mode as an unattended gate: its exit path fails closed without an interactive review.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Looking for a generic prompt-template loader: `tool-workflow` runs model-written JavaScript, not\n  repo-authored prompt files, and Pi's `--prompt-template` has no harness equivalent.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: \"agents as harness subagents\" could mean the `subagent` tool or the subagent\n  provider packages; both are recorded, and neither loads a persona file.\n- Contradictory evidence: the agent preset registry is present in the source and used by the web app,\n  but absent from base, so \"presets exist\" and \"headless can select a preset\" are different claims.\n- Missing dependencies: no configured provider, so no live preset or goal round was exercised.\n- Partial success: the mount inventory is confirmed from the base patch; the behavioral risks\n  (goal rounds, plan review) are documented but not run.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- `dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:315-453,521-526`\n- `dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/cordis.patch.yml:9-12,35-46`\n- `dsh-src/packages/bundle/web-app/cordis.patch.yml:598-599`\n- `dsh-src/packages/preset/agent-preset-registry/README.md:26-41`\n- `dsh-src/packages/preset/persona/README.md:24-35`\n- `dsh-src/packages/subagent/tool-subagent/README.md:47-53`\n- `dsh-src/docs/tool-catalog.md:2064-2110`\n- `dsh-src/packages/goal/{goal,tool-goal,goal-round-driver,command-goal}/README.md`\n- `dsh-src/packages/plan/plan-mode/README.md:63,93`\n- `dsh-src/docs/architecture.md:155`\n- `.pi/SYNC.md:26-34`, `.hermes/SYNC.md:22-26`\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 0.90 (seven fully new findings, one that reframes the known Pi/Hermes\n  prompt-template precedent as a missing harness feature)\n- Questions addressed: Angle 5 agents, subagents, commands and goal\n- Questions answered: How can this repo's agents, subagents, commands and goal binding be reached by the harness?\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: reading the base bundle before the feature packages showed that headless\n  already carries delegation, workflows and goals, which changes the integration from \"build these\"\n  to \"constrain these\".\n- What did not work and why: the preset documentation reads as if presets are universal, but the\n  registry mount is web-only; the bundle grep was the only way to see that.\n- What I would do differently: check the base mount list for every angle, since it is the single\n  source of what a headless dispatch can actually do.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nAngle 6: plugins, hooks and guards, including which lifecycle seams exist and which of the 18 Pi\nextensions must be ported, deferred or dropped.\n","path":"specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 5: Agents, subagents, commands and goal\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nAngle 5: how this repo's `.skilled/agents/*.md` personas can be reached (subagents, presets, skills\\nor prompt inlining), how the nested command tree can be exposed, and whether the harness's goal and\\nplan support maps onto the repo's packet `goal.md` binding.\\n\\n## Actions Taken\\n\\n- Read the base bundle patch to establish what a headless profile actually mounts.\\n- Read the `agent-preset-registry`, `persona`, `tool-subagent`, `command-goal`, `goal`,\\n  `goal-round-driver` and `plan-mode` references.\\n- Read the subagent tool schema and the command-plane line in the architecture note.\\n- Compared against `.pi/prompts`, `.pi/agents` and `.hermes/prompts`.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. The base bundle already mounts the goal service, the goal round driver, `command-goal`,\\n   `plan-mode`, `tool-goal`, both in-process subagent providers, the subagent and subagent-control\\n   tools, the workflow engine and tool, `skill-filesystem` and `tool-skill`. Headless inherits all of\\n   it and disables only HMR, credentials, authorization and the DeepSeek account row, so delegation,\\n   workflows and goals need no extra configuration to be present.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:315-453]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/cordis.patch.yml:35-46]\\n2. Agent identity in a headless dispatch is the `system-prompt` row's `personaPrefix`. Headless\\n   ships a one-line coding persona, and a `--patch` overlay can replace that prefix with an agent\\n   body, giving one persona per dispatch. There is no agent-file loader: nothing scans\\n   `.skilled/agents` or `.dsh/agents`. [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/cordis.patch.yml:9-12]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:521-526]\\n3. Presets are the declarative multi-composition mechanism: an `@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset` row\\n   with `id` and `plugins`, mounted beside `agent-preset-registry`, with a `persona` row able to set\\n   the prefix and suffix for that composition only. Preset definitions are plugin rows, not scanned\\n   directories, and the registry is mounted by the web app, not by base or headless.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/preset/agent-preset-registry/README.md:26-41]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/preset/persona/README.md:24-35]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/web-app/cordis.patch.yml:598-599]\\n4. The `subagent` tool takes `description`, `prompt`, optional `name` and `cwd`; a child preset is\\n   selectable only through `agent_preset` when the tool is configured with a non-empty\\n   `allowedAgentPresets`, and there is no persona-file argument. A repo agent therefore reaches a\\n   subagent either as an inlined persona in the `prompt` or as a preset that must first be mounted.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/tool-catalog.md:2064-2110]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/subagent/tool-subagent/README.md:47-53]\\n5. Human commands are plugin registrations on `ctx.commands` that dispatch without a model turn;\\n   there is no filesystem command loader. `command-goal` states that headless and automation apps\\n   without a command adapter do not need the `/goal` command, so `.skilled/commands/**` cannot\\n   become native slash commands in a headless dispatch and must be composed into the prompt as\\n   templates by the dispatcher. [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/architecture.md:155]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/goal/command-goal/README.md:12]\\n6. The goal service stores at most one goal per session, with compare-and-set updates keyed by id and\\n   revision, a default 256-round cap, and create/edit/pause/resume requiring a direct human request\\n   in a top-level agent turn; completing or blocking also works in an autonomous goal round. Nothing\\n   reads a `goal.md` file, so a packet goal binding needs either a plugin at session start or\\n   pre-step, or explicit instructions that make the model call `create_goal` with the packet goal's\\n   text. [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/goal/goal/README.md:12-34]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/goal/tool-goal/README.md:12-18]\\n7. The goal round driver automatically continues an active goal while the agent is idle and the round\\n   allowance remains. In an unattended headless dispatch that is a real overrun risk: a goal created\\n   from the task could keep the process alive for up to 256 rounds, far past a fan-out lineage's\\n   per-iteration timeout. [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/goal/goal-round-driver/README.md:12-30]\\n8. Plan mode is mounted in base, but `exit_plan_mode` presents the plan over the user-questions seam\\n   and fails closed when no interactive review exists; headless has no live approval-response\\n   channel. Plan mode must therefore stay off in unattended dispatches, and the exit tool is the\\n   first thing to disappear if a profile trims tools.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/plan/plan-mode/README.md:63]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/plan/plan-mode/README.md:93]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/README.md:100]\\n9. The Pi and Hermes precedents both expose commands as prompt templates\\n   (`.pi/prompts/<flattened-name>.md`, `.hermes/prompts/*.md`) and agents as mirrored files or\\n   persona skills. The harness has no `--prompt-template` or agent flag, so those two surfaces must\\n   be carried by the dispatch builder and the skill catalog, not by the runtime.\\n   [SOURCE: .pi/SYNC.md:26-34]\\n   [SOURCE: .hermes/SYNC.md:22-26]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- An agent-file loader or an `--agent` flag: none exists; personas come from the system-prompt row,\\n  presets or the prompt body.\\n- Native slash commands from `.skilled/commands/**`: the command plane is plugin-registered and\\n  headless has no adapter.\\n- Plan mode as an unattended gate: its exit path fails closed without an interactive review.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Looking for a generic prompt-template loader: `tool-workflow` runs model-written JavaScript, not\\n  repo-authored prompt files, and Pi's `--prompt-template` has no harness equivalent.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: \\\"agents as harness subagents\\\" could mean the `subagent` tool or the subagent\\n  provider packages; both are recorded, and neither loads a persona file.\\n- Contradictory evidence: the agent preset registry is present in the source and used by the web app,\\n  but absent from base, so \\\"presets exist\\\" and \\\"headless can select a preset\\\" are different claims.\\n- Missing dependencies: no configured provider, so no live preset or goal round was exercised.\\n- Partial success: the mount inventory is confirmed from the base patch; the behavioral risks\\n  (goal rounds, plan review) are documented but not run.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- `dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:315-453,521-526`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/cordis.patch.yml:9-12,35-46`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/bundle/web-app/cordis.patch.yml:598-599`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/preset/agent-preset-registry/README.md:26-41`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/preset/persona/README.md:24-35`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/subagent/tool-subagent/README.md:47-53`\\n- `dsh-src/docs/tool-catalog.md:2064-2110`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/goal/{goal,tool-goal,goal-round-driver,command-goal}/README.md`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/plan/plan-mode/README.md:63,93`\\n- `dsh-src/docs/architecture.md:155`\\n- `.pi/SYNC.md:26-34`, `.hermes/SYNC.md:22-26`\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 0.90 (seven fully new findings, one that reframes the known Pi/Hermes\\n  prompt-template precedent as a missing harness feature)\\n- Questions addressed: Angle 5 agents, subagents, commands and goal\\n- Questions answered: How can this repo's agents, subagents, commands and goal binding be reached by the harness?\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: reading the base bundle before the feature packages showed that headless\\n  already carries delegation, workflows and goals, which changes the integration from \\\"build these\\\"\\n  to \\\"constrain these\\\".\\n- What did not work and why: the preset documentation reads as if presets are universal, but the\\n  registry mount is web-only; the bundle grep was the only way to see that.\\n- What I would do differently: check the base mount list for every angle, since it is the single\\n  source of what a headless dispatch can actually do.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nAngle 6: plugins, hooks and guards, including which lifecycle seams exist and which of the 18 Pi\\nextensions must be ported, deferred or dropped.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916233911,"end":1793916240108}}}}
