{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916276981,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbe0fbc001V1z9N4WCz0FSuX","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbe0402001Eb3uQOACL8dD3n","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8ff8c8c21241ffba","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\nsed -n 1,40p packages/guard/timeout-policy/README.md; echo \"===HOOKS CC===\"; sed -n 1,30p packages/experimental/hooks-claude-code/README.md 2>/dev/null"},"output":"---\ndescription: \"Cooperative time limit for cancellation-aware tool calls, mapping a settled timeout to a clear model error for users and maintainers choosing or debugging the plugin.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-call-timeout-policy\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nUse this package to give tool calls their configured cooperative time limits and return a clear timeout error to the model after cancellation settles. Calls that finish in time are unchanged. A tool that ignores or slowly handles cancellation can keep the caller waiting because the package cannot hard-stop downstream work. Each tool supplies its own limit; the package has no configuration and is enabled in the `dsh` base bundle.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nThe common path is one line: add the plugin to the composition — the `dsh` base bundle already has it. Tools that have a limit configured are protected automatically; every other tool is untouched.\n\n### When to choose it\n\nChoose it when the model calls tools that can take a long time, those tools honor `exec.signal`, and you want a predictable timed-out answer after cancellation settles. Avoid it when a tool must be hard-stopped at its limit — the plugin can only ask a tool to stop, so a tool that ignores cancellation keeps running and keeps the caller waiting — and when you want one default limit for every tool, because each tool's limit comes from that tool's own configuration.\n\n### Setting it up\n\nMount the plugin with no configuration:\n\n```yaml\n- name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-call-timeout-policy'\n```\n===HOOKS CC===\n---\ndescription: \"Run your existing Claude Code hooks.json or settings hook config during agent runs — block prompts and tools, attach context, or force continuation — for users and maintainers of the bridge.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-hooks-claude-code\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-hooks-claude-code` runs command hooks from your existing Claude Code `hooks.json` or settings file during agent runs, without requiring a rewrite. Supported hooks can run when sessions, prompts, tools, stops, or subagents reach matching moments. They can block prompts or tool calls with model-visible reasons, add conversation context, or force another model turn. Choose this package to reuse Claude Code command hooks in the harness; use a native plugin for behavior that has no Claude Code equivalent.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nHook processes and their payload `cwd` use the Session's current working directory when each hook point starts. `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR` follows it unless `projectDir` is configured. `SubagentStop` reads the child's final committed directory after disposal; it cannot recover a missing directory or mutate that Session, and a failed stop hook is warned.\n\nMount this package, point `configPath` at your hook config, and the hooks you already have start firing at the corresponding moments in agent runs. There is nothing else to set up before the first hook works.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\ndescription: \"Cooperative time limit for cancellation-aware tool calls, mapping a settled timeout to a clear model error for users and maintainers choosing or debugging the plugin.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-call-timeout-policy\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nUse this package to give tool calls their configured cooperative time limits and return a clear timeout error to the model after cancellation settles. Calls that finish in time are unchanged. A tool that ignores or slowly handles cancellation can keep the caller waiting because the package cannot hard-stop downstream work. Each tool supplies its own limit; the package has no configuration and is enabled in the `dsh` base bundle.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nThe common path is one line: add the plugin to the composition — the `dsh` base bundle already has it. Tools that have a limit configured are protected automatically; every other tool is untouched.\n\n### When to choose it\n\nChoose it when the model calls tools that can take a long time, those tools honor `exec.signal`, and you want a predictable timed-out answer after cancellation settles. Avoid it when a tool must be hard-stopped at its limit — the plugin can only ask a tool to stop, so a tool that ignores cancellation keeps running and keeps the caller waiting — and when you want one default limit for every tool, because each tool's limit comes from that tool's own configuration.\n\n### Setting it up\n\nMount the plugin with no configuration:\n\n```yaml\n- name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-call-timeout-policy'\n```\n===HOOKS CC===\n---\ndescription: \"Run your existing Claude Code hooks.json or settings hook config during agent runs — block prompts and tools, attach context, or force continuation — for users and maintainers of the bridge.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-hooks-claude-code\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-hooks-claude-code` runs command hooks from your existing Claude Code `hooks.json` or settings file during agent runs, without requiring a rewrite. Supported hooks can run when sessions, prompts, tools, stops, or subagents reach matching moments. They can block prompts or tool calls with model-visible reasons, add conversation context, or force another model turn. Choose this package to reuse Claude Code command hooks in the harness; use a native plugin for behavior that has no Claude Code equivalent.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nHook processes and their payload `cwd` use the Session's current working directory when each hook point starts. `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR` follows it unless `projectDir` is configured. `SubagentStop` reads the child's final committed directory after disposal; it cannot recover a missing directory or mutate that Session, and a failed stop hook is warned.\n\nMount this package, point `configPath` at your hook config, and the hooks you already have start firing at the corresponding moments in agent runs. There is nothing else to set up before the first hook works.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\nsed -n 1,40p packages/guard/timeout-policy/README.md; echo \\\"===HOOKS CC===\\\"; sed -n 1,30p packages/experimental/hooks-claude-code/README.md 2>/dev/null\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916276668,"end":1793916276981}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916276982,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbe0e90001keHmBJzyyRWRcx","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbe0402001Eb3uQOACL8dD3n","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a392ee8f416ba7b9","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\ngrep -n \"apply(ctx\\|export function apply\\|local\\|file:\\|link:\\|path\" docs/cordis-primer.md | head -25; echo \"===PLUGIN ADD===\"; grep -n \"add\\|file:\\|git\\|local\" packages/boot/plugin-manager/README.md | head -25"},"output":"9:- **A plugin is a object that implements Service.** It can be a function with optional `inject` and `apply(ctx)` fields, or a `Service` subclass whose lifecycle Cordis mounts into the current context.\n===PLUGIN ADD===\n31:Base-backed profiles provide the manager. In Web, the sidebar's **Plugins** page ([ui-plugin-manager](../../client/ui-plugin-manager/README.md)) manages the profile's bundles and their uniquely addressable rows; the Settings Plugin list stays read-only. Agent-preset rows remain read-only. The `plugin_manager` tool exposes the same operations and is enabled in the Standard and Creator presets. Other Web presets keep it disabled by default. The full SDK profile enables it at profile scope in native/PTC mode; without HMR, saved changes return `restart-required` with `no-hmr` and apply after the runtime restarts. Every tool action requires `danger-full-access` or approval for that call. Under lower sandbox modes, `ask` requests approval; `never`, rejection, cancellation, or an unavailable approval channel prevents execution. An approval leaves the session permission mode unchanged. Profile changes persist across sessions, and installed Host code executes in-process outside the workspace sandbox. Dependency build-script approval remains separate.\n46:`listBundles` includes optional display `meta` for each bundle and its declared plugin rows, including disabled bundles. The Client selects a language from these values. The separate `description` field is the bundle's raw `package.json.description`; a metadata diagnostic does not block management. `source` gives a profile dependency as a spec `pnpm add` accepts: a git address or URL as recorded without the user information of an http(s) URL (a query string stays as recorded), a `file:` or `link:` path made absolute against the profile or the home directory for `~`, and a registry range, tag, or alias after the package name, as is any spec of a package installed under another name; a bundle the installation supplies has none, even when the profile also depends on it, because the installation's copy is the one that loads. The `plugin_manager` tool omits UI display metadata and update eligibility from list results.\n48:`listBundles` marks shipped optional bundles and on-demand catalog entries as `official`, independently of package installation. `availability` identifies a readable installation-provided or profile package, or `missing`; on-demand catalog entries exclude undeclared transitive copies. `installed` only records whether the profile declares the dependency. An absent on-demand entry carries embedded localized metadata and a host-derived `installTarget` containing the running DSH version and install spec, without registry access. Profile-declared packages with unreadable files retain their declaration and expose the normal error.\n50:`inspect(spec, options)` reads what a spec names before anything installs: a registry name is asked of the registry through `pnpm view`, run in the profile directory so the same proxy and authentication settings apply as to the install; an absolute path has its `package.json` read; a git address or tarball answers only its form and the `host` it is fetched from. The answer carries the name, version, description, whether the package declares a bundle, and the `registry` that answered, or a `problem`: `invalid-spec`, `already-installed`, `not-found`, `not-a-package`, `not-a-bundle`, `network`, or `unknown`, with the `registries` asked. A caller's `signal` or `inspectTimeoutMs` ends the lookup.\n54:Official on-demand installs call the same `installBundle` operation as other bundles. Registry targets use `saveExact: true`; development targets retain the ordinary `link:` dependency. An explicit update removes the installed package and installs the target with the current `enabled` selection; without HMR, a bundle that runs since startup cannot be removed, so the installation goes over it and reports `restart-required`. A failed or cancelled installation after the removal leaves the package uninstalled, and the reinstalled bundle joins the end of the selection. Turning Off only deselects the layer, and Remove uses the existing removal checks. Installation failure restores the profile manifest and lockfile; activation failure after installation can retain the saved dependency and selection. The existing peer-compatibility check still applies, without an additional Official-only version admission rule.\n60:`installBundle` checks GitHub repositories with `git ls-remote` before starting pnpm, using the profile directory and the installer's Git and proxy configuration. `githubConnectionTimeoutMs` defaults to 5000 ms and limits only this check, not package download or builds. The check disables credential helpers and prompts; only network failures and timeouts stop installation, reporting `failedAt: 'spec-host'` with the existing failure kind and diagnostic log. A stopped installation streams that failure under its `requestId` as the installation's only `plugin-manager/install-log` run, named by the `git ls-remote` command line with any URL user information removed; a timed-out check reports no exit code. The answer's `packageResult` stays a plain result, without the command line. Authentication, repository lookup and other failures are left to pnpm, including its HTTPS-to-SSH fallback. Cancellation and manager disposal stop the check and its descendants. Registry packages, paths, tarballs and other Git hosts skip this check. A reachable repository can still fail during download or bundle validation.\n62:An HTTP(S) Git URL on a host for which pnpm has no HTTPS-to-SSH fallback, that is, any host `hosted-git-info` does not know, such as a self-hosted GitLab, is probed with `git ls-remote` before pnpm starts. The probe uses the installer's Git configuration and credential helpers with Git's terminal and askpass prompts disabled; on POSIX it also has no controlling terminal, so SSH cannot prompt. When HTTP(S) cannot read the repository and `ssh://git@<host>/<path>` can, pnpm installs that SSH URL with the original fragment, and the profile records it. Otherwise the spec is installed unchanged. Each probe is bounded by `gitTransportTimeoutMs`, 5000 ms by default; cancellation and manager disposal stop it. URLs with credentials or a query skip the probe.\n68:`installBundle` accepts a caller-generated `requestId`, under which `plugin-manager/install-log` streams each pnpm run's output and `plugin-manager/install-state` announces `installing`, `cancelling`, and `applying`; a pnpm run that fails without printing streams the diagnostic its log carries, so the details panel is not left empty. `installing` is announced once per registry asked, with the `attempt`'s registry, position, and the plan's length. The install asks the same plan as the lookup, from `options.registry`, running `pnpm add` with `--registry` and restoring the profile files between attempts; it moves on for the failures another registry can change and stops at any other, and at a failure whose error line names the host a git or tarball spec is fetched from, which no registry stands in for; `failedAt` says which of the two the last failed run could not reach. `cancelInstall(requestId)` stops the run and answers `cancelled` only after the Git check or pnpm exited and the files are back, `too-late` once the bundle is being applied, and `not-running` for any other id; the install call then reports `application: 'cancelled'`. A run that fails, is cancelled, or adds a package without a bundle patch restores `package.json` and `pnpm-lock.yaml` as they were; `packageResult.kind` classifies the last run from its exit and output, `registries` names every registry asked, `bundle` names the package a finished run added, and `version` its manifest version, which can differ from the version `inspect` read, for example when pnpm's `minimumReleaseAge` holds back a recent release. `listBundles` carries each bundle's one-liner (the package `description`), the rows its patch declares with their live entries, and the built-in rows it overrides; it lists the profile's own bundles, the bundles the installation supplies, and a selected name without a bundle patch as a `not-bundle` problem, while an unselected plain dependency is left out. A bundle the launcher's `OPTIONAL_BUNDLES` names is `optional`: shipped switched off for the person to turn on, never removable, and selected by no shipped template ([rationale](../../../.agents/notes/implemented/process/2026-09-15-shipped-optional-bundles.md)). Every completed operation emits `plugin-manager/changed`; a patch generation applied outside the manager, by HMR's watcher after a CLI or hand edit, announces nothing, so the page learns of it on its next read.\n72:When pnpm 11 blocks dependency scripts, the failed installation reports every pending `allowBuilds` key in the profile under `pendingBuilds`, including keys left by earlier attempts, names the run reported as ignored, and the key pnpm suggests for a git-hosted package it refused to prepare. A key whose git URL carries a credential (a password, or a user name over HTTP(S)) is not recorded, so that installation offers only manual approval. pnpm writes those entries into `pnpm-workspace.yaml` only when it can prompt, so the manager records the reported names itself; a failed run restores `package.json` and `pnpm-lock.yaml` but deliberately not `pnpm-workspace.yaml`. The Web plugin page offers **Allow these scripts and retry**; the tool can grant permission on the user's behalf through `approvedBuilds` on `install_bundle`, after the user approves those scripts in the conversation. The service validates pending names; it does not verify conversation approval. Approval persists by the recorded key in this profile, which pins a git-hosted package to its commit, permits commands with the host user's permissions, and survives another installation failure. Only currently undecided names can be approved; existing denials and wildcard rules cannot be overridden through this action. Approval rejects YAML anchors or aliases inside `allowBuilds`. Retry preserves the original activation choice.\n77:An install command that names packages (`add`, or `install` with specs) is checked before pnpm runs: a local path is read from its own `package.json`, and a registry spec is resolved through pnpm's registry lookup for the version its range selects and the `engines` and `peerDependencies` that version declares. An unmet DSH requirement rejects the operation before pnpm runs, so nothing is downloaded and no build script runs; a build approval the caller supplied with the request is recorded before this check and remains. A git or tarball spec needs the fetch itself, so it is judged after installation: the operation then restores the profile manifest and lockfile, reinstalls the restored lockfile (or, when the profile had none, reinstalls from the restored manifest without creating one), and reports whether that recovery succeeded; already permitted build-script effects may remain. A dependency the run did not change never blocks an unrelated operation: it stays installed, the run reports a warning naming it, and profile startup denies it. Installation requests with `enabled: false` are checked the same way. Startup checks run independently; see [App boot](../app-boot/README.md#profiles) for range semantics. Version exemptions do not authorize dependency scripts.\n83:The CLI exposes `dsh plugin --profile <profile> version-exemptions`, `allow-version <package@version> --dsh-version <runtime> --accept-risk`, and `revoke-version <package@version> --dsh-version <runtime>`. A grant prints a risk warning before saving. A compatibility refusal carries the `incompatible-version` code with each refused package's `name`, `version`, `runtimeVersion`, and unmet `requirements`, keyed `engines.dsh` or by DSH peer package name; each surface renders that record itself. The Web page words it through its locale dictionary, and a CLI refusal prints the exact `allow-version` command. Use the tool or CLI to grant an exemption and retry the original operation.\n91:| `githubConnectionTimeoutMs` | `5000` | Deadline for the GitHub repository check before installation, in milliseconds. |\n92:| `gitTransportTimeoutMs` | `5000` | Deadline for each HTTP(S) and SSH access probe of a self-hosted Git repository before installation, in milliseconds. |\n100:| `detailMaxBytes` | `1048576` | Largest bundle detail document, and largest locale file read for one, in bytes. |\n108:`readBundleDetail(name, language, { npmFileSources? })` reads the long description that a bundle's page in **Plugins** shows, for a bundle `listBundles()` lists: the locale `meta.detail` file, otherwise the README, as steps 1 and 2 of the [document order](../../util/plugin-detail/README.md#document-order) define them. Without either, the result is `{ status: 'none' }`; the repository a bundle declares is not read. `npmFileSources` names npm file CDNs to ask first for an offered bundle, in order; configured CDNs it omits follow, and CDNs that `detailNpmFileSources` does not list are not asked. A found document carries its `origin` (`locale` or `readme`), its `path` inside the package, and its Markdown without a leading YAML front matter block.\n114:Failures: `plugin-manager/unknown-bundle` for a bundle `listBundles()` does not list or whose directory cannot be resolved, `plugin-manager/invalid-language`, and `plugin-manager/detail-unavailable` with `reason` `too-large`, `network`, or `invalid` for a malformed locale file or `meta.detail`, or a named file that does not exist. The image route answers them with 404, 413, 502, and 400.\n126:Without HMR, installing a new package, enabling a bundle and a successful removal publish the profile's runtime resolution; with HMR, every operation leaves publication to [HMR](../hmr/README.md)'s recomposition, which removes dropped mappings after their plugins stop. Shared dependencies may pass to another bundle's declaration when their normalized directory and scope stay unchanged. Without HMR, a deselected startup bundle still runs: while any such bundle is absent from the saved selection, subsequent package operations retain the entire current table and report `restart-required`. Other new installations and removals of non-running bundles can still publish without HMR; activation waits for restart. With HMR, `installBundle()` refuses a package that is already installed with `already-installed`, before pnpm runs, when the spec names it: a registry name, a local directory, or the git or tarball spec its dependency was saved with. A package that cannot be removed, such as a bundle carrying plugin management, therefore cannot be installed over with HMR. Installing another version is a removal and an installation, so the previous version's plugins stop, its modules are evicted and its mappings removed before the new one is installed; the Web page's Update for an Official plugin does both. A git or tarball spec whose package is known only after pnpm ran can still install over an installed package: without HMR it reports `restart-required`, and with HMR the recomposition replaces the running plugins whose loaded files changed. A replacement that fails to import or start reports `failed` with the activation diagnostic, which names the import error; the previous version no longer runs, and every later operation imports it again, so it starts once its files or dependencies are corrected. An unchanged import failure is a warning of later operations; a changed one fails them. A publication the runtime resolution rejects, because an installed package now resolves to another directory or a mapping of the DSH installation changed, leaves the composition as it was, after stopping and starting again the rows it would drop, and reports `restart-required` with `package-moved`. Disabling or removing a bundle evicts the modules of the packages whose mappings it drops. Every `restart-required` result carries `restartReason`: `no-hmr`, `package-moved`, or the [HMR pending reason](../hmr/README.md) of a changed module that loads only after a restart, unless the operation reports `overridden` ([decision](../../../.agents/notes/implemented/architecture/2026-09-30-profile-package-refresh-and-manifest-invalidation.md)).\n155:The [`plugin_manager` tool](../../../docs/tool-catalog.md#deepseek-aidsh-plugin-manager) lists plugin entries and bundles and performs profile-wide changes. Its results include saved-state changes, application status and package diagnostics. A change result that is not plainly applied adds `nextStep`, a sentence derived from its application status, refusal code, package-manager failure kind or restart reason that says what the model does next or tells the user. Management operations do not inject messages into Agents.\n159:The tool declaration is present when its consumer is mounted; each invocation adds its returned inventory or change result.\n170:- An approved git key stays in `pnpm-workspace.yaml` after the plugin moves to a newer commit; nothing removes stale approvals.\n174:- The Git transport probe keeps the parent console on Windows, so an SSH host-key or passphrase prompt can appear in the launching terminal until `gitTransportTimeoutMs`. On POSIX a host missing from `known_hosts` fails the SSH probe, so installation stays on HTTPS until the host key is accepted once, for example with `ssh -T git@<host>`. A credential-helper dialog or ssh-agent confirmation, such as a Touch ID or `ssh-add -c` dialog, that takes longer than the deadline counts as a failed probe, and an HTTPS probe that times out lets a reachable SSH form be saved to the profile. A private repository waits up to twice `gitTransportTimeoutMs` before pnpm starts. The SSH form always uses the `git` user and SSH's default port; other SSH endpoints need a `url.<base>.insteadOf` rule. When both probes fail, the diagnostic is pnpm's HTTPS failure; the SSH probe's reason is not reported.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9:- **A plugin is a object that implements Service.** It can be a function with optional `inject` and `apply(ctx)` fields, or a `Service` subclass whose lifecycle Cordis mounts into the current context.\n===PLUGIN ADD===\n31:Base-backed profiles provide the manager. In Web, the sidebar's **Plugins** page ([ui-plugin-manager](../../client/ui-plugin-manager/README.md)) manages the profile's bundles and their uniquely addressable rows; the Settings Plugin list stays read-only. Agent-preset rows remain read-only. The `plugin_manager` tool exposes the same operations and is enabled in the Standard and Creator presets. Other Web presets keep it disabled by default. The full SDK profile enables it at profile scope in native/PTC mode; without HMR, saved changes return `restart-required` with `no-hmr` and apply after the runtime restarts. Every tool action requires `danger-full-access` or approval for that call. Under lower sandbox modes, `ask` requests approval; `never`, rejection, cancellation, or an unavailable approval channel prevents execution. An approval leaves the session permission mode unchanged. Profile changes persist across sessions, and installed Host code executes in-process outside the workspace sandbox. Dependency build-script approval remains separate.\n46:`listBundles` includes optional display `meta` for each bundle and its declared plugin rows, including disabled bundles. The Client selects a language from these values. The separate `description` field is the bundle's raw `package.json.description`; a metadata diagnostic does not block management. `source` gives a profile dependency as a spec `pnpm add` accepts: a git address or URL as recorded without the user information of an http(s) URL (a query string stays as recorded), a `file:` or `link:` path made absolute against the profile or the home directory for `~`, and a registry range, tag, or alias after the package name, as is any spec of a package installed under another name; a bundle the installation supplies has none, even when the profile also depends on it, because the installation's copy is the one that loads. The `plugin_manager` tool omits UI display metadata and update eligibility from list results.\n48:`listBundles` marks shipped optional bundles and on-demand catalog entries as `official`, independently of package installation. `availability` identifies a readable installation-provided or profile package, or `missing`; on-demand catalog entries exclude undeclared transitive copies. `installed` only records whether the profile declares the dependency. An absent on-demand entry carries embedded localized metadata and a host-derived `installTarget` containing the running DSH version and install spec, without registry access. Profile-declared packages with unreadable files retain their declaration and expose the normal error.\n50:`inspect(spec, options)` reads what a spec names before anything installs: a registry name is asked of the registry through `pnpm view`, run in the profile directory so the same proxy and authentication settings apply as to the install; an absolute path has its `package.json` read; a git address or tarball answers only its form and the `host` it is fetched from. The answer carries the name, version, description, whether the package declares a bundle, and the `registry` that answered, or a `problem`: `invalid-spec`, `already-installed`, `not-found`, `not-a-package`, `not-a-bundle`, `network`, or `unknown`, with the `registries` asked. A caller's `signal` or `inspectTimeoutMs` ends the lookup.\n54:Official on-demand installs call the same `installBundle` operation as other bundles. Registry targets use `saveExact: true`; development targets retain the ordinary `link:` dependency. An explicit update removes the installed package and installs the target with the current `enabled` selection; without HMR, a bundle that runs since startup cannot be removed, so the installation goes over it and reports `restart-required`. A failed or cancelled installation after the removal leaves the package uninstalled, and the reinstalled bundle joins the end of the selection. Turning Off only deselects the layer, and Remove uses the existing removal checks. Installation failure restores the profile manifest and lockfile; activation failure after installation can retain the saved dependency and selection. The existing peer-compatibility check still applies, without an additional Official-only version admission rule.\n60:`installBundle` checks GitHub repositories with `git ls-remote` before starting pnpm, using the profile directory and the installer's Git and proxy configuration. `githubConnectionTimeoutMs` defaults to 5000 ms and limits only this check, not package download or builds. The check disables credential helpers and prompts; only network failures and timeouts stop installation, reporting `failedAt: 'spec-host'` with the existing failure kind and diagnostic log. A stopped installation streams that failure under its `requestId` as the installation's only `plugin-manager/install-log` run, named by the `git ls-remote` command line with any URL user information removed; a timed-out check reports no exit code. The answer's `packageResult` stays a plain result, without the command line. Authentication, repository lookup and other failures are left to pnpm, including its HTTPS-to-SSH fallback. Cancellation and manager disposal stop the check and its descendants. Registry packages, paths, tarballs and other Git hosts skip this check. A reachable repository can still fail during download or bundle validation.\n62:An HTTP(S) Git URL on a host for which pnpm has no HTTPS-to-SSH fallback, that is, any host `hosted-git-info` does not know, such as a self-hosted GitLab, is probed with `git ls-remote` before pnpm starts. The probe uses the installer's Git configuration and credential helpers with Git's terminal and askpass prompts disabled; on POSIX it also has no controlling terminal, so SSH cannot prompt. When HTTP(S) cannot read the repository and `ssh://git@<host>/<path>` can, pnpm installs that SSH URL with the original fragment, and the profile records it. Otherwise the spec is installed unchanged. Each probe is bounded by `gitTransportTimeoutMs`, 5000 ms by default; cancellation and manager disposal stop it. URLs with credentials or a query skip the probe.\n68:`installBundle` accepts a caller-generated `requestId`, under which `plugin-manager/install-log` streams each pnpm run's output and `plugin-manager/install-state` announces `installing`, `cancelling`, and `applying`; a pnpm run that fails without printing streams the diagnostic its log carries, so the details panel is not left empty. `installing` is announced once per registry asked, with the `attempt`'s registry, position, and the plan's length. The install asks the same plan as the lookup, from `options.registry`, running `pnpm add` with `--registry` and restoring the profile files between attempts; it moves on for the failures another registry can change and stops at any other, and at a failure whose error 