{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916310579,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbe917e0029fGsvjwXkWEBKZ","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbe7e71001PP0ljrvFPqauLh","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-95e6cae0a01d6301","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\ngrep -nE \"^\\| \\`@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-(fs|bash|web|search)\" docs/tool-catalog.md | head -10; grep -n \"### \\`write\\`\\|### \\`edit\\`\\|### \\`bash\\`\\|### \\`read\\`\\|### \\`webfetch\\`\" docs/tool-catalog.md | head -12"},"output":"26:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-bash` | `bash` | `ctx.workingDirectory`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.shell`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `ctx.shellEnv`, `ctx.jobs for run_in_background and the job-backed foreground path` | `tool/call`, `tool/result` | - | The bash tool is the model-facing consumer of the bash executor seam. With a job registry composed every call registers with the generic `ctx.jobs` runtime as it starts, collected/stopped through the `job_*` tools from `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-jobs`; without one, or with `enableRunInBackground: false`, the tool registers a foreground-only schema without the `run_in_background` parameter. |\n30:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-bash-persistent` | `bash` | `ctx.workingDirectory`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.terminals`, `an owning Agent at execution time` | `tool/call`, `PTY shell state`, `tool/result` | - | One owner-isolated persistent bash tool; deployment composition supplies the PTY backend and may override the model-facing environment description. |\n33:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-fs` | `apply_patch`, `edit`, `read`, `read_image`, `write` | `ctx.workingDirectory`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.fs`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `ctx.attachments (image-tool registration)`, `ctx.llm + an image-capable route (image-tool execution)` | `tool/call`, `fs/write-intent, fs/edit-intent, or fs/delete-intent for mutations`, `fs/observed after read presence/absence or successful file operation`, `durable attachment (read_image)`, `tool/result` | - | The read-before-write/edit policy is added by `@deepseek-ai/dsh-fs-observation-policy` (an `fs/*` event-gate plugin, no schema change); the shipped compositions leave its row disabled, so mutations are unconditional unless a deployment enables it. The image tool is not registered without `ctx.attachments`; its schema is route-independent, and execution refuses unless the exact routed model declares image input. `apply_patch` registers only with `applyPatch: true`; the catalog sets it so the schema is documented. `dsh-base` and the Web `standard` preset enable it; the Web `ptc` and `cordis` presets and the PTC overlay state `applyPatch: false`. |\n34:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-fs-search` | `glob`, `grep` | `ctx.workingDirectory`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.subprocess`, `ctx.systemPrompt` | `tool/call`, `tool/result` | - | glob and grep are unconditional discovery tools that spawn the packaged ripgrep binary (`@vscode/ripgrep`) through ctx.subprocess as ordinary foreground calls (never background jobs) — no host `rg` install and no shell layer. The catalog uses `sampleOverCapGlobResults: true`; deployments must choose that behavior explicitly. Capped results save the complete formatted list through the optional ctx.spillStore backend; returned locators are follow-up-readable/searchable when the backend exposes local paths in co-located deployments. |\n52:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-web` | `web_fetch`, `web_search` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.web`, `ctx.systemPrompt` | `tool/call`, `tool/result` | - | web_search and web_fetch keep provider selection behind ctx.web so model-visible schemas stay stable across backend swaps. |\n697:### `bash`\n886:### `bash`\n1067:### `edit`\n1102:### `read`\n1152:### `write`\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"26:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-bash` | `bash` | `ctx.workingDirectory`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.shell`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `ctx.shellEnv`, `ctx.jobs for run_in_background and the job-backed foreground path` | `tool/call`, `tool/result` | - | The bash tool is the model-facing consumer of the bash executor seam. With a job registry composed every call registers with the generic `ctx.jobs` runtime as it starts, collected/stopped through the `job_*` tools from `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-jobs`; without one, or with `enableRunInBackground: false`, the tool registers a foreground-only schema without the `run_in_background` parameter. |\n30:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-bash-persistent` | `bash` | `ctx.workingDirectory`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.terminals`, `an owning Agent at execution time` | `tool/call`, `PTY shell state`, `tool/result` | - | One owner-isolated persistent bash tool; deployment composition supplies the PTY backend and may override the model-facing environment description. |\n33:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-fs` | `apply_patch`, `edit`, `read`, `read_image`, `write` | `ctx.workingDirectory`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.fs`, `ctx.systemPrompt`, `ctx.attachments (image-tool registration)`, `ctx.llm + an image-capable route (image-tool execution)` | `tool/call`, `fs/write-intent, fs/edit-intent, or fs/delete-intent for mutations`, `fs/observed after read presence/absence or successful file operation`, `durable attachment (read_image)`, `tool/result` | - | The read-before-write/edit policy is added by `@deepseek-ai/dsh-fs-observation-policy` (an `fs/*` event-gate plugin, no schema change); the shipped compositions leave its row disabled, so mutations are unconditional unless a deployment enables it. The image tool is not registered without `ctx.attachments`; its schema is route-independent, and execution refuses unless the exact routed model declares image input. `apply_patch` registers only with `applyPatch: true`; the catalog sets it so the schema is documented. `dsh-base` and the Web `standard` preset enable it; the Web `ptc` and `cordis` presets and the PTC overlay state `applyPatch: false`. |\n34:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-fs-search` | `glob`, `grep` | `ctx.workingDirectory`, `ctx.tools`, `ctx.subprocess`, `ctx.systemPrompt` | `tool/call`, `tool/result` | - | glob and grep are unconditional discovery tools that spawn the packaged ripgrep binary (`@vscode/ripgrep`) through ctx.subprocess as ordinary foreground calls (never background jobs) — no host `rg` install and no shell layer. The catalog uses `sampleOverCapGlobResults: true`; deployments must choose that behavior explicitly. Capped results save the complete formatted list through the optional ctx.spillStore backend; returned locators are follow-up-readable/searchable when the backend exposes local paths in co-located deployments. |\n52:| `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-web` | `web_fetch`, `web_search` | `ctx.tools`, `ctx.web`, `ctx.systemPrompt` | `tool/call`, `tool/result` | - | web_search and web_fetch keep provider selection behind ctx.web so model-visible schemas stay stable across backend swaps. |\n697:### `bash`\n886:### `bash`\n1067:### `edit`\n1102:### `read`\n1152:### `write`\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\ngrep -nE \\\"^\\\\| \\\\`@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-(fs|bash|web|search)\\\" docs/tool-catalog.md | head -10; grep -n \\\"### \\\\`write\\\\`\\\\|### \\\\`edit\\\\`\\\\|### \\\\`bash\\\\`\\\\|### \\\\`read\\\\`\\\\|### \\\\`webfetch\\\\`\\\" docs/tool-catalog.md | head -12\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916309886,"end":1793916310579}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1793916311384,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbe9758001P6Ut5ILRi3IdJS","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbe7e71001PP0ljrvFPqauLh","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"e4f5712e15ba0df209414ca3a20344db473c8ab1","cost":0.001636632,"tokens":{"input":1100,"output":182,"reasoning":1264,"cache":{"read":201344,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793916313319,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbe9ee7001Zdc1QRm1Y9V2kJ","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbe975e001LXYk1B6f5knk9D","type":"step-start","snapshot":"e4f5712e15ba0df209414ca3a20344db473c8ab1"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916325100,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbeae63002EmIJJvn9C4A949","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbe975e001LXYk1B6f5knk9D","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-badba329e24bf287","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 6: Plugins, hooks and guards\n\n## Focus\n\nAngle 6: how a harness plugin is written, registered and loaded, which lifecycle seams exist, and\nhow each of this repo's Pi extensions should be handled — port, port later, or not relevant. The\nquestion behind it is whether one repo plugin bundle is smaller than one plugin per guard.\n\n## Actions Taken\n\n- Read the Cordis primer's plugin definition and the plugin-manager install contract.\n- Read the generated event producer/consumer matrix for the hook seam names.\n- Read the Claude Code hook bridge's supported-event table, mapping section and matcher rules.\n- Read the matcher implementation to check case sensitivity.\n- Inventoried `.pi/extensions/` and `.claude/settings.json` hook coverage by event and matcher.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. A harness plugin is a Cordis object: a function with optional `inject` and an `apply(ctx)` field,\n   or a `Service` subclass. It is registered as a profile row by package name (`id` + `name` +\n   optional `config`), not discovered from a project folder; a repo-local plugin must be installed\n   into the profile as a bundle (`dsh plugin --profile <name> add file:...` / `link:`), and without\n   HMR a newly installed or changed plugin reports `restart-required`.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/cordis-primer.md:9]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/boot/plugin-manager/README.md:31]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/boot/plugin-manager/README.md:46]\n2. The usable lifecycle seams are broader than the Pi extension API: `tools/pre-execute` (waterfall,\n   can deny or ask), `tools/execute`, `tools/post-execute` (waterfall, can block with feedback or\n   attach context), `tools/result` (emit), `agent/pre-step` (waterfall for prompt and context),\n   `agent/turn-stopping` (serial, can force another step), `agent/created`, `agent/disposed`,\n   `session/event`, `turn/start`, `turn/end`, `approval/request`, `fs/write-intent`,\n   `fs/edit-intent`, `fs/delete-intent`, `compaction/start|summary|prune|end` and `commands/change`.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/event-producer-consumer.md:30]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/event-producer-consumer.md:32-54]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/event-producer-consumer.md:76]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/event-producer-consumer.md:86-91]\n3. The shipped Claude Code hook bridge maps `SessionStart`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `PreToolUse`,\n   `PostToolUse`, `Stop`, `SubagentStart` and `SubagentStop` onto those seams; it does not support\n   `PreCompact` or `SessionEnd`. That leaves two Pi guards without a bridge point: the compact\n   context injector and the session-end cleanup.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/experimental/hooks-claude-code/README.md:56-70]\n4. Claude literal matchers are exact, case-sensitive pipe alternatives; only non-word patterns are\n   regexes. Harness tool names are lowercase (`bash`, `read`, `write`, `edit`, `glob`, `grep`,\n   `web_fetch`, `apply_patch`, `skill`, `subagent`), so the repo's existing `.claude/settings.json`\n   matchers `Bash`, `Write|Edit`, `WebFetch`, `Task`, `Task|Agent` and `AskUserQuestion` will not\n   select anything. A rewritten `.dsh/hooks.json` with harness tool names is required; the scripts\n   themselves are already runtime-neutral Node entry points.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/experimental/hook-protocol/src/matcher.ts:60-68]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/tool-catalog.md:26-52]\n5. The repo already carries the hook implementations this angle would otherwise port:\n   `.claude/settings.json` wires 9 `PreToolUse` entries (dispatch preflight, spec gate, git\n   preflight, git message gate, subagent guard, task dispatch guard twice, MCP route guard, spec\n   gate on writes), 5 `PostToolUse` entries (post-edit quality, dispatch audit, classifier injection\n   screen, context nudge, decision capture), 3 `UserPromptSubmit`, 8 `SessionStart`, 3 `Stop`,\n   1 `SessionEnd` and 1 `PreCompact`. The Pi extensions are thin wrappers over the same scripts.\n   [SOURCE: .claude/settings.json:1]\n6. Verdict per Pi extension: 16 of the 19 top-level `.ts` extensions are bridge-served by a\n   rewritten hook config (classifier injection screen, completion evidence, decision capture,\n   dispatch audit, dispatch preflight lint, git message gate, git preflight advisory, MCP route\n   guard, post-edit quality, prompt advisor, session relaunch, session-start advisories,\n   session-start context, session-stop context, spec-gate classify, spec-gate enforce). `task\n   dispatch guard` needs adaptation because the bridge's matcher subject is `subagent`, not `Task`,\n   and the script parses a Claude Task payload. `goal context` and `session compact context` need a\n   native plugin because the packet goal binding and `PreCompact` have no bridge point.\n   [SOURCE: .pi/extensions/README.md:1]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/experimental/hooks-claude-code/README.md:94-102]\n7. A single repo bundle is the smaller move only for the residual guards: the bulk arrives through\n   the shipped bridge, so `repo-guards` would carry two plugins (goal context, compact context) and\n   optionally a task-dispatch adapter, not eighteen. One bundle means one install, one `SYNC.md`\n   entry and one upgrade path; one plugin per guard multiplies profile dependencies for no gain.\n   [SOURCE: .hermes/SYNC.md:26-40]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/boot/plugin-manager/README.md:54]\n8. Hooks run in the agent's current session directory, with `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR` set per run, so the\n   repo's relative script paths work when the dispatch cwd is the repo root. A relative\n   `configPath` resolves from the directory that launched the process, which is a second reason the\n   dispatch builder must pin cwd. [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/experimental/hooks-claude-code/README.md:72-80]\n9. Headless disables HMR, so a plugin change cannot hot-reload in a dispatch: the profile reports\n   `restart-required` and the next process picks the change up. A fan-out lineage therefore installs\n   or updates the repo bundle before its run, never mid-run.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/cordis.patch.yml:35-36]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/boot/plugin-manager/README.md:126]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Porting all eighteen Pi extensions to native plugins: the scripts already exist and most hook\n  points are served by the shipped Claude Code bridge.\n- Mounting `.claude/settings.json` unchanged: its capitalized tool matchers never match the\n  harness's lowercase tool names.\n- A project-local plugin folder: plugin resolution is profile-package based, so a repo bundle must\n  be installed into the profile first.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Looking for a `PreCompact` equivalent in the bridge: the supported-event table stops at Stop and\n  the two subagent events.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: \"not relevant\" could mean irrelevant to dispatch or irrelevant to the harness;\n  the verdicts use \"not relevant to dispatch\" only for the two third-party extensions deferred to\n  Angle 7.\n- Contradictory evidence: the bridge README says it is a compatibility adapter and the matcher\n  source confirms exact matching, so \"the repo's hooks already work\" is false without a rewrite.\n- Missing dependencies: the bridge package is not in base or headless, and it is not in the installed\n  package's `node_modules`; mounting it needs a profile install.\n- Partial success: coverage is counted from configuration, not from a live hook firing.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- `dsh-src/docs/cordis-primer.md:9`\n- `dsh-src/packages/boot/plugin-manager/README.md:31,46,54,126`\n- `dsh-src/docs/event-producer-consumer.md:30-91`\n- `dsh-src/packages/experimental/hooks-claude-code/README.md:56-102`\n- `dsh-src/packages/experimental/hook-protocol/src/matcher.ts:60-68`\n- `dsh-src/docs/tool-catalog.md:26-52`\n- `dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/cordis.patch.yml:35-36`\n- `.claude/settings.json` hook inventory\n- `.pi/extensions/README.md`\n- `.hermes/SYNC.md:26-40`\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 0.88 (eight fully new findings, one that reframes the Hermes bundle\n  precedent for a much smaller residual)\n- Questions addressed: Angle 6 plugin seams and the Pi-extension port decision\n- Questions answered: Which lifecycle seams exist for plugins and hooks, and which Pi extensions must be ported, deferred or dropped?\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: the event matrix is the authoritative seam list, and the matcher source\n  turned \"the hooks should work\" into a concrete case-sensitivity failure before any port was\n  planned.\n- What did not work and why: the Claude bridge README's supported-event table is easy to read as\n  complete; checking the implementation's event map confirmed `PreCompact` is absent.\n- What I would do differently: inventory `.claude/settings.json` before reading the Pi extensions,\n  since the repo's own hook layer is the real integration surface.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nAngle 7: third-party Pi plugin migration, one verdict row per package in `.pi/PLUGINS.md` and\n`.pi/settings.json`.\n","path":"specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 6: Plugins, hooks and guards\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nAngle 6: how a harness plugin is written, registered and loaded, which lifecycle seams exist, and\\nhow each of this repo's Pi extensions should be handled — port, port later, or not relevant. The\\nquestion behind it is whether one repo plugin bundle is smaller than one plugin per guard.\\n\\n## Actions Taken\\n\\n- Read the Cordis primer's plugin definition and the plugin-manager install contract.\\n- Read the generated event producer/consumer matrix for the hook seam names.\\n- Read the Claude Code hook bridge's supported-event table, mapping section and matcher rules.\\n- Read the matcher implementation to check case sensitivity.\\n- Inventoried `.pi/extensions/` and `.claude/settings.json` hook coverage by event and matcher.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. A harness plugin is a Cordis object: a function with optional `inject` and an `apply(ctx)` field,\\n   or a `Service` subclass. It is registered as a profile row by package name (`id` + `name` +\\n   optional `config`), not discovered from a project folder; a repo-local plugin must be installed\\n   into the profile as a bundle (`dsh plugin --profile <name> add file:...` / `link:`), and without\\n   HMR a newly installed or changed plugin reports `restart-required`.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/cordis-primer.md:9]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/boot/plugin-manager/README.md:31]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/boot/plugin-manager/README.md:46]\\n2. The usable lifecycle seams are broader than the Pi extension API: `tools/pre-execute` (waterfall,\\n   can deny or ask), `tools/execute`, `tools/post-execute` (waterfall, can block with feedback or\\n   attach context), `tools/result` (emit), `agent/pre-step` (waterfall for prompt and context),\\n   `agent/turn-stopping` (serial, can force another step), `agent/created`, `agent/disposed`,\\n   `session/event`, `turn/start`, `turn/end`, `approval/request`, `fs/write-intent`,\\n   `fs/edit-intent`, `fs/delete-intent`, `compaction/start|summary|prune|end` and `commands/change`.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/event-producer-consumer.md:30]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/event-producer-consumer.md:32-54]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/event-producer-consumer.md:76]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/event-producer-consumer.md:86-91]\\n3. The shipped Claude Code hook bridge maps `SessionStart`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `PreToolUse`,\\n   `PostToolUse`, `Stop`, `SubagentStart` and `SubagentStop` onto those seams; it does not support\\n   `PreCompact` or `SessionEnd`. That leaves two Pi guards without a bridge point: the compact\\n   context injector and the session-end cleanup.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/experimental/hooks-claude-code/README.md:56-70]\\n4. Claude literal matchers are exact, case-sensitive pipe alternatives; only non-word patterns are\\n   regexes. Harness tool names are lowercase (`bash`, `read`, `write`, `edit`, `glob`, `grep`,\\n   `web_fetch`, `apply_patch`, `skill`, `subagent`), so the repo's existing `.claude/settings.json`\\n   matchers `Bash`, `Write|Edit`, `WebFetch`, `Task`, `Task|Agent` and `AskUserQuestion` will not\\n   select anything. A rewritten `.dsh/hooks.json` with harness tool names is required; the scripts\\n   themselves are already runtime-neutral Node entry points.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/experimental/hook-protocol/src/matcher.ts:60-68]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/tool-catalog.md:26-52]\\n5. The repo already carries the hook implementations this angle would otherwise port:\\n   `.claude/settings.json` wires 9 `PreToolUse` entries (dispatch preflight, spec gate, git\\n   preflight, git message gate, subagent guard, task dispatch guard twice, MCP route guard, spec\\n   gate on writes), 5 `PostToolUse` entries (post-edit quality, dispatch audit, classifier injection\\n   screen, context nudge, decision capture), 3 `UserPromptSubmit`, 8 `SessionStart`, 3 `Stop`,\\n   1 `SessionEnd` and 1 `PreCompact`. The Pi extensions are thin wrappers over the same scripts.\\n   [SOURCE: .claude/settings.json:1]\\n6. Verdict per Pi extension: 16 of the 19 top-level `.ts` extensions are bridge-served by a\\n   rewritten hook config (classifier injection screen, completion evidence, decision capture,\\n   dispatch audit, dispatch preflight lint, git message gate, git preflight advisory, MCP route\\n   guard, post-edit quality, prompt advisor, session relaunch, session-start advisories,\\n   session-start context, session-stop context, spec-gate classify, spec-gate enforce). `task\\n   dispatch guard` needs adaptation because the bridge's matcher subject is `subagent`, not `Task`,\\n   and the script parses a Claude Task payload. `goal context` and `session compact context` need a\\n   native plugin because the packet goal binding and `PreCompact` have no bridge point.\\n   [SOURCE: .pi/extensions/README.md:1]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/experimental/hooks-claude-code/README.md:94-102]\\n7. A single repo bundle is the smaller move only for the residual guards: the bulk arrives through\\n   the shipped bridge, so `repo-guards` would carry two plugins (goal context, compact context) and\\n   optionally a task-dispatch adapter, not eighteen. One bundle means one install, one `SYNC.md`\\n   entry and one upgrade path; one plugin per guard multiplies profile dependencies for no gain.\\n   [SOURCE: .hermes/SYNC.md:26-40]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/boot/plugin-manager/README.md:54]\\n8. Hooks run in the agent's current session directory, with `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR` set per run, so the\\n   repo's relative script paths work when the dispatch cwd is the repo root. A relative\\n   `configPath` resolves from the directory that launched the process, which is a second reason the\\n   dispatch builder must pin cwd. [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/experimental/hooks-claude-code/README.md:72-80]\\n9. Headless disables HMR, so a plugin change cannot hot-reload in a dispatch: the profile reports\\n   `restart-required` and the next process picks the change up. A fan-out lineage therefore installs\\n   or updates the repo bundle before its run, never mid-run.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/cordis.patch.yml:35-36]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/boot/plugin-manager/README.md:126]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- Porting all eighteen Pi extensions to native plugins: the scripts already exist and most hook\\n  points are served by the shipped Claude Code bridge.\\n- Mounting `.claude/settings.json` unchanged: its capitalized tool matchers never match the\\n  harness's lowercase tool names.\\n- A project-local plugin folder: plugin resolution is profile-package based, so a repo bundle must\\n  be installed into the profile first.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Looking for a `PreCompact` equivalent in the bridge: the supported-event table stops at Stop and\\n  the two subagent events.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: \\\"not relevant\\\" could mean irrelevant to dispatch or irrelevant to the harness;\\n  the verdicts use \\\"not relevant to dispatch\\\" only for the two third-party extensions deferred to\\n  Angle 7.\\n- Contradictory evidence: the bridge README says it is a compatibility adapter and the matcher\\n  source confirms exact matching, so \\\"the repo's hooks already work\\\" is false without a rewrite.\\n- Missing dependencies: the bridge package is not in base or headless, and it is not in the installed\\n  package's `node_modules`; mounting it needs a profile install.\\n- Partial success: coverage is counted from configuration, not from a live hook firing.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- `dsh-src/docs/cordis-primer.md:9`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/boot/plugin-manager/README.md:31,46,54,126`\\n- `dsh-src/docs/event-producer-consumer.md:30-91`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/experimental/hooks-claude-code/README.md:56-102`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/experimental/hook-protocol/src/matcher.ts:60-68`\\n- `dsh-src/docs/tool-catalog.md:26-52`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/cordis.patch.yml:35-36`\\n- `.claude/settings.json` hook inventory\\n- `.pi/extensions/README.md`\\n- `.hermes/SYNC.md:26-40`\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 0.88 (eight fully new findings, one that reframes the Hermes bundle\\n  precedent for a much smaller residual)\\n- Questions addressed: Angle 6 plugin seams and the Pi-extension port decision\\n- Questions answered: Which lifecycle seams exist for plugins and hooks, and which Pi extensions must be ported, deferred or dropped?\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: the event matrix is the authoritative seam list, and the matcher source\\n  turned \\\"the hooks should work\\\" into a concrete case-sensitivity failure before any port was\\n  planned.\\n- What did not work and why: the Claude bridge README's supported-event table is easy to read as\\n  complete; checking the implementation's event map confirmed `PreCompact` is absent.\\n- What I would do differently: inventory `.claude/settings.json` before reading the Pi extensions,\\n  since the repo's own hook layer is the real integration surface.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nAngle 7: third-party Pi plugin migration, one verdict row per package in `.pi/PLUGINS.md` and\\n`.pi/settings.json`.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916317284,"end":1793916325100}}}}
