{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793916339809,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbf0661001nOw5Am705tdogI","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbefe9d001lLg1Otn3Ceq0Q6","type":"step-start","snapshot":"336c423a26a57fabf204da4658f4e430c1ab63af"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916340859,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbf0885002I19nx17FNuE3nr","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbefe9d001lLg1Otn3Ceq0Q6","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4b0c8f17c7d159b","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled\nsed -n 1,140p .pi/PLUGINS.md"},"output":"# Pi Plugins\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nReference list of all pi plugins enabled for this environment, matching the\n`.pi/settings.json` `packages` array. Six npm packages install at both user\nlevel (`~/.pi/agent/npm`) and project level (`.pi/npm`); two git-source\nextensions (`pi-btw`, `pi-plan-build`) install the same way under `git/`;\n`pi-cache-optimizer` and `pi-fast-mode-w-subagent-support` load from local\nextension directories (`.pi/extensions/`). The npm manifests at both scopes\nare install artifacts and can retain packages that are no longer enabled.\nEntries are sorted alphabetically by package name.\n\n---\n\n#### @juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question (v2.15.0)\n[github.com/juicesharp/rpiv-mono/tree/main/packages/rpiv-ask-user-question](https://github.com/juicesharp/rpiv-mono/tree/main/packages/rpiv-ask-user-question)\n\nA structured questionnaire the model can put to you when it would otherwise guess — typed options instead of free-form replies\n\n---\n\n#### @juicesharp/rpiv-todo (v2.15.0)\n[github.com/juicesharp/rpiv-mono/tree/main/packages/rpiv-todo](https://github.com/juicesharp/rpiv-mono/tree/main/packages/rpiv-todo)\n\nA todo list for the model, rendered as a live overlay that survives /reload and conversation compaction\n\n---\n\n#### pi-blackhole (v0.5.16)\n[github.com/k0valik/pi-blackhole](https://github.com/k0valik/pi-blackhole)\n\nUnified compaction + observational memory — compresses conversation context while preserving durable observations and reflections\n\n---\n\n#### pi-btw (v0.8.0)\n[github.com/dbachelder/pi-btw](https://github.com/dbachelder/pi-btw)\n\nA pi extension for parallel side conversations with /btw\n\n---\n\n#### pi-cache-optimizer (v2.8.0)\n[github.com/jiangge/pi-cache-optimizer](https://github.com/jiangge/pi-cache-optimizer)\n\nImprove Pi prompt/KV cache hit rates — stable prompt ordering, OpenAI-compatible cache keys, proxy-compat warnings, and footer cache stats\n\n**Cache-compat overlay (`.pi/models.json`).** This repo ships a small `models.json`\nthat Pi layers on top of its login-provisioned model catalog\n(`~/.pi/agent/models-store.json`, auto-refreshed from the gateway and not a durable\nedit target). It currently sets `sendSessionAffinityHeaders: true` on the\n`opencode-go` channel (the OpenCode Zen gateway that serves DeepSeek V4 Pro / Flash\nover an OpenAI-compatible API), so a Pi session stays pinned to one upstream backend\nand the provider-side prefix cache stays warm. This also silences the extension's\nper-channel \"DeepSeek-like … missing compat\" startup warning for that channel.\n\n`supportsLongCacheRetention` is deliberately **left off**: DeepSeek's prefix cache is\nautomatic and does not need it, and the Zen gateway's support for OpenAI long\n`prompt_cache_retention` is unverified — enabling it blindly risks\n`400 Unsupported parameter: prompt_cache_retention`. Add it per-model only after\nconfirming the endpoint accepts it. No credentials live in `models.json`; auth stays\nin `auth.json` / `models-store.json`. The file is a symlinked canonical\n(`~/.pi/agent/models.json` → repo `.pi/models.json`) — see [`SYNC.md`](SYNC.md).\n\n---\n\n#### pi-claude-code-ui (v1.0.102)\n[github.com/FammasMaz/pi-cc-tools](https://github.com/FammasMaz/pi-cc-tools)\n\nClaude Code style UI for pi: one-line tool groups, syntax-highlighted diffs, an activity spinner and colors from your terminal theme\n\n**Terminal theme off (`\"terminalTheme\": false`).** By default the package switches Pi\nto its built-in `system` theme, which takes its colors from the terminal's palette.\nThis repo's `.pi/settings.json` sets `terminalTheme` to `false`, so Pi keeps the\n`dark` theme named in the same file. The package still takes the colors of its\ndiffs, connectors and spinner from whichever theme is active.\n\n---\n\n#### pi-fast-mode-w-subagent-support (v0.3.0)\nLocal fork at `.pi/extensions/pi-fast-mode-w-subagent-support` — based on [pi-openai-fast-mode](https://github.com/johncmunson/pi-openai-fast-mode) commit `9b28456`\n\nToggles OpenAI / OpenAI-Codex priority `service_tier` for the configured GPT-5.4–5.6 targets via `/fast [on|off|toggle]` and the `--fast` startup flag. Propagates the fast-mode preference to child Pi processes through the strict `PI_FAST_MODE_W_SUBAGENT_SUPPORT=1|0` environment handoff. Replaces the former `pi-gpt-fast-mode`.\n\n---\n\n#### pi-plan-build (v0.1.120)\n[github.com/janvitos/pi-plan-build](https://github.com/janvitos/pi-plan-build)\n\nPlan safely, approve explicitly, then implement here or in a clean session.\n\n---\n\n#### pi-statusline (v0.0.2)\n[github.com/hsingjui/pi-statusline](https://github.com/hsingjui/pi-statusline)\n\nClaude Code-compatible command-driven statusline extension\n\n---\n\n#### pi-web-access (v0.45.0)\n[github.com/nicobailon/pi-web-access](https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-web-access)\n\nWeb search, URL fetching, GitHub repo cloning, PDF extraction, YouTube video understanding, and local video analysis — zero-config Exa search plus many provider backends\n\n---\n\n## Updating packages\n\nPackages install at **two scopes**: user (`~/.pi/agent/npm`) and project\n(`.pi/npm` in any repo whose `.pi/settings.json` lists them). The startup\n\"Package Updates Available\" banner checks **both** and prefers the project\nscope when the same package is listed twice — a stale project install keeps\nthe banner firing even after a plain update.\n\n`pi update --extensions` updates the user scope, but **skips project packages\nunless the project is trusted**. Without `--approve` the CLI does not even\nload project settings, so the project scope is silently skipped and only git\nsources still run. Fix the stale project scope with:\n\n```bash\ncd <repo root with project packages> && pi update --extensions --approve\n```\n\n`--approve` forces the project-trust override for that command. After updating,\nverify the banner check passes: the installed version in\n`.pi/npm/node_modules/<pkg>/package.json` must equal `npm view <pkg> version`.\n\n---\n\nSource of truth: `pi list` and the `repository`/`homepage` fields of each\npackage's `package.json`. Versions reflect the installed release.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Pi Plugins\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nReference list of all pi plugins enabled for this environment, matching the\n`.pi/settings.json` `packages` array. Six npm packages install at both user\nlevel (`~/.pi/agent/npm`) and project level (`.pi/npm`); two git-source\nextensions (`pi-btw`, `pi-plan-build`) install the same way under `git/`;\n`pi-cache-optimizer` and `pi-fast-mode-w-subagent-support` load from local\nextension directories (`.pi/extensions/`). The npm manifests at both scopes\nare install artifacts and can retain packages that are no longer enabled.\nEntries are sorted alphabetically by package name.\n\n---\n\n#### @juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question (v2.15.0)\n[github.com/juicesharp/rpiv-mono/tree/main/packages/rpiv-ask-user-question](https://github.com/juicesharp/rpiv-mono/tree/main/packages/rpiv-ask-user-question)\n\nA structured questionnaire the model can put to you when it would otherwise guess — typed options instead of free-form replies\n\n---\n\n#### @juicesharp/rpiv-todo (v2.15.0)\n[github.com/juicesharp/rpiv-mono/tree/main/packages/rpiv-todo](https://github.com/juicesharp/rpiv-mono/tree/main/packages/rpiv-todo)\n\nA todo list for the model, rendered as a live overlay that survives /reload and conversation compaction\n\n---\n\n#### pi-blackhole (v0.5.16)\n[github.com/k0valik/pi-blackhole](https://github.com/k0valik/pi-blackhole)\n\nUnified compaction + observational memory — compresses conversation context while preserving durable observations and reflections\n\n---\n\n#### pi-btw (v0.8.0)\n[github.com/dbachelder/pi-btw](https://github.com/dbachelder/pi-btw)\n\nA pi extension for parallel side conversations with /btw\n\n---\n\n#### pi-cache-optimizer (v2.8.0)\n[github.com/jiangge/pi-cache-optimizer](https://github.com/jiangge/pi-cache-optimizer)\n\nImprove Pi prompt/KV cache hit rates — stable prompt ordering, OpenAI-compatible cache keys, proxy-compat warnings, and footer cache stats\n\n**Cache-compat overlay (`.pi/models.json`).** This repo ships a small `models.json`\nthat Pi layers on top of its login-provisioned model catalog\n(`~/.pi/agent/models-store.json`, auto-refreshed from the gateway and not a durable\nedit target). It currently sets `sendSessionAffinityHeaders: true` on the\n`opencode-go` channel (the OpenCode Zen gateway that serves DeepSeek V4 Pro / Flash\nover an OpenAI-compatible API), so a Pi session stays pinned to one upstream backend\nand the provider-side prefix cache stays warm. This also silences the extension's\nper-channel \"DeepSeek-like … missing compat\" startup warning for that channel.\n\n`supportsLongCacheRetention` is deliberately **left off**: DeepSeek's prefix cache is\nautomatic and does not need it, and the Zen gateway's support for OpenAI long\n`prompt_cache_retention` is unverified — enabling it blindly risks\n`400 Unsupported parameter: prompt_cache_retention`. Add it per-model only after\nconfirming the endpoint accepts it. No credentials live in `models.json`; auth stays\nin `auth.json` / `models-store.json`. The file is a symlinked canonical\n(`~/.pi/agent/models.json` → repo `.pi/models.json`) — see [`SYNC.md`](SYNC.md).\n\n---\n\n#### pi-claude-code-ui (v1.0.102)\n[github.com/FammasMaz/pi-cc-tools](https://github.com/FammasMaz/pi-cc-tools)\n\nClaude Code style UI for pi: one-line tool groups, syntax-highlighted diffs, an activity spinner and colors from your terminal theme\n\n**Terminal theme off (`\"terminalTheme\": false`).** By default the package switches Pi\nto its built-in `system` theme, which takes its colors from the terminal's palette.\nThis repo's `.pi/settings.json` sets `terminalTheme` to `false`, so Pi keeps the\n`dark` theme named in the same file. The package still takes the colors of its\ndiffs, connectors and spinner from whichever theme is active.\n\n---\n\n#### pi-fast-mode-w-subagent-support (v0.3.0)\nLocal fork at `.pi/extensions/pi-fast-mode-w-subagent-support` — based on [pi-openai-fast-mode](https://github.com/johncmunson/pi-openai-fast-mode) commit `9b28456`\n\nToggles OpenAI / OpenAI-Codex priority `service_tier` for the configured GPT-5.4–5.6 targets via `/fast [on|off|toggle]` and the `--fast` startup flag. Propagates the fast-mode preference to child Pi processes through the strict `PI_FAST_MODE_W_SUBAGENT_SUPPORT=1|0` environment handoff. Replaces the former `pi-gpt-fast-mode`.\n\n---\n\n#### pi-plan-build (v0.1.120)\n[github.com/janvitos/pi-plan-build](https://github.com/janvitos/pi-plan-build)\n\nPlan safely, approve explicitly, then implement here or in a clean session.\n\n---\n\n#### pi-statusline (v0.0.2)\n[github.com/hsingjui/pi-statusline](https://github.com/hsingjui/pi-statusline)\n\nClaude Code-compatible command-driven statusline extension\n\n---\n\n#### pi-web-access (v0.45.0)\n[github.com/nicobailon/pi-web-access](https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-web-access)\n\nWeb search, URL fetching, GitHub repo cloning, PDF extraction, YouTube video understanding, and local video analysis — zero-config Exa search plus many provider backends\n\n---\n\n## Updating packages\n\nPackages install at **two scopes**: user (`~/.pi/agent/npm`) and project\n(`.pi/npm` in any repo whose `.pi/settings.json` lists them). The startup\n\"Package Updates Available\" banner checks **both** and prefers the project\nscope when the same package is listed twice — a stale project install keeps\nthe banner firing even after a plain update.\n\n`pi update --extensions` updates the user scope, but **skips project packages\nunless the project is trusted**. Without `--approve` the CLI does not even\nload project settings, so the project scope is silently skipped and only git\nsources still run. Fix the stale project scope with:\n\n```bash\ncd <repo root with project packages> && pi update --extensions --approve\n```\n\n`--approve` forces the project-trust override for that command. After updating,\nverify the banner check passes: the installed version in\n`.pi/npm/node_modules/<pkg>/package.json` must equal `npm view <pkg> version`.\n\n---\n\nSource of truth: `pi list` and the `repository`/`homepage` fields of each\npackage's `package.json`. Versions reflect the installed release.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled\\nsed -n 1,140p .pi/PLUGINS.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916340357,"end":1793916340859}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916340864,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbf091a001ill6o1IPDkQuzO","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbefe9d001lLg1Otn3Ceq0Q6","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a8f228575949a864","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\nsed -n 1,50p docs/user/guide/plugin-market.md; echo \"===TODO===\"; sed -n 1,30p packages/todo/*/README.md | head -40; echo \"===COMPACTION===\"; ls packages/compaction"},"output":"# Plugin indexes\n\nEnglish | [中文](plugin-market.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nA plugin index is one JSON file that lists installable DSH bundles with their titles, icons, authors, and versions. Turn on the experimental **Custom plugin indexes** bundle to browse indexes in a **Discover** tab on the Plugins page and install a listed version after a confirmation. To publish an index, write or generate an index file and serve it, together with its icon files, from any static host.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Turn on Discover](#turn-on)\n- [Browse and install](#browse-and-install)\n- [Publish an index](#publish-an-index)\n- [Generate an index with the reference generator](#generate-an-index)\n- [Index format reference](#index-format)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n\n<a id=\"turn-on\"></a>\n## Turn on Discover\n\nOpen **Plugins**, find **Custom plugin indexes** under **Experimental plugins**, and switch it on. A **Discover** tab appears next to **Installed**, and Creator mode gains the read-only `plugin_market` tool. Switching the bundle off removes both again.\n\nDSH ships no index source. Select **Discover**, choose **Add index source**, and enter the URL of an index file. A URL must use `http:` or `https:` and must not contain a user name, password, or `#` fragment. Anyone on the network path can change an `http:` index, including the install specs it lists, so use `https:` for an index reached over a network you do not control. Each source belongs to the current profile.\n\n<a id=\"browse-and-install\"></a>\n## Browse and install\n\nThe index menu at the left of the toolbar switches between sources and opens **Manage index sources…**, where each source shows its last error. Search matches names, authors, keywords, and titles, descriptions, categories, and labels in every language, and tolerates typos; results come in best-match order until you choose another. The **Labels** menu, category pills, and **Sort** menu use the categories, labels, and sort keys that the index declares, and **Publish date** sorts by each plugin's latest version.\n\nEach card shows the plugin's recommended version: the highest version compatible with the running DSH that is not a prerelease, or the highest compatible prerelease when the plugin lists no version without a prerelease tag. **Install** opens a confirmation with that version's source: the npm package and registry, or the Git repository, commit, and directory. Confirming starts the normal plugin installation, including its compatibility and registry checks; nothing is installed without that confirmation.\n\nSelect a card to open its page. The **Versions** list shows every listed version with its compatibility and offers **Install** for any version DSH accepts, including older releases and prereleases. A card that shows **Installed**, **Update available**, or **Provided by DSH** opens the installed bundle instead.\n\nIndexes are third-party content. An index chooses each plugin's title, description, icon, author, and source; DSH checks the file format but not those claims. Add only sources you trust, and check the source in the confirmation before installing.\n\n<a id=\"publish-an-index\"></a>\n## Publish an index\n\nAn index is a UTF-8 JSON file, at most 128 MiB by default, served with a 2xx status and without redirects. Put it on any static host, for example GitLab Pages, GitHub Pages, or an object store behind https.\n\nIcons can be inline or separate files. An inline icon is a base64 `data:` URL in the index; it needs no extra request but makes the index larger. A separate icon is a URL. A relative icon resolves against the index URL, the way an image in a web page resolves against the page: for an index at `https://market.example/dsh/index.json`, `\"icon\": \"icons/foo.svg\"` loads `https://market.example/dsh/icons/foo.svg`. Publishing the index and an `icons/` directory side by side therefore works on any host without editing the file. An absolute icon URL on another origin (scheme, host, and port) must use `https:`.\n\nThis is a minimal index with one npm plugin and one plugin from a Git monorepo:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"format\": 1,\n  \"title\": { \"en\": \"Team plugins\", \"zh\": \"团队插件\" },\n  \"plugins\": [\n    {\n===TODO===\n---\ndescription: \"The model-facing todo_write tool over the DeepSeek Harness session log: whole-list replacement, per-session ownership, and the todos projection, for users and maintainers choosing, configuring, or debugging the tool.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-todo\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-tool-todo` gives the agent a structured task list to plan with: break multi-step work into concrete tasks, mark the task you are working on, and check tasks off as they finish. The list survives across turns and reopened sessions, so the agent and the UI always see the latest plan. One configuration flag decides whether several tasks may be in progress at once, for agents that run work in parallel. Use it wherever an agent should keep a visible task list; each update replaces the whole list, and only the owning agent session can change it.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nUse this package when you want the agent to maintain a visible task list while it works: plan multi-step work, show what is active, and record completion. Mounting it with the parallelism flag is the only setup; the agent then updates the list through its own planning tool whenever the plan changes.\n\n### When to choose it\n===COMPACTION===\ncommand-compact\ncompaction\ncompaction-basic\ncompaction-image-offload\ncompaction-tool-result-pruner\nREADME.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Plugin indexes\n\nEnglish | [中文](plugin-market.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\nA plugin index is one JSON file that lists installable DSH bundles with their titles, icons, authors, and versions. Turn on the experimental **Custom plugin indexes** bundle to browse indexes in a **Discover** tab on the Plugins page and install a listed version after a confirmation. To publish an index, write or generate an index file and serve it, together with its icon files, from any static host.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Turn on Discover](#turn-on)\n- [Browse and install](#browse-and-install)\n- [Publish an index](#publish-an-index)\n- [Generate an index with the reference generator](#generate-an-index)\n- [Index format reference](#index-format)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n\n<a id=\"turn-on\"></a>\n## Turn on Discover\n\nOpen **Plugins**, find **Custom plugin indexes** under **Experimental plugins**, and switch it on. A **Discover** tab appears next to **Installed**, and Creator mode gains the read-only `plugin_market` tool. Switching the bundle off removes both again.\n\nDSH ships no index source. Select **Discover**, choose **Add index source**, and enter the URL of an index file. A URL must use `http:` or `https:` and must not contain a user name, password, or `#` fragment. Anyone on the network path can change an `http:` index, including the install specs it lists, so use `https:` for an index reached over a network you do not control. Each source belongs to the current profile.\n\n<a id=\"browse-and-install\"></a>\n## Browse and install\n\nThe index menu at the left of the toolbar switches between sources and opens **Manage index sources…**, where each source shows its last error. Search matches names, authors, keywords, and titles, descriptions, categories, and labels in every language, and tolerates typos; results come in best-match order until you choose another. The **Labels** menu, category pills, and **Sort** menu use the categories, labels, and sort keys that the index declares, and **Publish date** sorts by each plugin's latest version.\n\nEach card shows the plugin's recommended version: the highest version compatible with the running DSH that is not a prerelease, or the highest compatible prerelease when the plugin lists no version without a prerelease tag. **Install** opens a confirmation with that version's source: the npm package and registry, or the Git repository, commit, and directory. Confirming starts the normal plugin installation, including its compatibility and registry checks; nothing is installed without that confirmation.\n\nSelect a card to open its page. The **Versions** list shows every listed version with its compatibility and offers **Install** for any version DSH accepts, including older releases and prereleases. A card that shows **Installed**, **Update available**, or **Provided by DSH** opens the installed bundle instead.\n\nIndexes are third-party content. An index chooses each plugin's title, description, icon, author, and source; DSH checks the file format but not those claims. Add only sources you trust, and check the source in the confirmation before installing.\n\n<a id=\"publish-an-index\"></a>\n## Publish an index\n\nAn index is a UTF-8 JSON file, at most 128 MiB by default, served with a 2xx status and without redirects. Put it on any static host, for example GitLab Pages, GitHub Pages, or an object store behind https.\n\nIcons can be inline or separate files. An inline icon is a base64 `data:` URL in the index; it needs no extra request but makes the index larger. A separate icon is a URL. A relative icon resolves against the index URL, the way an image in a web page resolves against the page: for an index at `https://market.example/dsh/index.json`, `\"icon\": \"icons/foo.svg\"` loads `https://market.example/dsh/icons/foo.svg`. Publishing the index and an `icons/` directory side by side therefore works on any host without editing the file. An absolute icon URL on another origin (scheme, host, and port) must use `https:`.\n\nThis is a minimal index with one npm plugin and one plugin from a Git monorepo:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"format\": 1,\n  \"title\": { \"en\": \"Team plugins\", \"zh\": \"团队插件\" },\n  \"plugins\": [\n    {\n===TODO===\n---\ndescription: \"The model-facing todo_write tool over the DeepSeek Harness session log: whole-list replacement, per-session ownership, and the todos projection, for users and maintainers choosing, configuring, or debugging the tool.\"\nkind: \"package-reference\"\n---\n\n# @deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-todo\n\nEnglish | [中文](README.zh.md)\n\n## Summary\n\n`dsh-tool-todo` gives the agent a structured task list to plan with: break multi-step work into concrete tasks, mark the task you are working on, and check tasks off as they finish. The list survives across turns and reopened sessions, so the agent and the UI always see the latest plan. One configuration flag decides whether several tasks may be in progress at once, for agents that run work in parallel. Use it wherever an agent should keep a visible task list; each update replaces the whole list, and only the owning agent session can change it.\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Use this package](#use-this-package)\n- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)\n- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)\n- [Model Experience](#model-experience)\n- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)\n- [Dev Note](#dev-note)\n\n-----\n\n<a id=\"use-this-package\"></a>\n## Use this package\n\nUse this package when you want the agent to maintain a visible task list while it works: plan multi-step work, show what is active, and record completion. Mounting it with the parallelism flag is the only setup; the agent then updates the list through its own planning tool whenever the plan changes.\n\n### When to choose it\n===COMPACTION===\ncommand-compact\ncompaction\ncompaction-basic\ncompaction-image-offload\ncompaction-tool-result-pruner\nREADME.i18n.yaml\nREADME.md\nREADME.zh.md\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\nsed -n 1,50p docs/user/guide/plugin-market.md; echo \\\"===TODO===\\\"; sed -n 1,30p packages/todo/*/README.md | head -40; echo \\\"===COMPACTION===\\\"; ls packages/compaction\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916340506,"end":1793916340864}}}}
