{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793916348981,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbf2a35001M04i1socro0SOW","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbf23f6001y5FdVnEm1azIAt","type":"step-start","snapshot":"336c423a26a57fabf204da4658f4e430c1ab63af"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916350399,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbf2e22002Qo8Xi5EMFQPijQ","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbf23f6001y5FdVnEm1azIAt","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae1a3d3cdb7bf462","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\ngrep -n \"| \\`headers\\`\\|headers\" packages/llm/llm-pi-ai/README.md | head -8; echo \"===MODEL OPTIONS===\"; grep -n \"contextWindow\\|maxTokens\\|input\" packages/llm/llm-pi-ai/README.md | head -10"},"output":"119:The plugin answers \"which models can this provider serve?\" for a route a configuration surface is editing or drafting. A route the installed catalog ships is answered from that catalog with no network call, preserving its `input` array as discovery `inputModalities`; only a route the catalog does not describe is interrogated over the wire. `openai-completions` and `openai-responses` use `GET {baseURL}/models` with bearer auth, while `anthropic-messages` uses native `GET /v1/models?limit=1000` semantics with `x-api-key` and `anthropic-version`; its listing URL accepts the API root with or without a trailing `/v1` because gateway documentation publishes both spellings, and only that listing URL normalizes the segment, so model requests receive the configured `baseURL` unchanged. A named configured route supplies its stored credential and profile `headers` inside the Host, so deployment headers configured through `cordis.patch.yml` or Cordis config reach model discovery without becoming discovery-request or Models-page fields; a key typed into the form still wins over the stored credential. The parser accepts either the standard `data` array or an enriched `models` map, normalizing each candidate's id, display name, context window, and output-token cap; Anthropic's `max_input_tokens` and `max_tokens` feed the same capacity fields, a map key remains the request id even when its entry names a different canonical id, primitive-valued map properties are ignored, and a missing display name falls back to that request id. The reply is candidate metadata a surface may offer for adoption — nothing is stored, and `cordis.patch.yml` remains the only thing that decides what a route serves.\n147:<a id=\"request-headers\"></a>\n148:### Request headers\n150:Plugins can contribute model-hidden transport metadata through the synchronous `llm-pi-ai/request-headers` waterfall. Each inference call supplies immutable routing metadata (`PiAiRequestHeaderContext`) and a fresh `Headers` copy of its profile headers; when configured names differ only in capitalization, the last entry wins. The endpoint belongs to the prepared model, so concurrent calls and configuration changes cannot exchange header state. Listeners edit that copy, call `next()`, and release their references on return; `ctx.on()` removes their registration when the owning plugin unloads. Harness attribution is applied after all contributors, and pi-ai receives a detached header record.\n261:- **`headers` can carry a credential the redactor never sees** — profile resolution rejects names and values Fetch cannot represent, but the dict remains plain strings; store credentials as `apiKeyEnv` references.\n266:- **An unauthenticated route depends on its protocol** — a route naming no credential resolves as configured-but-keyless, but pi-ai's OpenAI-compatible implementation still requires an API key or an `Authorization` header, so a keyless local server needs a placeholder credential referenced by `apiKeyEnv` or an `Authorization` entry in `headers`.\n283:- The offered protocol set is deliberately narrower than pi-ai's full API set: Bedrock, Vertex, Azure, and Codex authenticate through flows a profile cannot completely describe with a key, an endpoint, and headers; catalog routes still reach them through their own provider, and only an explicit override is refused. Codex is sign-in-able through the authorization flow's OAuth grant.\n===MODEL OPTIONS===\n30:The adapter accepts the LLM service's [request-only user inputs](../llm/README.md#use-this-package) alongside durable history. User identity and attribution do not enter pi-ai content; assistant replay metadata and tool-call correlation remain attached to durable messages.\n60:            contextWindow: 200000\n71:            contextWindow: 262144\n119:The plugin answers \"which models can this provider serve?\" for a route a configuration surface is editing or drafting. A route the installed catalog ships is answered from that catalog with no network call, preserving its `input` array as discovery `inputModalities`; only a route the catalog does not describe is interrogated over the wire. `openai-completions` and `openai-responses` use `GET {baseURL}/models` with bearer auth, while `anthropic-messages` uses native `GET /v1/models?limit=1000` semantics with `x-api-key` and `anthropic-version`; its listing URL accepts the API root with or without a trailing `/v1` because gateway documentation publishes both spellings, and only that listing URL normalizes the segment, so model requests receive the configured `baseURL` unchanged. A named configured route supplies its stored credential and profile `headers` inside the Host, so deployment headers configured through `cordis.patch.yml` or Cordis config reach model discovery without becoming discovery-request or Models-page fields; a key typed into the form still wins over the stored credential. The parser accepts either the standard `data` array or an enriched `models` map, normalizing each candidate's id, display name, context window, and output-token cap; Anthropic's `max_input_tokens` and `max_tokens` feed the same capacity fields, a map key remains the request id even when its entry names a different canonical id, primitive-valued map properties are ignored, and a missing display name falls back to that request id. The reply is candidate metadata a surface may offer for adoption — nothing is stored, and `cordis.patch.yml` remains the only thing that decides what a route serves.\n196:Successful assistant responses store a versioned, lossless-JSON replay state beside the provider and model that produced them — response-level facts plus one per-block entry per streamed block. At request time, `LlmRuntime` passes replay state only when the same adapter instance owns both routes; the adapter validates it and restores native response ids, provider signatures, and optional `providerThinkingLevel` effort metadata, keeping absent effort metadata absent. Replay validates the requested model identity against the assistant source and uses it to retain same-model signatures; provider-reported response models remain diagnostic metadata. Absent or unusable replay state degrades to provider-neutral content while preserving the assistant source’s required provider and model. pi-ai tool-call arguments are parsed objects, so the adapter parses input and re-stringifies output to the harness raw-JSON convention; pi-ai in-stream error events map to terminal `finish` chunks. Protocol conversion operates on request-local copies. Switching from A to B and back to A preserves the stored content, including empty text blocks, tool ids, original block order, and native replay metadata; requests to a different provider, API, or model omit unsupported signatures and reasoning blocks. Degraded replay without native metadata also omits reasoning. Valid native replay retains the stored reasoning when the original model resumes.\n227:Provider tokenization governs exact input. Retained images add the stable attachment and coordinate descriptor; the offload placeholder replaces an omitted image's visual tokens. Replay metadata may let a native API reuse provider-side state.\n241:Generated content affects later inputs only after the loop records it. pi-ai folds reasoning tokens into output usage when the provider does not report them separately, and preserves its exact `totalTokens` value unchanged.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"119:The plugin answers \"which models can this provider serve?\" for a route a configuration surface is editing or drafting. A route the installed catalog ships is answered from that catalog with no network call, preserving its `input` array as discovery `inputModalities`; only a route the catalog does not describe is interrogated over the wire. `openai-completions` and `openai-responses` use `GET {baseURL}/models` with bearer auth, while `anthropic-messages` uses native `GET /v1/models?limit=1000` semantics with `x-api-key` and `anthropic-version`; its listing URL accepts the API root with or without a trailing `/v1` because gateway documentation publishes both spellings, and only that listing URL normalizes the segment, so model requests receive the configured `baseURL` unchanged. A named configured route supplies its stored credential and profile `headers` inside the Host, so deployment headers configured through `cordis.patch.yml` or Cordis config reach model discovery without becoming discovery-request or Models-page fields; a key typed into the form still wins over the stored credential. The parser accepts either the standard `data` array or an enriched `models` map, normalizing each candidate's id, display name, context window, and output-token cap; Anthropic's `max_input_tokens` and `max_tokens` feed the same capacity fields, a map key remains the request id even when its entry names a different canonical id, primitive-valued map properties are ignored, and a missing display name falls back to that request id. The reply is candidate metadata a surface may offer for adoption — nothing is stored, and `cordis.patch.yml` remains the only thing that decides what a route serves.\n147:<a id=\"request-headers\"></a>\n148:### Request headers\n150:Plugins can contribute model-hidden transport metadata through the synchronous `llm-pi-ai/request-headers` waterfall. Each inference call supplies immutable routing metadata (`PiAiRequestHeaderContext`) and a fresh `Headers` copy of its profile headers; when configured names differ only in capitalization, the last entry wins. The endpoint belongs to the prepared model, so concurrent calls and configuration changes cannot exchange header state. Listeners edit that copy, call `next()`, and release their references on return; `ctx.on()` removes their registration when the owning plugin unloads. Harness attribution is applied after all contributors, and pi-ai receives a detached header record.\n261:- **`headers` can carry a credential the redactor never sees** — profile resolution rejects names and values Fetch cannot represent, but the dict remains plain strings; store credentials as `apiKeyEnv` references.\n266:- **An unauthenticated route depends on its protocol** — a route naming no credential resolves as configured-but-keyless, but pi-ai's OpenAI-compatible implementation still requires an API key or an `Authorization` header, so a keyless local server needs a placeholder credential referenced by `apiKeyEnv` or an `Authorization` entry in `headers`.\n283:- The offered protocol set is deliberately narrower than pi-ai's full API set: Bedrock, Vertex, Azure, and Codex authenticate through flows a profile cannot completely describe with a key, an endpoint, and headers; catalog routes still reach them through their own provider, and only an explicit override is refused. Codex is sign-in-able through the authorization flow's OAuth grant.\n===MODEL OPTIONS===\n30:The adapter accepts the LLM service's [request-only user inputs](../llm/README.md#use-this-package) alongside durable history. User identity and attribution do not enter pi-ai content; assistant replay metadata and tool-call correlation remain attached to durable messages.\n60:            contextWindow: 200000\n71:            contextWindow: 262144\n119:The plugin answers \"which models can this provider serve?\" for a route a configuration surface is editing or drafting. A route the installed catalog ships is answered from that catalog with no network call, preserving its `input` array as discovery `inputModalities`; only a route the catalog does not describe is interrogated over the wire. `openai-completions` and `openai-responses` use `GET {baseURL}/models` with bearer auth, while `anthropic-messages` uses native `GET /v1/models?limit=1000` semantics with `x-api-key` and `anthropic-version`; its listing URL accepts the API root with or without a trailing `/v1` because gateway documentation publishes both spellings, and only that listing URL normalizes the segment, so model requests receive the configured `baseURL` unchanged. A named configured route supplies its stored credential and profile `headers` inside the Host, so deployment headers configured through `cordis.patch.yml` or Cordis config reach model discovery without becoming discovery-request or Models-page fields; a key typed into the form still wins over the stored credential. The parser accepts either the standard `data` array or an enriched `models` map, normalizing each candidate's id, display name, context window, and output-token cap; Anthropic's `max_input_tokens` and `max_tokens` feed the same capacity fields, a map key remains the request id even when its entry names a different canonical id, primitive-valued map properties are ignored, and a missing display name falls back to that request id. The reply is candidate metadata a surface may offer for adoption — nothing is stored, and `cordis.patch.yml` remains the only thing that decides what a route serves.\n196:Successful assistant responses store a versioned, lossless-JSON replay state beside the provider and model that produced them — response-level facts plus one per-block entry per streamed block. At request time, `LlmRuntime` passes replay state only when the same adapter instance owns both routes; the adapter validates it and restores native response ids, provider signatures, and optional `providerThinkingLevel` effort metadata, keeping absent effort metadata absent. Replay validates the requested model identity against the assistant source and uses it to retain same-model signatures; provider-reported response models remain diagnostic metadata. Absent or unusable replay state degrades to provider-neutral content while preserving the assistant source’s required provider and model. pi-ai tool-call arguments are parsed objects, so the adapter parses input and re-stringifies output to the harness raw-JSON convention; pi-ai in-stream error events map to terminal `finish` chunks. Protocol conversion operates on request-local copies. Switching from A to B and back to A preserves the stored content, including empty text blocks, tool ids, original block order, and native replay metadata; requests to a different provider, API, or model omit unsupported signatures and reasoning blocks. Degraded replay without native metadata also omits reasoning. Valid native replay retains the stored reasoning when the original model resumes.\n227:Provider tokenization governs exact input. Retained images add the stable attachment and coordinate descriptor; the offload placeholder replaces an omitted image's visual tokens. Replay metadata may let a native API reuse provider-side state.\n241:Generated content affects later inputs only after the loop records it. pi-ai folds reasoning tokens into output usage when the provider does not report them separately, and preserves its exact `totalTokens` value unchanged.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Skilled/617b2600-a71c-4154-80bb-9def2171203a/scratchpad/src/dsh-src\\ngrep -n \\\"| \\\\`headers\\\\`\\\\|headers\\\" packages/llm/llm-pi-ai/README.md | head -8; echo \\\"===MODEL OPTIONS===\\\"; grep -n \\\"contextWindow\\\\|maxTokens\\\\|input\\\" packages/llm/llm-pi-ai/README.md | head -10\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916349986,"end":1793916350399}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1793916351177,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbf32c9001RV5kFFAEoKLEUZ","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbf23f6001y5FdVnEm1azIAt","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"336c423a26a57fabf204da4658f4e430c1ab63af","cost":0.001080528,"tokens":{"input":1352,"output":165,"reasoning":231,"cache":{"read":213376,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1793916353233,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"id":"prt_1adbf3ad1001Kqu6RcGF4xvBwe","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbf32cf001xZap2OmcMjuKP2","type":"step-start","snapshot":"336c423a26a57fabf204da4658f4e430c1ab63af"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793916363562,"sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","part":{"partID":"prt_1adbf47ba002oAcrHGwMwBSYdA","sessionID":"ses_e52476d2effeOgPb5IbtWFIN6s","messageID":"msg_1adbf32cf001xZap2OmcMjuKP2","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ade1b2ab774710af","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 7: Third-party plugin migration\n\n## Focus\n\nAngle 7: one verdict per third-party Pi package in `.pi/PLUGINS.md` and `.pi/settings.json` —\ndoes the harness already ship the capability natively, is there a harness plugin-market equivalent,\nor is the package not relevant to dispatch?\n\n## Actions Taken\n\n- Read `.pi/PLUGINS.md` and the `.pi/settings.json` package list.\n- Read the plugin-market guide.\n- Checked the base bundle for native `todo`, `user-questions`, compaction and web tools.\n- Checked the `llm-pi-ai` reference for cache and header controls, and the config catalog for a\n  service-tier field.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. Six of the ten packages have a native equivalent already mounted by `dsh-base`: `todo_write`\n   (`tool-todo`), the user-questions seam, compaction (`compaction-basic`, tool-result pruner, image\n   offload), subagents plus the team task board, plan mode, and `web_fetch`/`web_search`\n   (`tool-web`). No Pi package needs a line-by-line port.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:79-80]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:355-359]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:434-446]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:499-500]\n2. `@juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question`: native user-questions exists, but a headless dispatch has\n   no live approval or answer channel, so a structured questionnaire cannot be answered. Verdict:\n   **drop for dispatch**, keep for interactive profiles.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/README.md:100]\n3. `@juicesharp/rpiv-todo`: `todo_write` is mounted in base and persists across turns and reopened\n   sessions, with whole-list replacement and per-session ownership. Verdict: **native replacement**,\n   no migration.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/todo/tool-todo/README.md:12]\n4. `pi-blackhole`: the compaction half maps to `compaction-basic` plus the tool-result pruner and\n   image offload; the observational-memory half has no native store. Verdict: **replace compaction,\n   drop observational memory**; the session log is the durable record.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:355-359]\n   [SOURCE: .pi/PLUGINS.md:34]\n5. `pi-btw`: parallel side conversations map to `subagent`/`subagent_fork` plus the team task board,\n   which share the checkout and keep intermediate traffic out of the parent conversation. Verdict:\n   **native replacement**, no plugin.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:363-403]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/team/agent-team/README.md:12]\n6. `pi-cache-optimizer`: cache behavior is route configuration, not a plugin — `cacheControlFormat`,\n   `supportsLongCacheRetention` and profile `headers` cover prompt-cache markers and session\n   affinity. The `.pi/models.json` affinity overlay becomes route `headers` or a compat field.\n   Verdict: **native replacement by config**, no plugin.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/config-catalog.md:2194-2199]\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/llm/llm-pi-ai/README.md:150]\n7. `pi-fast-mode-w-subagent-support`: the config catalog has no `serviceTier` or `service_tier`\n   field for a model route; the harness executor's `serviceTier` is an OpenCode-runner concept, not\n   a dsh request control. Verdict: **not portable, drop for dsh** unless a specific gateway is shown\n   to honor a header — `UNKNOWN` today.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/config-catalog.md:2031-2108]\n   [SOURCE: .pi/PLUGINS.md:69]\n8. `pi-plan-build`: native plan mode covers plan-then-approve in interactive profiles, but\n   `exit_plan_mode` fails closed without an interactive review, so a headless dispatch cannot use it\n   as a gate. Verdict: **native for interactive, prompt-phase pattern for headless**.\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/plan/plan-mode/README.md:63]\n9. `pi-claude-code-ui` and `pi-statusline`: rendering-only packages with no headless surface.\n   Verdict: **not relevant to dispatch**, drop.\n   [SOURCE: .pi/PLUGINS.md:44-60]\n10. `pi-web-access`: `web_fetch` and `web_search` are native, and the shipped headless profile routes\n    DeepSeek Search and Fetch through the DeepSeek model credential. PDF extraction, YouTube and\n    local-video analysis have no native equivalent. Verdict: **partial native replacement**.\n    [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/README.md:56]\n    [SOURCE: .pi/PLUGINS.md:97]\n11. The plugin market is not a Pi compatibility layer: dsh ships no index source, indexes list\n    installable dsh bundles, and a Pi package is not a dsh bundle. Verdict for all ten: **no market\n    path**; the market matters only as a future distribution channel for the repo's own bundle.\n    [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/user/guide/plugin-market.md:12]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Porting any Pi package as a dsh plugin: none is a dsh bundle, and six capabilities already exist.\n- Installing from the plugin market as a migration shortcut: Pi packages are not listed as dsh\n  bundles.\n- A service-tier plugin for `pi-fast-mode`: no request field exists to carry it.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Searching for a `serviceTier` model-route field: the catalog exposes only executor-side service\n  tier, not a model request control.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: \"not relevant\" is scoped to headless dispatch; the UI packages stay useful in Pi.\n- Contradictory evidence: `pi-blackhole` claims unified compaction plus memory; the harness has the\n  compaction half only.\n- Missing dependencies: no configured provider, so no native replacement was exercised live.\n- Partial success: verdicts are based on the shipped compositions and package contracts.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- `.pi/PLUGINS.md:12-110`\n- `.pi/settings.json` package list\n- `dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:79-80,355-359,363-403,434-446,499-500`\n- `dsh-src/packages/todo/tool-todo/README.md:12`\n- `dsh-src/packages/team/agent-team/README.md:12`\n- `dsh-src/packages/plan/plan-mode/README.md:63`\n- `dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/README.md:56,100`\n- `dsh-src/docs/config-catalog.md:2031-2108,2194-2199`\n- `dsh-src/packages/llm/llm-pi-ai/README.md:150`\n- `dsh-src/docs/user/guide/plugin-market.md:12`\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 0.90 (nine new per-package verdicts, one that extends the plugin-market\n  finding into a \"no migration path\" conclusion)\n- Questions addressed: Angle 7 third-party plugin migration\n- Questions answered: For each third-party Pi plugin, does the harness ship the capability natively, offer a market equivalent, or is it irrelevant to dispatch?\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: checking the base bundle first turned the question from \"port or not\" into\n  \"already mounted or not\", and six of ten were already mounted.\n- What did not work and why: the plugin-market guide is about index files, not third-party\n  compatibility; reading it confirmed the absence of a Pi path rather than a presence.\n- What I would do differently: put the ten packages in a table before the research, so each verdict\n  has a fixed slot and no package is dropped silently.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nAngle 8: MCP configuration, transports, per-dispatch enable/deny, and whether `.pi/mcp.json` and the\ncode-mode server can connect.\n","path":"specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-007.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-007.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-007.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 7: Third-party plugin migration\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nAngle 7: one verdict per third-party Pi package in `.pi/PLUGINS.md` and `.pi/settings.json` —\\ndoes the harness already ship the capability natively, is there a harness plugin-market equivalent,\\nor is the package not relevant to dispatch?\\n\\n## Actions Taken\\n\\n- Read `.pi/PLUGINS.md` and the `.pi/settings.json` package list.\\n- Read the plugin-market guide.\\n- Checked the base bundle for native `todo`, `user-questions`, compaction and web tools.\\n- Checked the `llm-pi-ai` reference for cache and header controls, and the config catalog for a\\n  service-tier field.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. Six of the ten packages have a native equivalent already mounted by `dsh-base`: `todo_write`\\n   (`tool-todo`), the user-questions seam, compaction (`compaction-basic`, tool-result pruner, image\\n   offload), subagents plus the team task board, plan mode, and `web_fetch`/`web_search`\\n   (`tool-web`). No Pi package needs a line-by-line port.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:79-80]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:355-359]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:434-446]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:499-500]\\n2. `@juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question`: native user-questions exists, but a headless dispatch has\\n   no live approval or answer channel, so a structured questionnaire cannot be answered. Verdict:\\n   **drop for dispatch**, keep for interactive profiles.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/README.md:100]\\n3. `@juicesharp/rpiv-todo`: `todo_write` is mounted in base and persists across turns and reopened\\n   sessions, with whole-list replacement and per-session ownership. Verdict: **native replacement**,\\n   no migration.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/todo/tool-todo/README.md:12]\\n4. `pi-blackhole`: the compaction half maps to `compaction-basic` plus the tool-result pruner and\\n   image offload; the observational-memory half has no native store. Verdict: **replace compaction,\\n   drop observational memory**; the session log is the durable record.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:355-359]\\n   [SOURCE: .pi/PLUGINS.md:34]\\n5. `pi-btw`: parallel side conversations map to `subagent`/`subagent_fork` plus the team task board,\\n   which share the checkout and keep intermediate traffic out of the parent conversation. Verdict:\\n   **native replacement**, no plugin.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:363-403]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/team/agent-team/README.md:12]\\n6. `pi-cache-optimizer`: cache behavior is route configuration, not a plugin — `cacheControlFormat`,\\n   `supportsLongCacheRetention` and profile `headers` cover prompt-cache markers and session\\n   affinity. The `.pi/models.json` affinity overlay becomes route `headers` or a compat field.\\n   Verdict: **native replacement by config**, no plugin.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/config-catalog.md:2194-2199]\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/llm/llm-pi-ai/README.md:150]\\n7. `pi-fast-mode-w-subagent-support`: the config catalog has no `serviceTier` or `service_tier`\\n   field for a model route; the harness executor's `serviceTier` is an OpenCode-runner concept, not\\n   a dsh request control. Verdict: **not portable, drop for dsh** unless a specific gateway is shown\\n   to honor a header — `UNKNOWN` today.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/config-catalog.md:2031-2108]\\n   [SOURCE: .pi/PLUGINS.md:69]\\n8. `pi-plan-build`: native plan mode covers plan-then-approve in interactive profiles, but\\n   `exit_plan_mode` fails closed without an interactive review, so a headless dispatch cannot use it\\n   as a gate. Verdict: **native for interactive, prompt-phase pattern for headless**.\\n   [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/plan/plan-mode/README.md:63]\\n9. `pi-claude-code-ui` and `pi-statusline`: rendering-only packages with no headless surface.\\n   Verdict: **not relevant to dispatch**, drop.\\n   [SOURCE: .pi/PLUGINS.md:44-60]\\n10. `pi-web-access`: `web_fetch` and `web_search` are native, and the shipped headless profile routes\\n    DeepSeek Search and Fetch through the DeepSeek model credential. PDF extraction, YouTube and\\n    local-video analysis have no native equivalent. Verdict: **partial native replacement**.\\n    [SOURCE: dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/README.md:56]\\n    [SOURCE: .pi/PLUGINS.md:97]\\n11. The plugin market is not a Pi compatibility layer: dsh ships no index source, indexes list\\n    installable dsh bundles, and a Pi package is not a dsh bundle. Verdict for all ten: **no market\\n    path**; the market matters only as a future distribution channel for the repo's own bundle.\\n    [SOURCE: dsh-src/docs/user/guide/plugin-market.md:12]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- Porting any Pi package as a dsh plugin: none is a dsh bundle, and six capabilities already exist.\\n- Installing from the plugin market as a migration shortcut: Pi packages are not listed as dsh\\n  bundles.\\n- A service-tier plugin for `pi-fast-mode`: no request field exists to carry it.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Searching for a `serviceTier` model-route field: the catalog exposes only executor-side service\\n  tier, not a model request control.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: \\\"not relevant\\\" is scoped to headless dispatch; the UI packages stay useful in Pi.\\n- Contradictory evidence: `pi-blackhole` claims unified compaction plus memory; the harness has the\\n  compaction half only.\\n- Missing dependencies: no configured provider, so no native replacement was exercised live.\\n- Partial success: verdicts are based on the shipped compositions and package contracts.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- `.pi/PLUGINS.md:12-110`\\n- `.pi/settings.json` package list\\n- `dsh-src/packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml:79-80,355-359,363-403,434-446,499-500`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/todo/tool-todo/README.md:12`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/team/agent-team/README.md:12`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/plan/plan-mode/README.md:63`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/bundle/headless/README.md:56,100`\\n- `dsh-src/docs/config-catalog.md:2031-2108,2194-2199`\\n- `dsh-src/packages/llm/llm-pi-ai/README.md:150`\\n- `dsh-src/docs/user/guide/plugin-market.md:12`\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 0.90 (nine new per-package verdicts, one that extends the plugin-market\\n  finding into a \\\"no migration path\\\" conclusion)\\n- Questions addressed: Angle 7 third-party plugin migration\\n- Questions answered: For each third-party Pi plugin, does the harness ship the capability natively, offer a market equivalent, or is it irrelevant to dispatch?\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: checking the base bundle first turned the question from \\\"port or not\\\" into\\n  \\\"already mounted or not\\\", and six of ten were already mounted.\\n- What did not work and why: the plugin-market guide is about index files, not third-party\\n  compatibility; reading it confirmed the absence of a Pi path rather than a presence.\\n- What I would do differently: put the ten packages in a table before the research, so each verdict\\n  has a fixed slot and no package is dropped silently.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nAngle 8: MCP configuration, transports, per-dispatch enable/deny, and whether `.pi/mcp.json` and the\\ncode-mode server can connect.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/cli-external-orchestration/085-cli-deepseek-harness-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-007.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1793916356538,"end":1793916363562}}}}
